astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_024,24,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian X1,1 k1X−2,−1 k2Eι(ω1−ω2)+X1,−1 k1X−2,1 k2E−ι(ω1−ω2)−αX2,0 k1X−3,0 k2/parenrightBig .(26) For convenience, we omit the explicit dependence on eccentricities e1ande2in the Hansen coeﬃcients Xn,m k1(e1) andXn,m k2(e2), unless needed for clarity. The indices k1 andk2refer to the inner and outer planet, respectively. To isolate the secular part of A1, we retain only terms with zero frequency in the mean longitudes. In the ﬁrst sum, this corresponds to k2= 0 and l∈ {−1,0,1}. In 8 the second sum, secular terms correspond to k1=−landk2=l. This yields","X1,1","X1,1",,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,116,89,False,True,0,89
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_025,25,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order The formalism presented at orders 0 and 1 for this mixed expansion c an be systemat- ically extended to arbitrary order. At each order k, we express a2/∆ as a sum of the ﬁrstk+1 terms of the classical Laplace expansion, with each term reform ulated in a way that is exact in both eandα. Speciﬁcally, we write a2 ∆=k/summationdisplay j=0Aj, (29) where each term Ajtakes the form Aj=a2 r2CjVjA−j−1/2, C j= (−1)j(2j)! 4jj!2. (30) To extract the secular component from each term, we follow the pr ocedure outlined for orders 0 and 1. The term A−j−1/2is expanded as a Fourier series in the angle λ1−λ2using Laplace coeﬃcients: A−s=1 2/summationdisplay l∈Zb(l) s(α)Eιl(λ1−λ2). (31) The expression Vjconsists of a ﬁnite sum of products of trigonometricterms involving the angles ( λi,ωi,νi) and powers of the ratios ri/ai. The product ( a2/r2)Vjcan thus be expressed as a seriesin the mean anomalies Mi, using Hansen coeﬃcients deﬁned in Eq. (6). This allows us to write Ajas a Fourier series in the mean longitudes",3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order,3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_026,26,"and powers of the ratios ri/ai. The product ( a2/r2)Vjcan thus be expressed as a seriesin the mean anomalies Mi, using Hansen coeﬃcients deﬁned in Eq. (6). This allows us to write Ajas a Fourier series in the mean longitudes λi, from which we extract its secular part A(0,0) jby isolating the terms with zero frequency in λi. The secular component of the direct part of the perturbing func tion at order kis 9 then given by /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}ht=−Gm1m2 a2k/summationdisplay j=0A(0,0) j. (32) The derivation of A2and its secular component A(0,0) 2is provided in Appendix Aas an additional example of the steps outlined here. While the algebraic complexity increases with the order k, the structure of the expansion remains recursive and systematic. This makes the appro ach well-suited for symbolic computation using a computer algebrasystem. We implement this formalism usingTRIP, a computer algebra system designed for handling perturbation se ries in celestial mechanics ( Gastineau and Laskar 2011 ,2021). It includes built-in libraries for manipulating both Hansen and Laplace coeﬃcients, and evaluatin g them when needed for numerical application.",3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order,3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,232,178,False,True,140,318
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_027,27,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion The convergence of truncated secular expansions of the disturb ing function has been investigated in several previous studies, primarily through numeric al assessments of theaccuracyofhigh-orderexpansions.In( Laskar1985 ),theconvergenceoftheLaplace expansion at second order of the masses is analyzed for all planeta ry pairs in the Solar System with respect to the truncation in the harmonics of the ﬁrst order expansion. For the coplanar 3-body problem, Libert & Henrard ( 2005) considered Hamiltonian expansions carried to degree 12 in eccentricity and showed that th e resulting secular models accurately reproduce the long-term dynamics of exoplanet ary systems with large eccentricities, while also demonstrating that the series of sec ular terms con- verges better than the series associated with the full perturbat ion. Migaszewski & Go´ zdziewski ( 2008) performed a similar convergencestudy of the Legendre expansio n, extending it up to order 24 in the semi-major axis ratio and applying it t o a range of exoplanetary conﬁgurations. They showed that the expansion converges rapidly for suﬃciently hierarchical systems, while",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_028,28,"similar convergencestudy of the Legendre expansio n, extending it up to order 24 in the semi-major axis ratio and applying it t o a range of exoplanetary conﬁgurations. They showed that the expansion converges rapidly for suﬃciently hierarchical systems, while the convergence becomes p rogressively slower as the system becomes more compact. In this section, we compare the convergence properties of the La place–Legendre expansion introduced in Sect. 3with those of the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions. In line with the aforementioned studies, we focus here on a numerical assessment of convergence based on the evaluation of the series at diﬀerent truncation orders.InthecelebratedchapterVIIIonFormalcalculus,in Les M´ ethodesNouvelles de la M´ ecanique C´ eleste , Poincar´ e ( 1892) distinguishes the convergence in the sens of the astronomers and mathematicians, and reminds that both approac hes are legitimate. As discussed in Sect. 2.2, we know that for suﬃciently small values of the ratio of semi major axis, α, eccentricities and inclinations, the series expansion ( 2.2) is convergent. But in the present work, we consider formal expansions and addre ss the convergence",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_029,29,"2.2, we know that for suﬃciently small values of the ratio of semi major axis, α, eccentricities and inclinations, the series expansion ( 2.2) is convergent. But in the present work, we consider formal expansions and addre ss the convergence questions in a numerical way,in the sense ofthe astronomers,with out addressingmore mathematical questions such as the domain of convergenceof the series, the size of the remainder, or the decay rate of the terms. Our goal is to assess t he accuracy of each series in approximating the secular direct part of the perturbing fu nction,/an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}ht, as a 10 function of the truncation order. As in the previous section, we fo cus on the planar problem, where /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}htdepends on four parameters: the eccentricities ei, the diﬀerence of arguments of pericenter ∆ ω=ω1−ω2, and the semi-major axis ratio α. For simplicity, we assume equal eccentricities, e1=e2=e, and explore a range of values ineandα, from nearly circular ( e≪1) to moderately eccentric orbits ( e∼0.2), and from hierarchical ( α≪1) to moderately compact ( α∼0.2) conﬁgurations. We also consider two representative values of",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_030,30,"assume equal eccentricities, e1=e2=e, and explore a range of values ineandα, from nearly circular ( e≪1) to moderately eccentric orbits ( e∼0.2), and from hierarchical ( α≪1) to moderately compact ( α∼0.2) conﬁgurations. We also consider two representative values of ∆ ω, namely 0◦and 180◦. For each set of parameters, we compute a reference value of /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}htby numerically averaging the inverse of mutual distance a2/∆ over the mean anomalies. In practice, we perform the average over the eccentric anomalies Eiinstead, using the standard relation d Mi= (1−eicosEi)dEi. We discretize the integral on a uniform 2D grid in Ei, evaluate a2/∆ at each grid point, and compute the average over the grid using th e trapezoidal rule. We denote this reference value as /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}htnum. We then evaluate /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}ht using the three expansions at increasing truncation orders N, deﬁned as follows. For the Legendre expansion, Ndenotes the truncation order in the semi-major axis ratio αused in Eq. ( 7) to compute the secular part. For the Laplace expansion, Nrep- resents the maximum total degree in eccentricities, and the secula r part is",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_031,31,"the Legendre expansion, Ndenotes the truncation order in the semi-major axis ratio αused in Eq. ( 7) to compute the secular part. For the Laplace expansion, Nrep- resents the maximum total degree in eccentricities, and the secula r part is obtained by truncating Eq. ( 12) to retain all terms such that n1+ ¯n1+n2+ ¯n2≤N. In the Laplace–Legendre formulation, Nrefers to the upper index in the sum/summationtextN j=0A(0,0) j, so thatN+ 1 terms are retained from the Laplace expansion (Eq. ( 8)). The secu- lar contribution at each order is computed using Eq. ( 32), with each term evaluated according to the formalism presented in Sect. 3. It should be noted that for each k >0, the expression for the term A(0,0) kinvolves an inﬁnite summation over Laplace coeﬃcients b(l) k+1 2; see Eq. ( 27) forA(0,0) 1as an example. These coeﬃcients decay expo- nentially as l→ ∞, since they are Fourier coeﬃcients of an analytic function when α <1. This allows the summation to be truncated for numerical implement ation. One option is to deﬁne a global truncation limit",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,560,740
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_032,32,"These coeﬃcients decay expo- nentially as l→ ∞, since they are Fourier coeﬃcients of an analytic function when α <1. This allows the summation to be truncated for numerical implement ation. One option is to deﬁne a global truncation limit Lthat ensures a given precision across all terms considered. Alternatively, an adaptive approach consists in selecting, for each k, a value of Llarge enough to ensure A(0,0) kconverges to the requested precision. In our study, convergence of each A(0,0) kwas veriﬁed to machine precision ( ∼10−16) over all parameter regimes explored in the present section. This inc ludes conﬁgura- tions with e,α∈[0,0.2], as well as two limiting cases: nearly circular but compact conﬁgurations with e= 0.01 andα∈ {0.4,0.5}, and hierarchical, highly eccentric con- ﬁgurations with e= 0.6 andα∈ {0.001,0.01}. The truncation limits Lkadopted for eachA(0,0) k, together with the corresponding number of terms in its resulting e xpres- sion, are reported in Appendix Bfor orbital conﬁgurations with e,α∈[0,0.2]. For completeness, we also report the number of terms obtained at a giv en order in the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions.",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,700,880
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_033,33,"number of terms in its resulting e xpres- sion, are reported in Appendix Bfor orbital conﬁgurations with e,α∈[0,0.2]. For completeness, we also report the number of terms obtained at a giv en order in the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions. In all cases, the relat ive error is deﬁned as relative error =/vextendsingle/vextendsingle/vextendsingle/vextendsingle1−/an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}ht /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}htnum/vextendsingle/vextendsingle/vextendsingle/vextendsingle. (33) Figure1displays the relative error in computing /an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}htas a function of the trun- cation order Nfor the three expansions, with ∆ ω= 0◦. The grid layout allows for a 11 10−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0.001e = 0.01 e = 0.1 e = 0. 2 10−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0.01 10−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0.1 0 2 4 6 8 1010−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0. 2 0 2 4 6 8 10 0 2 4 6 8 10 truncation order Nrelative errorΔ ω = 0 Legendre Laplace Laplace-Legendre Fig. 1 Relative error in the computation of /angbracketleftU1/angbracketrightas a function of the truncation order N, comparing the Legendre (black), Laplace (red), and Laplace–Legendre (blue) expansions. Each panel corresponds to a speciﬁc conﬁguration of eccentricity and semi-major ax is ratio. Columns show increasing eccen- tricities e= 0.01,",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,840,1020
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_034,34,"of /angbracketleftU1/angbracketrightas a function of the truncation order N, comparing the Legendre (black), Laplace (red), and Laplace–Legendre (blue) expansions. Each panel corresponds to a speciﬁc conﬁguration of eccentricity and semi-major ax is ratio. Columns show increasing eccen- tricities e= 0.01, 0.1, and 0 .2 (left to right), while rows correspond to increasing semi- major axis ratiosα= 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, and 0.2 (top to bottom). The value of ∆ ωis ﬁxed at 0◦. All the numerical evaluations are performed to double precision. direct comparison across diﬀerent dynamical regimes: each row co rresponds to a ﬁxed value ofα, and each column to a ﬁxed value of the eccentricities (with e1=e2). The Laplace–Legendre expansion improves over the Legendre expans ion, in many of the tested conﬁgurations. This improvement is most pronounced in the low-eccentricity regime (e.g., for e= 0.01), where it reaches high accuracy with fewer orders, particu- larly asαincreases. This is because it is constructed as a series in V, which scales as αe(see Eq. ( 13)), whereas the Legendre expansion (Eq. ( 7)) is a series in α, and does not",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,980,1160
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_035,35,"accuracy with fewer orders, particu- larly asαincreases. This is because it is constructed as a series in V, which scales as αe(see Eq. ( 13)), whereas the Legendre expansion (Eq. ( 7)) is a series in α, and does not beneﬁt from the smallness of e. In this regime, its performance closely matches that of the Laplace expansion, which is already well suited for nearly circular orbits, 12 10−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0.001e = 0.01 e = 0.1 e = 0. 2 10−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0.01 10−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0.1 0 2 4 6 8 1010−1410−1110−810−510−2α = 0. 2 0 2 4 6 8 10 0 2 4 6 8 10 truncation order Nrelative errorΔ ω = π Legendre Laplace Laplace-Legendre Fig. 2 Same as Fig. 1, but for a ﬁxed value of ∆ ω= 180◦. independently of α. An intermediate regime ( e= 0.1–0.2,α= 0.1) appears where the Laplace–Legendreexpansion can outperform both classical m ethods provided that the truncation order Nis suﬃciently high. At higher eccentricities ( e= 0.2), the Laplace–Legendre expansion behaves similarly to the Legendre exp ansion and shows moderate improvements",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,1120,1300
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_036,36,"0.1–0.2,α= 0.1) appears where the Laplace–Legendreexpansion can outperform both classical m ethods provided that the truncation order Nis suﬃciently high. At higher eccentricities ( e= 0.2), the Laplace–Legendre expansion behaves similarly to the Legendre exp ansion and shows moderate improvements across all values of α. In this regime, both methods remain more accurate than the Laplace expansion in the hierarchical limit ( α= 0.001, 0.01), whereas the Laplace expansion provides the best accuracy in comp act conﬁgurations (α= 0.2). Figure2presents the same comparison for ∆ ω= 180◦. The qualitative behavior of the three expansions remains consistent with the ∆ ω= 0◦, particularly in the small eandαregimes. A notable diﬀerence arises in the moderately eccentric and compact conﬁgurations ( e= 0.1–0.2,α= 0.2), where the Laplace–Legendre expansion shows improved convergence and matches the performance of the Lapla ce expansion more 13 closely. We also note that Eq. ( 8) is an alternating series when V >0. This can lead to an non-monotonic behavior in the convergence of the Laplace-L egendre expansion, wheretherelativeerrormayincreaseatcertainoddtruncationor dersbeforeimproving again. 10−1310−910−510−1α = 0.4e = 0.01 10−1410−1110−8α = 0.001e",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,1260,1440
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_037,37,"We also note that Eq. ( 8) is an alternating series when V >0. This can lead to an non-monotonic behavior in the convergence of the Laplace-L egendre expansion, wheretherelativeerrormayincreaseatcertainoddtruncationor dersbeforeimproving again. 10−1310−910−510−1α = 0.4e = 0.01 10−1410−1110−8α = 0.001e = 0.6 0 2 4 6 8 1010−1310−910−510−1α = 0.5 0 2 4 6 8 1010−1410−1110−810−5α = 0.01 truncation order Nrelative errorΔ ω = 0 Legendre Laplace Laplace-Legendre Fig. 3 Relative error in the computation of /angbracketleftU1/angbracketrightas a function of the truncation order N, comparing the Legendre (black), Laplace (red), and Laplace–Legendre (blue) expansions, for ∆ ω= 0◦. The left column corresponds to the nearly circular compact regime ( e= 0.01,α= 0.4,0.5), while the right column shows the hierarchical eccentric one ( e= 0.6,α= 0.001,0.01). We further explore the performanceofthe Laplace–Legendreex pansion in twocon- trasting regimes: the nearly circular, compact conﬁguration ( e= 0.01,α= 0.4,0.5), where the Laplace expansion is known to perform best, and the hier archical, highly eccentric case ( e= 0.6,α≪1), where the Legendre expansion yields the most accu- rate results. These regimes are illustrated in Fig.",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,1400,1580
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_038,38,"conﬁguration ( e= 0.01,α= 0.4,0.5), where the Laplace expansion is known to perform best, and the hier archical, highly eccentric case ( e= 0.6,α≪1), where the Legendre expansion yields the most accu- rate results. These regimes are illustrated in Fig. 3, which shows the relative error as a function of truncation order Nfor the three expansions. In both regimes, the Laplace–Legendre expansion tracks the classical expansion that is best suited to the respective regime. The slightly reduced accuracy seen in Fig. 3could be justiﬁed by the fact that the new formulation orders the series in powers of th e parameter V, rather than in the small parameter that is appropriate to each reg ime (efor Laplace, 14 αfor Legendre). As a result, in these limiting cases the ordering is not as locally opti- mal as in the corresponding classical expansion. This is a trade-oﬀ f or the optimality of the new expansion in the regime e,α→0. Anyway, the clear advantage of the new expansion is that now we have a global formulation that bridges the L aplace and Leg- endre",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,1540,1720
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_039,39,"This is a trade-oﬀ f or the optimality of the new expansion in the regime e,α→0. Anyway, the clear advantage of the new expansion is that now we have a global formulation that bridges the L aplace and Leg- endre regimes.This broaderapplicability comesat the cost ofa large rnumber ofterms retained at a given expansion order compared to the classical Lapla ce and Legendre expansions (see Appendix B). Finally, we note that when both eandαare not small (e.g, e,α >0.2), the convergenceoftheLaplace-Legendreexpansiondeterioratesa ndbecomesprogressively slower, similarly to the classical expansions. This behavior is expecte d, as in such regimestheparameter Visnolongersmallandtheassumptionsunderlyingthepresent expansion are no longer satisﬁed (see Sect. 3).",4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergence of the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,138,106,False,False,1680,1786
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_040,40,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 5 Conclusion In this work, we explored an alternative expansion of the disturbing function for the planar planetary 3-body problem. This formulation, referr ed to as the Laplace–Legendre expansion, combines elements from both the cla ssical Laplace and Legendreseries.ItusesthesameseriesstructureastheLaplac eformulation,but diﬀers in that each term of the expansion retains its full dependence in bot h the semi-major axis ratio and eccentricity. The method provides, for example, an a lternative means to construct the secular Hamiltonian suitable for modeling the long-t erm evolution of planetary systems. We evaluated the convergence of this expansion across several c hoices of eccen- tricities eand semi-major axis ratios α, and compared its performance with that of the Laplace and Legendre series. We explored conﬁgurations ch aracterized by e,α∈[0,0.2], as well as two limiting regimes: nearly circular, compact systems wit h e= 0.01andα∈ {0.4,0.5},andhierarchical,highlyeccentricsystemswith e= 0.6and α∈ {0.001,0.01}. The results show that the Laplace–Legendre expansion perform s best in regimes where both eandαare small (e.g., e= 0.01,α= 0.01). It can also outperform both classical methods in an intermediate regime,",5 Conclusion,5 Conclusion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_041,41,"wit h e= 0.01andα∈ {0.4,0.5},andhierarchical,highlyeccentricsystemswith e= 0.6and α∈ {0.001,0.01}. The results show that the Laplace–Legendre expansion perform s best in regimes where both eandαare small (e.g., e= 0.01,α= 0.01). It can also outperform both classical methods in an intermediate regime, wher e neither param- eter is particularly small but their product remains small (e.g., e= 0.1,α= 0.1). In the hierarchical limit ( α <0.1), the Laplace–Legendre expansion improves upon the Legendre expansion at low eccentricities ( e <0.1), while at higher eccentricities (e≥0.2) it closely reproduces the performance of the Legendre expans ion. In the nearly circular regime ( e/lessorsimilar0.01), and as long as αremains moderate, its behavior closely matches that of the Laplace expansion. In the last two regim es, combining the Laplace and Legendre approaches does not necessarily lead to an expansion that outperforms both classical methods at the same time. Instead, t he Laplace–Legendre expansion tends to match the method that is best suited to each limit . In the extremes of these limits (namely, when α≪1 andeis large, or when e∼0 whileαis large), the new expansion",5 Conclusion,5 Conclusion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_042,42,"the same time. Instead, t he Laplace–Legendre expansion tends to match the method that is best suited to each limit . In the extremes of these limits (namely, when α≪1 andeis large, or when e∼0 whileαis large), the new expansion may exhibit slightly reduced accuracy compared t o the classical method most appropriate for that regime. Nevertheless, its clear advantage is to span in a single formulation both the Laplace and Legendre expansions. It may therefore 15 be useful when the system transitions between diﬀerent dynamica l regimes, or when the prevailing regime cannot be determined a priori. Overall, the Laplace–Legendre expansion provides consistent per formance across a wide range of orbital parameters which includes the domain of applic ability of both the Laplace and Legendre expansions, making it an interesting optio n for secular stud- ies. The price to pay for this broader applicability is an increasing comp lexity of the analytical expression of the Hamiltonian at each order with respect to the classical expansions, which results in a largernumber ofterms involvedat a giv en order.In par- ticular, the",5 Conclusion,5 Conclusion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_043,43,"pay for this broader applicability is an increasing comp lexity of the analytical expression of the Hamiltonian at each order with respect to the classical expansions, which results in a largernumber ofterms involvedat a giv en order.In par- ticular, the expression of the secular Hamiltonian involves an inﬁnite s ummation over the Laplace coeﬃcients and therefore is not in a closed form. This na turally calls for the use of a computer algebra system to manipulate large expressio ns, particularly for the spatial problem, which we did not address in this exploratory wor k. Extending the Laplace–Legendreformulationtothe spatialcaseis feasiblealongt he samelines ofthis work, but would substantially increase the algebraic complexity, as is already appar- ent in the classical expansions when moving from the planar to the sp atial formulation (e.g.,Laskar and Bou´ e 2010 ). While a dedicated study would be required to quantify convergence in the spatial case, the new formulation remains a for mal expansion in the parameter V. We therefore expect similar qualitative convergence behavior in th e regime where Vremains small, i.e., for small to moderate",5 Conclusion,5 Conclusion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_044,44,"be required to quantify convergence in the spatial case, the new formulation remains a for mal expansion in the parameter V. We therefore expect similar qualitative convergence behavior in th e regime where Vremains small, i.e., for small to moderate eccentricities, inclinations and semi-major axis ratios. Appendix A Laplace-Legendre expansion of order 2 We present here the Laplace–Legendre expansion of order 2, deﬁ ned by a2",5 Conclusion,5 Conclusion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,86,66,False,False,560,626
