astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_000,0,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System front_matter Draft version January 14, 2026 Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System Hanno Rein ,1, 2, 3Kavi Dey ,4and Daniel Tamayo 4 1Dept. of Physical and Environmental Sciences, University of Toronto at Scarborough, Toronto, Ontario, M1C 1A4, Canada 2Dept. of Physics, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 3H4, Canada 3Dept. of Astronomy and Astrophysics, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 3H4, Canada 4Dept. of Physics, Harvey Mudd College, 301 Platt Blvd., Claremont 91711, USA",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,128,98,False,False,0,98
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_001,1,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System ABSTRACT Wisdom-Holman (WH) integrators are symplectic operator-splitting methods widely used for long- term N-body simulations of planetary systems. Most implementations use either Jacobi coordinates or democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC) for the Hamiltonian splitting, resulting in slightly different algorithms. In this paper we report results from numerical experiments, which show that integrations of the Solar System using DHC coordinates with typical timesteps of a few days suppress instabilities of the planet Mercury. We further show that this is due to an eccentricity dependent artificial numerical precession introduced by the DHC splitting. While the DHC splitting converges to the correct results at shorter timesteps of∼0.6 days, we argue that Jacobi coordinates remain reliable to significantly longer timesteps when orbits become moderately eccentric, and are thus a better choice when the innermost planet can reach high eccentricities. Keywords:N-body simulations (1083) — Celestial mechanics (211) — Computational methods (1965) – Solar system evolution (2293) — Planetary system evolution (2292)",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,221,170,False,False,0,170
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_002,2,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrations of the Solar System, have a long history (see e.g. W. J. Eckert et al. 1951 for one of the first simulations of this kind, and J. Laskar 2013 for a historical summary of the field). Although dif- ferent authors use different numerical algorithms, most modern long-term simulations use symplectic integra- tors that make use of the Wisdom-Holman splitting (J. L. Wisdom 1981; J. Wisdom & M. Holman 1991; H. Kinoshita et al. 1991). Various variations of the Wisdom-Holman (WH) algorithm exist, differing in the order of the scheme, the coordinate system used for the Hamiltonian splitting (D. M. Hernandez & W. Dehnen 2017), and other details such as whether or not symplec- tic correctors are used (for a comparisons of methods see H. Rein et al. 2019). Several authors have argued that it is important for the adopted timestep to be short enough to resolve peri- center passages, which becomes challenging at high or- bital eccentricities like those achieved when Mercury",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_003,3,"see H. Rein et al. 2019). Several authors have argued that it is important for the adopted timestep to be short enough to resolve peri- center passages, which becomes challenging at high or- bital eccentricities like those achieved when Mercury goes unstable (K. P. Rauch & M. Holman 1999; J. Wis- dom 2015; D. M. Hernandez et al. 2022). This paper shows that this criterion depends on the coordinate system used. To clarify, we do not refer to thecoordinate system in which the user inputs and outputs their positions and velocities (e.g. ecliptic vs equato- rial). Our focus is rather on the choice of coordinates used to split off a dominant Keplerian term from the full Hamiltonian as part of the WH algorithm. The two most commonly used coordinate systems for the Hamil- tonian splitting are Jacobi coordinates and democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC). Both coordinate sys- tems have advantages and disadvantages. For example, DHC (M. J. Duncan et al. 1998) can be used for hy- brid symplectic integrators and can be more easily par- allelized (P. Javaheri et al. 2023).",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_004,4,"heliocentric coordinates (DHC). Both coordinate sys- tems have advantages and disadvantages. For example, DHC (M. J. Duncan et al. 1998) can be used for hy- brid symplectic integrators and can be more easily par- allelized (P. Javaheri et al. 2023). Jacobi coordinates on the other hand are more accurate and can be used with symplectic correctors to achieve very high order (J. Wisdom et al. 1996; S. Blanes et al. 2013). For a pre- cise definition and a more detailed comparison of these coordinate systems see H. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). Both coordinate systems have been used to study the long-term evolution and stability of the Solar System. The first direct integrations over 5 Gyr were performed by J. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) with the SABA integrator, a high order integrator using Jacobi coordi- nates (J. Laskar & P. Robutel 2001). More recently, N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) used the MER- CURY hybrid integrator (J. E. Chambers 1999) witharXiv:2601.08019v1 [astro-ph.EP] 12 Jan 2026 2 DHC to study the influence of passing field stars on the",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_005,5,"P. Robutel 2001). More recently, N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) used the MER- CURY hybrid integrator (J. E. Chambers 1999) witharXiv:2601.08019v1 [astro-ph.EP] 12 Jan 2026 2 DHC to study the influence of passing field stars on the Solar System. Notably, the simulations that constitute the control group of N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) and should be comparable to those of J. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) show an instability rate of only 0.1%, whereas the simulations of J. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) and others (R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbot et al. 2021; D. S. Abbot et al. 2023) result in an instability rate an order of magnitude larger,∼1%. Similar discrepancies were also observed by R. E. Zeebe (2015) who studied the position error of Mer- cury when using different Hamiltonian splittings. R. E. Zeebe (2015) correctly concluded that DHC coordinates lack accuracy and Jacobi coordinates are better suited to the problem. We begin in Sec. 2.1 by running numerical experi- ments, which show that this discrepancy is indeed due to the",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_006,6,"R. E. Zeebe (2015) correctly concluded that DHC coordinates lack accuracy and Jacobi coordinates are better suited to the problem. We begin in Sec. 2.1 by running numerical experi- ments, which show that this discrepancy is indeed due to the different coordinate systems used in the Hamiltonian splitting. We show that simulations using DHC require a significantly smaller timestep in order to be statisti- cally converged. In Sec. 2.2, we demonstrate that the issue suppressing dynamical instabilities is best under- stood in terms of errors not in the position but rather in the precession rate, as the Solar System’s evolution is primarily governed by secular dynamics. With this understanding, we show that integrations using DHC become unreliable when a planet’s eccentricity becomes large, or more precisely when the pericenter crossing timescale becomes comparable to the adopted timestep. We show in Sec. 2.3 that the stabilizing effect of the arti- ficial numerical precession is the same as the stabilizing effect due to general relativistic (GR) precession. By contrast, the splitting using Jacobi coordinates solves the two-body problem for the inner-most planet",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,560,740
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_007,7,"Sec. 2.3 that the stabilizing effect of the arti- ficial numerical precession is the same as the stabilizing effect due to general relativistic (GR) precession. By contrast, the splitting using Jacobi coordinates solves the two-body problem for the inner-most planet exactly and reliably reproduces the correct precession rate out to much larger eccentricities. This allows for a signif- icantly longer timestep when using Jacobi coordinates as compared to DHC. In Sec. 3 we discuss how the pre- cession error originates from the integrator’s jump step. We conclude in Sec. 4 with some general advice.",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,123,94,False,False,700,794
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_008,8,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND package (H. Rein & S. F. Liu 2012). REBOUND in- cludes several integrators, in particular the WH integra- tor WHFast (H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2015) which allows us to choose the coordinate system for the Hamiltonian splitting. We also run simulations with the WHFast512 integrator (P. Javaheri et al. 2023), which only supports DHC. We include comparisons with WHFast512 because it does not reuse any code from WHFast, and can there- fore be seen as an independent implementation of a WHintegrator with DHC. This helps rule out any errors in the implementation as a cause for the observed discrep- ancies. For all simulations we use the present-day positions and velocities of all eight Solar System planets as initial conditions. We combine each planet’s mass with that of any satellites into a single point particle located at the planet-satellite barycenter. We ignore stellar mass- loss, the solar quadrupole, tides, and all minor planets and asteroids. We do",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_009,9,"as initial conditions. We combine each planet’s mass with that of any satellites into a single point particle located at the planet-satellite barycenter. We ignore stellar mass- loss, the solar quadrupole, tides, and all minor planets and asteroids. We do however include general relativis- tic corrections in the form of an additional potential (A. Nobili & I. W. Roxburgh 1986; D. Tamayo et al. 2020) as the additional precession rate is very important to correctly capture the long-term stability of the Solar System (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). Numerical experiments by D. S. Abbot et al. (2023) suggest that this simplified physical setup incorporates all the relevant physics to capture the in- stability rate of Mercury. We determine if a system is unstable by monitoring the eccentricity of planets. Specifically, we define a sys- tem as unstable if an eccentricity (most likely that of Mercury) reaches 0.7 at any time during the integra- tion. We ran additional tests with different instability criteria, but they do not change the results significantly (see also Table",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_010,10,"sys- tem as unstable if an eccentricity (most likely that of Mercury) reaches 0.7 at any time during the integra- tion. We ran additional tests with different instability criteria, but they do not change the results significantly (see also Table 1 of the Supplementary Information of J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009). 2.1.Instability rate in 5 Gyr integrations As a first test, we run sets of 5 Gyr integrations of the Solar System with different integrators, Hamilto- nian splittings, and timesteps and measure the insta- bility rate in each set. Each simulation is slightly per- turbed by changing the position of Earth on the order of a few meters to obtain a set of equally valid realizations. The results are shown in Table 2, together with previous results in the literature. We see that integrations using Jacobi coordinates all agree with the instability rate of∼1% determined by J. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009). However, our simula- tions with DHC and timesteps of∼6 days suppress all instabilities. The fact that we reproduce this result with different integrators and agree with the",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_011,11,"with the instability rate of∼1% determined by J. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009). However, our simula- tions with DHC and timesteps of∼6 days suppress all instabilities. The fact that we reproduce this result with different integrators and agree with the integrations of N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) is reassuring as it shows this effect is not due to a specific implementation. Importantly, we are able to reproduce the correct in- stability rate with DHC simulations that use an order of magnitude smaller timestep of 0.6 days (bottom row). This suggests that the issue at hand is a simple con- vergence issue that can be resolved by reducing the timestep. We will show more evidence for this inter- pretation in the next section. 3 Table 1.Comparison of instability rates for 5 Gyr simulations of the Solar System using Jacobi (top) vs. democratic heliocentric (bottom) coordinates, using a range of integrators. Integrations are sorted in descending order by timestep. Additional columns list the number of runs in each simulation suite, as well as whether the timestep was decreased once",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_012,12,"Jacobi (top) vs. democratic heliocentric (bottom) coordinates, using a range of integrators. Integrations are sorted in descending order by timestep. Additional columns list the number of runs in each simulation suite, as well as whether the timestep was decreased once Mercury’s eccentricity rose above a particular threshold (that varies by paper). The final column lists the percentage of cases in which Mercury goes unstable, with errors denoting 1σcounting statistics. Integrations in Jacobi coordinates agree statistically on the instability rate over a broad range of timesteps, while the DHC integrations only converge on the correct instability rate for timesteps ≲0.6 days. Coordinates Integrator Timestep Runs VarieddtReference Instability rate Jacobi SABA 9.3 days 2501 Yes J. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) 0.9%±0.1% Jacobi WHCKL 8.1 days 1008 No D. S. Abbot et al. (2021) 1.2%±0.3% Jacobi WHFast 6 days 640 No This Paper 0.5%±0.3% Jacobi HNBody 4 days 1600 Yes R. E. Zeebe (2015) 0.6%±0.2% Jacobi WHFast 3.16 days 2750 No D. S. Abbot et al. (2023) 0.8%±0.2% Jacobi WHFast 3.16 days 2750 Yes D. S. Abbot et al. (2023) 1.0%±0.2% DHC",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,560,740
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_013,13,"Paper 0.5%±0.3% Jacobi HNBody 4 days 1600 Yes R. E. Zeebe (2015) 0.6%±0.2% Jacobi WHFast 3.16 days 2750 No D. S. Abbot et al. (2023) 0.8%±0.2% Jacobi WHFast 3.16 days 2750 Yes D. S. Abbot et al. (2023) 1.0%±0.2% DHC WHFast512 6.5 days 1000 No This Paper 0% DHC WHFast512 6 days 400 No This Paper 0% DHC WHFast 6 days 960 No This Paper 0% DHC MERCURY 1.5 days 1000 No N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) 0.1%±0.1% DHC WHFast512 0.6 days 480 No This Paper 1.3%±0.5% 2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next run a set of simulations for 105yrs with both DHC and Jacobi coordinates. We again use the present day values for all initial orbital parameters except for Mercury’s eccentricitye 1which we adjust manually this time. To measure the errors of different schemes, we compare them against integrations using the high-order, adaptive-timestep IAS15 integrator (H. Rein & D. S. Spiegel 2015), which obtains correct results to within accumulated floating-point errors. In Fig. 1 we plot the absolute error in the precession rate of Mercury as a",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,700,880
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_014,14,"against integrations using the high-order, adaptive-timestep IAS15 integrator (H. Rein & D. S. Spiegel 2015), which obtains correct results to within accumulated floating-point errors. In Fig. 1 we plot the absolute error in the precession rate of Mercury as a function of the timestep for the three cases ofe 1= 0.4, 0.6, and 0.8. We also plot the precession rate of Mercury due to general relativistic effects (horizontal line) and the timescale for Mercury to cross the pericenter (vertical line) defined as: Tf≡2π n1(1−e 1)2 p 1−e2 1,(1) wheren 1is the mean motion of Mercury (J. Wisdom 2015). Note that the pericenter timescale can be sig- nificantly shorter than the orbital period for eccentric orbits. One can see that the WH integrator with Jacobi co- ordinates does not result in a significant precession er- ror for timesteps≲10 days. On the other hand, the WH integrator with DHC introduces an artificial nu- merical precession which is strongly dependent on both the timestep and the eccentricity of Mercury. For large eccentricities, the artificial numerical precession rate be- comes larger than the GR",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,840,1020
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_015,15,"other hand, the WH integrator with DHC introduces an artificial nu- merical precession which is strongly dependent on both the timestep and the eccentricity of Mercury. For large eccentricities, the artificial numerical precession rate be- comes larger than the GR precession rate. Previousstudies have shown that the instability fraction is sen- sitive to GR and any additional precession (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). We therefore expect a significant effect on the stability rate from such numerical effects. We note that the artificial numerical precession has the same direction as the physical GR precession, and that the breakdown of the WH integrator with DHC occurs when the timestep becomes comparable to the pericenter-crossing timescale,dt≳T f/4. We thus con- firm our earlier claim that this issue is due to the non- convergence of simulations with DHC. 2.3.A physical cause for the different instability rates Our results above suggest that simulations using DHC only break down when eccentricities reach large values. To explore this further, we take one of the unstable Solar System integrations and restart",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,980,1160
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_016,16,"DHC. 2.3.A physical cause for the different instability rates Our results above suggest that simulations using DHC only break down when eccentricities reach large values. To explore this further, we take one of the unstable Solar System integrations and restart it 5 Myrs prior to the eccentricity of Mercury reaching large (0.7) values. We then integrate it forward in time for 20 Myrs. We once again run simulations with different coordinate systems and timesteps. This time we also vary the general rela- tivistic precession rate artificially from 0 (no GR preces- sion), to 1 (normal GR precession), to 2 (double the GR precession). We run 100 simulations for each case and slightly perturb again the position of Earth to obtain a set of equally valid realizations. This setup lets us quickly measure the instability rate for an ensemble of simulations. We chose to restart sim- ulations 5 Myrs before the instability, roughly one Lya- punov timescale (J. Laskar 1989), to give individual sim- ulations time to diverge slightly through phase space. If 4 1 2 46 10 20 40 dt",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,1120,1300
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_017,17,"We chose to restart sim- ulations 5 Myrs before the instability, roughly one Lya- punov timescale (J. Laskar 1989), to give individual sim- ulations time to diverge slightly through phase space. If 4 1 2 46 10 20 40 dt [days]105 103 101 1011031051 ['' / Julian century] GR precession rate Pericenter timescalee1=0.4 Democratic Heliocentric Jacobi 1 2 46 10 20 40 dt [days]GR precession rate Pericenter timescalee1=0.6 1 2 46 10 20 40 dt [days]GR precession rate Pericenter timescalee1=0.8 Figure 1.Error in Mercury’s precession rate as a function of timestep for different eccentricities of Mercury. The blue curve shows the error in simulations using Jacobi coordinates. The red curve shows the error in simulations using democratic heliocentric coordinates. Also shown are the pericenter timescale of MercuryT fas a vertical line and the precession rate of Mercury due to general relativity. 1 2 3 4 5 time [Gyrs]0.00.20.40.60.81.0Eccentricity of Mercury Jacobi Coordinates 1 2 3 4 5 time [Gyrs] Democratic Heliocentric Coordinates Figure 2.Left panel shows Mercury’s eccentricity as a function of time in 640 integrations using Jacobi coordinates, exhibiting",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,1260,1440
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_018,18,"relativity. 1 2 3 4 5 time [Gyrs]0.00.20.40.60.81.0Eccentricity of Mercury Jacobi Coordinates 1 2 3 4 5 time [Gyrs] Democratic Heliocentric Coordinates Figure 2.Left panel shows Mercury’s eccentricity as a function of time in 640 integrations using Jacobi coordinates, exhibiting near vertical take-offs when the frequency dominantly associated with Mercury’s eccentricity precessiong 1becomes resonant with that of Jupiterg 5. Right panel shows the same for 640 integrations using democratic heliocentric coordinates, where the vertical takeoffs are suppressed by artificial precession (see text). Both sets of integrations use a 6 day timestep. Table 2.Instability rate in simulations restarted just prior to a phase of high eccentricity in Mercury’s orbit. Coordinates Timestep GR Instability rate Jacobi 6 days 1.0 100% Jacobi 6 days 2.0 0% Jacobi 6 days 0.0 100% DHC 6 days 1.0 0% DHC 0.6 days 1.0 100% we were to restart them much earlier, then the small per- turbations we add to the initial conditions would move most trajectories away from the high eccentricity phase we aim to explore. Our results are shown in Table 2. First, note that",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,1400,1580
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_019,19,"to restart them much earlier, then the small per- turbations we add to the initial conditions would move most trajectories away from the high eccentricity phase we aim to explore. Our results are shown in Table 2. First, note that just as in Sect. 2.1, when using a 6 day timestep, DHC inte-grations again suppress all instabilities. The instability rate of Jacobi coordinates is only recovered when the timestep for DHC is reduced to 0.6 days. Second, the runs with Jacobi coordinates and dou- ble the GR precession rate yield no instabilities. This reaffirms that a change in the precession rate of order ∼40′′/Julian century is sufficient to alter the instabil- ity rate (G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). When turning off GR precession completely in the simulations with Ja- cobi coordinates, we still see an instability rate of 100%. This shows that the sign of this change matters. More- over, because the artificial numerical precession present in DHC simulations is in the same direction as the GR precession, we can conclude that the artificial precession is making the simulations",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,1540,1720
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_020,20,"This shows that the sign of this change matters. More- over, because the artificial numerical precession present in DHC simulations is in the same direction as the GR precession, we can conclude that the artificial precession is making the simulations more stable in the same way that the GR precession does. 5 We can connect this directly to the dynamics behind these instabilities. Chaos causes the precession frequen- cies of the secular modes in the Solar System to diffuse on Gyr-timescales, particularly the frequencyg 1associ- ated with Mercury (e.g., N. H. Hoang et al. 2021). G. Bou´ e et al. (2012) showed that Mercury is lost when g1diffuses from its present day value of∼560′′/Julian century down to∼425′′/Julian century, where the sepa- ratrix of the secularg 1−g5resonance appears, and Mer- cury’s eccentricity approaches unity effectively instanta- neously. This rapid evolution on the secular timescale, which is much shorter than the Gyr timescale for chaotic diffusion, gives rise to the near-vertical unstable trajec- tories in the left panel of Fig. 2 where we plot the eccen- tricity of Mercury as a function",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,1680,1860
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_021,21,"the secular timescale, which is much shorter than the Gyr timescale for chaotic diffusion, gives rise to the near-vertical unstable trajec- tories in the left panel of Fig. 2 where we plot the eccen- tricity of Mercury as a function of time in simulations using Jacobi coordinates (left) and DHC (right). Asg 1approaches resonance in the DHC integra- tions and the eccentricity of Mercury starts to rise, the eccentricity-dependent artificial precession (Fig. 1) raisesg 1and detunes it away from resonance, remov- ing the separatrix and suppressing further eccentricity growth. In the right panel of Fig. 2 we see several in- stances where the eccentricity of Mercury starts to rise, only to fall back down as the artificial precession moves the system away from resonance. This self-limiting be- haviour thus shuts down the primary pathway to Mer- cury’s instability.",2.TESTS,2.TESTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,180,138,False,False,1820,1958
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_022,22,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec. 2 show clear evidence that Ja- cobi coordinates perform better than DHC in Solar Sys- tem integrations. This is because when using Jacobi co- ordinates for the Hamiltonian splitting, Mercury’s mo- tion is decomposed into a Keplerian step on a two-body orbit around the Sun, and an interaction step with the other planets. In the limit where Mercury has a high eccentricity, the Kepler solver still correctly solves for the Keplerian motion around the Sun-Mercury barycen- ter for arbitrarily large timesteps. And because both the orbital timescales and the timescales for planet- planet interactions are much longer than the pericenter timescaleT f, no significant errors are expected by not resolvingT f(see Fig. 1). DHC on the other hand split the Hamiltonian into three parts: Keplerian motion, planet-planet interac- tions, and a so-called jump step or solar term (see M. J. Duncan et al. 1998; H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2019). The jump step depends on Mercury’s momentum (and that of",3.DISCUSSION,3.DISCUSSION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_023,23,"Hamiltonian into three parts: Keplerian motion, planet-planet interac- tions, and a so-called jump step or solar term (see M. J. Duncan et al. 1998; H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2019). The jump step depends on Mercury’s momentum (and that of the other planets) relative to the Solar System barycenter. The Keplerian motion solved for in the Ke- pler step is also different from Jacobi case. Mercury does not orbit the Mercury-Sun barycenter as in the Jacobicase, but rather the Solar System barycenter5. In the limit of high eccentricity, the difference between Mer- cury’s true trajectory and the Keplerian orbit the Ke- pler solver solves for becomes significant especially near pericenter. The error of a symplectic integrator (or more gener- ally an operator splitting method) can be understood in terms of an infinite sum of commutators consisting of compositions of the integrator’s individual steps (see e.g. P. Saha & S. Tremaine 1992; H. Rein et al. 2019; D. Tamayo et al. 2020). The commutators involving the jump step lead to error terms not present in the case of Jacobi coordinates and",3.DISCUSSION,3.DISCUSSION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_024,24,"integrator’s individual steps (see e.g. P. Saha & S. Tremaine 1992; H. Rein et al. 2019; D. Tamayo et al. 2020). The commutators involving the jump step lead to error terms not present in the case of Jacobi coordinates and it is those commutators which lead to the artificial numerical precession. The comb- like structure seen in the curve for DHC in Fig. 1 is due to strong timestep resonances (K. P. Rauch & M. Hol- man 1999). To suppress these timestep resonances and keep the artificial numerical precession rate below that of GR we see that we need to resolveT fwith at least a few (∼4) timesteps. The effect we discuss primarily affects the innermost planet, i.e. Mercury in the case of the Solar System. Venus, for example, has a 2.6 times longer orbital period than Mercury. We are guaranteed to resolve Venus’ peri- center timescale up to eccentricities ofe 2∼0.4 as long as we resolve Mercury’s orbital period. Furthermore, as soon as the eccentricity of Venus reachese 2∼0.4 it crosses orbits with Mercury at which point close encoun-",3.DISCUSSION,3.DISCUSSION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_025,25,"guaranteed to resolve Venus’ peri- center timescale up to eccentricities ofe 2∼0.4 as long as we resolve Mercury’s orbital period. Furthermore, as soon as the eccentricity of Venus reachese 2∼0.4 it crosses orbits with Mercury at which point close encoun- ters can occur and all of the fixed timestep integrators we discuss break down.",3.DISCUSSION,3.DISCUSSION,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,71,54,False,False,420,474
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_026,26,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability rates of the Solar System have been reported in the literature, ranging from∼0.1% to 1% (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbot et al. 2021; D. S. Abbot et al. 2023; N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond 2025). In this paper we traced this discrepancy back to the coordinate system used in the Hamiltonian splitting of the Wisdom-Holman inte- grator. We showed that simulations using democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC) require a significantly smaller timestep in order to be statistically converged compared to simulations using Jacobi coordinates. The reason for this slow convergence is the artificial numeri- cal precession introduced by DHC which is strongly ec- centricity and timestep dependent. Our results show that DHC should not be used in sim- ulations which require high accuracy during times when 5More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen- tric velocities. See M. J. Duncan et al. (1998); H. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). 6 the eccentricity of",4.CONCLUSIONS,4.CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_027,27,"not be used in sim- ulations which require high accuracy during times when 5More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen- tric velocities. See M. J. Duncan et al. (1998); H. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). 6 the eccentricity of planets is high. The Solar System is one example where high accuracy is required to measure the correct instability rate because the eccentricity of Mercury becomes large (≳0.4) just before instabilities occur and numerical errors can suppress the behaviour. Reducing the timestep in simulations using DHC is one possible solution. Another, pursued by H. F. Lev- ison & M. J. Duncan (2000) and T. Lu et al. (2024) is to shift the problematic terms in the jump step during close pericenter passages into the Keplerian step and in- tegrate it using Bulirsch-Stoer (J. E. Chambers 1999). However, we note that a simple scheme using Jacobi co- ordinates can reproduce the correct instability rate for surprisingly large timesteps (H. Rein et al. 2025). As a result, statistically converged simulations with DHC are significantly more computationally expensive than simulations using Jacobi coordinates",4.CONCLUSIONS,4.CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_028,28,"a simple scheme using Jacobi co- ordinates can reproduce the correct instability rate for surprisingly large timesteps (H. Rein et al. 2025). As a result, statistically converged simulations with DHC are significantly more computationally expensive than simulations using Jacobi coordinates so that Jacobi co- ordinates are advantageous in most scenarios. We have focused our experiments and discussion on the Solar System. However, the general advice for run- ning simulations of any planetary system where one wants to integrate until an instability occurs is the same:Jacobi coordinates perform significantly better. As soon as orbit crossing occurs and planets can have close en- counters, Jacobi coordinates are no longer suitable. Of- ten at this point a simulation can be stopped and the system can be considered unstable. But if it is necessary to integrate further, then a switch to a hybrid symplec- tic integrator or a brute force non-symplectic integra- tor can be made at this point. Since hybrid integrators work in DHC, it is important to check that the timestep is small enough, at the very leastdt≲T f/4, or ideally dt≲T",4.CONCLUSIONS,4.CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_029,29,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thank Nathan Kaib, Sean Raymond, and Garett Brown for helpful discussions. We are also grateful for Muhammed Sajanlal’s help with a literature review. This research has been supported by the Natural Sci- ences and Engineering Research Council (NSERC) Dis- covery Grant RGPIN-2020-04513.",ACKNOWLEDGMENTS,ACKNOWLEDGMENTS,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,77,59,False,False,0,59
