astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_039,39,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration −RGC Rd exp −|z| zd (16) is the unnormalised disk density. The remaining global nor- malisationZ Vcancels against the selection term, which we will introduce in Section 3.4. The true period and metallicity are nuisance latent pa- rameters that can be marginalised analytically. Combining the measurement likelihoods with the Gaussian population prior of Eq. (7), the joint marginalised likelihood is Lmarg(mW H,obs,i ,logP obs,i,[O/H] obs,i|di,Λ) =Z d logP id[O/H] iL(mW H,obs,i |di,logP i,[O/H] i,Λ) × L(logP obs,i|logP i)L([O/H] obs,i|[O/H] i) ×π(logP i|Λ)π([O/H] i|Λ).(17) The delta-function period likelihood of Eq. (14) collapses the integral overlogP i, settinglogP i= logP obs,iand leav- ing behind the period priorπ(logP obs,i|Λ). The true metallicity[O/H] ienters the magnitude likelihood linearly through theZ W[O/H] iterm of the period–luminosity rela- tion, while both the metallicity likelihood and population prior are Gaussian; the three factors are jointly Gaussian in [O/H] iand integrate analytically to give Lmarg(mW H,obs,i ,logP obs,i,[O/H] obs,i|di,Λ)",−RGC,−RGC,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,214,164,False,True,0,164
