astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_042,42,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025) 8R. Stiskalek et al. Cepheid photometry is modelled as a multivariate normal: L(mW H,j|µj,Λ) =N(mW H,j|MW H,j+µj,Σj),(22) whereMW H,jis the vector of predicted absolute magnitudes from the period–luminosity relation andΣ jis the SH0ES- reported covariance matrix. As for the MW Cepheids, the delta-function period likelihood setslogP k= logP obs,kin each predicted absolute magnitude, leaving behind the pe- riod prior factorsπ(logP obs,k|Λ). For N4258, which has spectroscopic[O/H]measurements,thetruemetallicitiesare marginalised as in Eq. (18), adoptingϵ [O/H] = 0.06 dex. Each Cepheid’s predicted absolute magnitude is evaluated at the effective metallicity[O/H] ⋆,kof Eq. (19), and the marginalisationaddsZ2 W˜σ2 [O/H]tothediagonalofthecovari- ance.BecauseeachCepheid’smagnitudedependslinearlyon its own metallicity, and the per-star metallicity posteriors are conditionally independent given the population hyper- parameters, the off-diagonal structure ofΣ jis unchanged. The marginalised anchor Cepheid likelihood is therefore Lmarg j=N mW H,j|MW H,j(logP obs,[O/H] ⋆,j) +µ j, Σj+Z2 W˜σ2","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,198,152,False,True,0,152
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_043,43,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration [O/H] ,(23) whereN jis the number of Cepheids in galaxyj, and logP obsand[O/H] ⋆,jdenote the vectors of observed pe- riods and effective metallicities for those Cepheids. For the LMC, the catalogue reports a single spectroscopic metal- licity value (Section 2); we therefore treat it as a fixed co- variate in the predicted absolute magnitudes, dropping the metallicity marginalisation and the covariance contribution Z2 W˜σ2 [O/H]Ifrom Eq. (23), and do not infer the population hyperparametersµ [O/H]andσ [O/H]for the LMC. Both anchor catalogues are truncated atlogP cut= −0.3(i.e.P cut≈0.5 day), excluding short-period Cepheids. Following the same selection framework as for the MW cam- paigns(Section3.4),theselection-adjustedanchorlikelihood is Eq. (23) divided by the detection probability—the prob- ability that a Cepheid drawn from the population prior sat- isfies the period cut. The per-star period prior in Eq. (23) remains the full GaussianN(logP obs,k|µlogP, σlogP); the per-star selection weight is unity for all observed Cepheids (since they all satisfylogP obs,k >logP cut), and only the normalisation factor contributes. Because there is no mag- nitude",[O/H],[O/H],,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_044,44,"period prior in Eq. (23) remains the full GaussianN(logP obs,k|µlogP, σlogP); the per-star selection weight is unity for all observed Cepheids (since they all satisfylogP obs,k >logP cut), and only the normalisation factor contributes. Because there is no mag- nitude selection for the LMC and N4258, the integral of the multivariate magnitude likelihood overmevaluates to unity irrespective of the covarianceΣ j, and the detection proba- bility reduces to p(S= 1|Λ) =Z∞ logP cutN(logP|µ logP, σlogP) d logP = ΦµlogP−logP cut σlogP .(24) The selection-adjusted anchor likelihood is there- fore Eq. (23) divided byp(S= 1|Λ)Nj.3.4 Selection modelling Both the C22 and C27 samples are subject to selection cri- teria. We account for these following the framework of Kelly (2007) (recently applied and extended by Stiskalek et al. 2026), which introduces two terms: a per-star selection weight and a normalisation factor. We model each selection criterion as a smooth cut on some observablex, with transi- tion widthw x(the sharp period cut of Eq. (24) is recovered in thew x→0limit). The selection function for a lower or upper threshold is, respectively, Slow(x) =",[O/H],[O/H],,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_045,45,"model each selection criterion as a smooth cut on some observablex, with transi- tion widthw x(the sharp period cut of Eq. (24) is recovered in thew x→0limit). The selection function for a lower or upper threshold is, respectively, Slow(x) = Φx−x min wx , Shigh(x) = Φxmax−x wx .(25) The selection function enters the posterior of Eq. (47) through two terms. The first is the per-star selection weight, given by Eq. (25) evaluated at the observed catalogue val- ues. For example, for a star with observed magnitudemand an upper limit atm max, the per-star selection weight is S(m) = Φmmax−m wm .(26) Thesecondtermisanormalisationfactor[p(S= 1|Λ)]−np, wheren pis the number of observed stars andp(S= 1|Λ)is the marginalised detection probability. This is obtained by integrating the selection function weighted by the likelihood and prior over all observed and latent variables: p(S= 1|Λ) =Z dxdθS(x)L(x|θ,Λ)π(θ|Λ),(27) wherexdenotes the observed quantities,θthe per-source latent parameters (such as distance),L(x|θ,Λ)the likeli- hood of the observables, andπ(θ|Λ)the prior, which may depend on some of the global parameters. For the Cepheid problem, we consider the per-star se- lection weight of Eq.",[O/H],[O/H],,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_046,46,"dxdθS(x)L(x|θ,Λ)π(θ|Λ),(27) wherexdenotes the observed quantities,θthe per-source latent parameters (such as distance),L(x|θ,Λ)the likeli- hood of the observables, andπ(θ|Λ)the prior, which may depend on some of the global parameters. For the Cepheid problem, we consider the per-star se- lection weight of Eq. (26) to comprise cuts on up to four observables: the Wesenheit magnitudemW H,obs, the parallax ϖobs, the pulsation periodlogP obs, and theH-band extinc- tionA H. We assume no selection on metallicity, though one could be straightforwardly included. We model the magni- tude selection as an effective cut in the Wesenheit band; the underlying photometric criteria are not strictly in Wesen- heit, but the Wesenheit magnitude is directly predictable from the period–luminosity relation, making this consider- ably easier in practice (see Section 5.4 for further discus- sion). The corresponding marginalised detection probability of Eq. (27) is p(S= 1|Λ) =Z dΩdddmdϖ ×d logPd logP obsd[O/H] d[O/H] obs × S(m, ϖ,logP obs, AH(d,Ω)) × L(m|d,logP,[O/H],Λ)L(ϖ|d,Λ) × L(logP obs|logP)L([O/H] obs|[O/H]) ×π(d,Ω)π(logP,[O/H]|Λ),(28) whereΩdenotes the angular position on the sky,m≡mW H the Wesenheit magnitude,ϖthe parallax, andA H(d,Ω) the line-of-sight (LOS)H-band extinction obtained from a combination of",[O/H],[O/H],,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,420,600
