astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_056,56,"applied), and ΦK(h(d);R(d))encapsulates allKselection cuts on the Gaussian observables. The dimensionKand the explicit formsofh(d)andR(d)foreachcampaignarederivedinSec- tions 3.4.1 and 3.4.2. Thus, the original nine-dimensional integral of Eq. (28) reduces to a numerical integral over(d, ℓ, b). The distance integralisevaluatedviaSimpson’sruleonafinegridforeach sightline, while the integral overΩ= (ℓ, b)is approximated by Monte Carlo sampling. For a uniform-in-volume prior with no extinction cut, the integrand would be independent ofΩand the detection probability would reduce to a single one-dimensional integral overd; the disk geometry of Eq. (2) and the sightline-dependent extinction, however, require the full angular integration. Expandingπ(d,Ω) =ϱ(d,Ω)/Z V from Eq. (2), whereϱ(d,Ω)is the unnormalised disk density of Eq. (16), the factorZ−1 Vappears identically in the per- star prior and in the detection probability and cancels in the posterior (Section 3.3). Only the unnormalised integral over ϱis therefore needed: p(S= 1|Λ) ≈4π ZVNlosNlosX j=1Zdmax dminddS ext(d,Ω j) ΦK(h(d);R(d)) ×ϱ(d,Ω j), (39) where theΩ jare sampled uniformly on the sky and the dis- tanceintegralalongeachsightlineisevaluatedviaSimpson’s ruleonafinegrid.Inpractice,weimportance-sampletheΩ j fromπ(Ω)(Eq. 5) to concentrate draws near the Galactic plane, whereϱis largest.",×Φ,×Φ,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,221,170,False,True,420,590
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_057,57,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 3.4.1 C27 selection The C27 sample targets nearby Cepheids, selected primarily on the photometry-predicted parallaxϖ pred>0.8 mascom- puted following Eq. (1). Because this selection was applied",3.4.1 C27 selection,3.4.1 C27 selection,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,51,39,False,False,0,39
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_058,58,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025) Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids11 using a fixed set of fiducial period–luminosity parameters, modelling the criterion exactly would couple the selection function to those assumed values. We instead approximate this as a smooth lower cut on the observed parallax atϖ min with a transition widthw ϖ= 0.05 mas, which is indepen- dent ofΛ. We similarly assume a lower cut on the Wesen- heit magnitude atmW H,minto exclude the nearest, brightest Cepheids. The effective thresholdmW H,minis inferred with a uniform prior. Although C27 also applied an extinction cut (AH<0.6 mag), we treat this as negligible for the nearby C27 sample. The per-star selection weight is therefore S(x obs,i) = Φϖobs,i−ϖ min wϖ Φ mW H,obs,i −mW H,min wm! , (40) depending only on the observed parallax and magnitude. For the marginalised detection probability, the[O/H] andlogPintegrals proceed as in Eqs. (30) and (31), but without a period selection cut; thelogPmarginalisation therefore yields a Gaussian inmwith varianceσ2 2and mean ˆmpred(d), without theΦfactor of Eq. (31). The two remain-","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_059,59,"and magnitude. For the marginalised detection probability, the[O/H] andlogPintegrals proceed as in Eqs. (30) and (31), but without a period selection cut; thelogPmarginalisation therefore yields a Gaussian inmwith varianceσ2 2and mean ˆmpred(d), without theΦfactor of Eq. (31). The two remain- ing selection factors act on separate Gaussian observables (K= 2,n= 2): the magnitude cut onmand the parallax cutonϖ.Sincetheseareindependentgiventhedistance,the correlation matrixRis diagonal andΦ 2(h;R)factorises as Φ(h 1) Φ(h 2). SettingS ext= 1in Eq. (38), the C27 detection probability is p(S= 1|Λ) =Z dΩddΦ(h 1(d)) Φ(h 2(d)) ×π(d,Ω),(41) where h1(d) =ˆmpred(d)−mW H,minp w2m+σ2 2, h2(d) =ϖpred(d)−ϖ min√w2ϖ+σ2ϖ.(42) Following Eq. (39), we evaluate Eq. (41) withS ext= 1, Nlos= 5000sightlines,d min= 0.1 kpc, andd max= 2.0 kpc. Because the analytic marginalisation reduces the problem to a set of one-dimensional integrals over distance, the detec- tion probability is computationally tractable even on-the-fly during sampling.","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,182,140,False,True,140,280
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_060,60,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 3.4.2 C22 selection The C22 sample is subject to four selection criteria. The original photometric selection was not performed in the We- senheit band; however, we approximate it as an effective upper cut on the Wesenheit magnitude,mW H< mW H,max, noting that the magnitude threshold is sufficiently broad that the sensitivity to the choice of band is minimised. We similarly model an effective lower cut on the observed par- allax,ϖ obs> ϖ min, and a lower cut on pulsation period, logP >logP min;anextinctioncut,A H< A H,max,isoption- allyincludedbutdisabledinthefiducialmodel(Section5.4). The effective thresholdsϖ minandmW H,maxare jointly in-ferred with uniform priors. The per-star selection weight is S(x obs,i, di) = Φ mW H,max−mW H,obs,i wm! Φϖobs,i−ϖ min wϖ ×ΦlogP obs,i−logP min wlogP ×ΦAH,max−AH(di, ℓi, bi) wAH ,(43) where the extinction factor depends on the latent distance dithrough the three-dimensional dust map. The marginalised detection probability follows Eq. (39). The period selection enters through theΦfactor of Eq. (31), which depends onmvia˜µ logP(m, d). Combined with the magnitude cut, this givesK= 3selection factors",3.4.2 C22 selection,3.4.2 C22 selection,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
