astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_062,62,"bivariate normal density with correlationt. The integrand is smooth and the inte- gral is computed to high accuracy with a low-order Gauss– Legendre quadrature rule, making the evaluation ofΦ 2es- sentially as fast as that of the univariateΦ. When the ex- tinction cut is enabled, the extinction selectionS ext(d,Ω) = Φ((A H,max−AH(d,Ω))/w AH)is included in the integrand; in the fiducial modelS ext= 1. We useN los= 5000sight- lines,d min= 0.1 kpc, andd max= 8.5 kpc.",3.4.2 C22 selection,3.4.2 C22 selection,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,98,75,False,True,280,355
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_063,63,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 3.4.3 LMC and N4258 selection The period truncation atlogP cut=−0.3common to both theLMCandN4258cataloguesisaccountedforasdescribed in Section 3.3 (Eq. 24). Beyond this, we do not model fur- ther selection effects for the LMC and N4258 Cepheids. A complete treatment would additionally address magnitude- dependent selection within each galaxy. For the LMC (µ≈ 18.5), Cepheids at the period cut havemW H≈17 mag, far brighter than anyHSTdetection limit; magnitude selection is therefore irrelevant. For N4258 (µ≈29.4), the faintest",3.4.3 LMC and N4258 selection,3.4.3 LMC and N4258 selection,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,114,87,False,True,0,87
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_064,64,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025) 12R. Stiskalek et al. Cepheids reachmW H≈28 mag, still brighter than but ap- proaching theHSTWFC3/IR detection limit in crowded fields. Modelling this would require integrating the multi- variate likelihood of Eq. (23) over the selection region and marginalising over the magnitudes of undetected Cepheids, whose covariance with the observed sample is not available.","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,90,69,False,False,0,69
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_065,65,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 3.5 Full posterior and inference Combining the priors, likelihoods, and selection modelling, the full posterior is P(Λ,{d i},{d j} |D)∝π(Λ) ×Y p∈{C22,C27}"" [pp(S= 1|Λ)]−np ×Y i∈pLiS(x obs,i, di)π(d i, ℓi, bi)# ×Y j∈{LMC,N4258}[pj(S= 1|Λ)]−NjLmarg jL(˜µj|µj)π(d j). (47) The first product runs over the MW campaigns, wheren pis the number of Cepheids in campaignp,L ithe marginalised per-star likelihood of Eq. (20),S(·)the per-star selection weight of Eqs. (40) and (43),p p(S= 1|Λ)the campaign- level detection probability of Eq. (28), andπ(d i, ℓi, bi)the joint position prior of Eq. (2). The second product runs over the LMC and N4258, wherep j(S= 1|Λ)is the per-star period detection probability of Eq. (24),Lmarg jthe marginalised host Cepheid likelihood of Eq. (23),L(˜µ j|µj) the geometric distance constraint of Eq. (21), andπ(d j) a uniform-in-volume distance prior. All Cepheids within a given host galaxy are placed at a common distanced j, which is a free parameter of the model. Thesampledparametersarethereforetheglobalparam- etersΛ—comprising the shared period–luminosity relation parameters, the parallax offset, the per-campaign intrin- sic scatters, and",3.5 Full posterior and inference,3.5 Full posterior and inference,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_066,66,"distance prior. All Cepheids within a given host galaxy are placed at a common distanced j, which is a free parameter of the model. Thesampledparametersarethereforetheglobalparam- etersΛ—comprising the shared period–luminosity relation parameters, the parallax offset, the per-campaign intrin- sic scatters, and the population hyperparameters—together with the distanced ito each MW Cepheid and the distance djto each host galaxy. Table 1 lists the observables enter- ing the model, and Table 3 collects all inferred and fixed parameters alongside the populations to which they apply; in total the model has91sampled parameters. We imple- mentthemodelinJAXandsamplewiththeNo-U-TurnSam- pler (NUTS; Hoffman & Gelman 2011) vianumpyro2(Phan et al. 2019). We run four independent chains, each with1000 warm-up and5000sampling steps, and verify convergence by requiring ˆR <1.01for all parameters; we collect at least 5000effective samples per parameter. For comparison, we also implement the R21 parallax- space model, which avoids sampling per-star distances. For each Cepheid, we compute a photometric parallax from the observed Wesenheit magnitude and the period–luminosity relation, ϖphot= 10−0.2 (mW",3.5 Full posterior and inference,3.5 Full posterior and inference,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,212,163,True,True,140,303
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_067,67,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration H−MW H,pred−10),(48) 2num.pyro.aiand theχ2per star is χ2 i=(ϖobs−ϖ phot+δϖ)2 ˜σ2 ϖ,i,(49) where ˜σ2 ϖ,i= (0.2 ln 10ϖ photσm,tot)2+ (α σ ϖ,EDR3 )2(50) combining the propagated magnitude uncertainty with the Gaiaparallax error inflated by a factorα= 1.1. This model applies no additional selection modelling and fixes the in- trinsic scatter to0.06 mag3.",H−MW,H−MW,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,84,64,False,True,0,64
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_068,68,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 4 RESULTS The fiducial inference combines the C22 and C27 samples with the disk distance prior (Eq. 2) and selection modelling applied to both MW campaigns; the LMC and N4258 are optionally included as additional geometric calibrators. The term “MW Cepheids” denotes the joint C22+C27 sample; individual campaign results appear in Appendix B. Subse- quent variants omit the selection modelling or replace the disk prior with a uniform-in-volume prior. The R21χ2method applied to the MW Cepheids yields results closely matching our forward model. In Appendix D we show that this agreement is driven by the small intrinsic scatteroftheperiod–luminosityrelation,andinAppendixE we validate the inference methods on mock catalogues mim- icking the C22 and C27 campaigns.",4 RESULTS,4 RESULTS,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,164,126,False,False,0,126
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_069,69,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 4.1 Fiducial posteriors Figure 4 shows the marginalised posterior of the period– luminosity parameters (MW H,1,bW,ZW) and the parallax off- setδ ϖfor three model configurations: (i) C22 and C27 with the disk prior and selection modelling but without the LMC and N4258, (ii) the same with the LMC and N4258 Cepheid populations and their geometric distance calibrations, and (iii) a model including all four populations that neglects se- lection modelling, adopts a uniform-in-volume MW distance prior, and uses a wideδ ϖprior, designed to emulate the analysis of HM26. The MW-only posterior is substantially wider, by fac- tors of1.8,2.1, and2.1inMW H,1,bW, andZ W, respectively, but only by a factor of1.2inδ ϖ. Bothb WandZ Ware strongly correlated withMW H,1in the MW-only inference; including the LMC and N4258 Cepheids breaks these de- generacies.Sinceδ ϖentersonlythroughtheMWparallaxes, the LMC and N4258 do not substantially tighten the con- straint; the fiducial posterior givesδ ϖ=−12.4±5.3µas, compared with−9.9±6.1µasfrom the MW-only infer- ence. For comparison, Cruz Reyes & Anderson (2023) report δϖ=−19±3µasfromGaiaDR3 parallaxes of open clusters hostingCepheids;thedifferencereflectsthesensitivityofthe",4.1 Fiducial posteriors,4.1 Fiducial posteriors,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_070,70,"these de- generacies.Sinceδ ϖentersonlythroughtheMWparallaxes, the LMC and N4258 do not substantially tighten the con- straint; the fiducial posterior givesδ ϖ=−12.4±5.3µas, compared with−9.9±6.1µasfrom the MW-only infer- ence. For comparison, Cruz Reyes & Anderson (2023) report δϖ=−19±3µasfromGaiaDR3 parallaxes of open clusters hostingCepheids;thedifferencereflectsthesensitivityofthe inferred offset to the assumed prior and sample composition. The addition of the LMC and N4258 introduces no tension 3We note that R21 unintentionally fixed the parameters in theϖ photexpression (Eq. (48)) when computing the error terms (Eq. (50)), but we allow them to vary here, which may contribute to the∼0.01 magdifference between R21 as reported and our emulation as shown in Table 4.",4.1 Fiducial posteriors,4.1 Fiducial posteriors,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,136,104,False,True,140,244
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_071,71,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration ZW50 0 δ[µas] C22 + C27 (disk prior, sel.) C22 + C27 (R21 χ2) C22 + C27 + LMC + N4258 (disk prior, sel.) C22 + C27 + LMC + N4258 (unif-in-vol prior, no sel., wide δ) (emulating HM26) Baseline SH0ES Figure 4.Marginalised posterior of the period–luminosity parameters(MW H,1, bW, ZW)and the parallax offsetδ ϖfor three model configurations: C22 and C27 (MW only) with a MW disk distance prior and selection (blue, filled), the fiducial model including the LMC and N4258 with the same MW disk prior and selection (red, filled), and all four populations with a uniform-in-volume MW distance prior, no selection, and a wideδ ϖprior, emulating HM26 (orange). The0.7σagreement inMW H,1with the baseline SH0ES value translates to a shift inH 0of less than0.2 km s−1Mpc−1(Section 5.3). The baseline SH0ES contours from R22 are shown in black, and the R21χ2 contours for C22+C27 are shown in green. The baseline SH0ES (R22) constraints derive from the full three-rung fit (geometric anchors, Cepheids, and Type Ia supernovae), while",ZW50,ZW50,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_072,72,"5.3). The baseline SH0ES contours from R22 are shown in black, and the R21χ2 contours for C22+C27 are shown in green. The baseline SH0ES (R22) constraints derive from the full three-rung fit (geometric anchors, Cepheids, and Type Ia supernovae), while the R21χ2uses only the MW Cepheid parallaxes. For visual clarity, the SH0ES constraints are dashed in the panels along the diagonal. All contours show the1σand2σcredible regions. with the MW-only result. We also compare these posteriors to the baseline SH0ES contours from the joint three-rung fit reported in table 5 of R22, combining geometric calibrators, MW and extragalactic Cepheids, and Type Ia supernovae. All three parameters agree with our fiducial posteriors to within1σ.Applying the R21χ2method to the C22 and C27 sam- ples yieldsMW H,1=−5.904±0.035 magandδ ϖ=−14.0± 6.3µas(Table 4). Compared to our MW-only forward model with selection (MW H,1=−5.889±0.040 mag,δ ϖ=−9.9± 6.1µas), theχ2method recovers a∼0.02 magbrighter zero- point and a∼4µasmore negative parallax offset. On the",ZW50,ZW50,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,199,153,False,True,140,293
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_073,73,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025) 14R. Stiskalek et al. other hand, our MW-only forward model zero-point more closely agrees with theMW H,1=−5.903±0.024 magre- ported in table 4 of R22 from a global period–luminosity fit to the R21 sample, differing by0.4σ. It likewise agrees with the baseline SH0ES value ofMW H,1=−5.894 mag(ta- ble 2 of R22) to within0.1σ. Including the LMC and N4258 tightens the constraint and brings the zero-point into closer agreement: the fiducialMW H,1=−5.909±0.022 magdiffers from the R22 table 4 value by0.006 mag(0.3σ) and from the baseline SH0ES value by0.7σ. This0.7σshift corresponds to a change inH 0of less than0.2 km s−1Mpc−1; the impli- cations are further discussed in Section 5.3. Figure 5 shows the marginalised posterior distributions of the per-campaign intrinsic scatterσ int. The C22 scatter peaks at∼0.07 mag, roughly1.8times larger than the C27 value of∼0.04 mag, possibly reflecting residual extinction scatter in the more distant C22 sample, where dust cor- rections are larger and the Wesenheit magnitude does not fully remove sightline-to-sightline variations in the redden- ing","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_074,74,"mag, roughly1.8times larger than the C27 value of∼0.04 mag, possibly reflecting residual extinction scatter in the more distant C22 sample, where dust cor- rections are larger and the Wesenheit magnitude does not fully remove sightline-to-sightline variations in the redden- ing law. Including the LMC and N4258 tightens the period– luminosity relation parameters but leaves the MW scatter posteriors unchanged, as expected sinceσ intis determined by the spread of MW magnitudes about the relation and is largely independent of the global zero-point. To test whether the MW, LMC, and N4258 scat- ters are consistent, we run two additional configurations. First, we impose a single sharedσ intacross all four pop- ulations (with the0.06 magalready inΣ jsubtracted in quadrature for the LMC and N4258). The∼500LMC and N4258 Cepheids dominate the constraint, yieldingσ int= 0.063±0.009 mag; without these populations the same shared model givesσ int= 0.051±0.015 mag, a1.7× wider uncertainty. Second, we allow separate scatters for C22, C27, and the LMC+N4258 jointly. The LMC+N4258 scatter isσLMC+N4258 int = 0.070±0.010 mag, roughly15% larger than the0.06 magassumed by R22, while the MW campaigns separate cleanly:σC22 int=","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_075,75,"int= 0.051±0.015 mag, a1.7× wider uncertainty. Second, we allow separate scatters for C22, C27, and the LMC+N4258 jointly. The LMC+N4258 scatter isσLMC+N4258 int = 0.070±0.010 mag, roughly15% larger than the0.06 magassumed by R22, while the MW campaigns separate cleanly:σC22 int= 0.071±0.022 mag andσC27 int= 0.041±0.015 mag. Despite these differences, the period–luminosity parameters are robust to the scat- ter model:MW H,1,bW, andZ Wshift by less than0.3σbe- tween the shared and per-campaign configurations when the LMC and N4258 are included. We also run models with a free parallax uncertainty scalingf ϖthat multiplies the re- portedGaiaparallax uncertainties (R21 adopt a fixed factor of1.1). With a single sharedσ intacross C22 and C27 in the MW-only configuration, we obtainσ int= 0.039±0.016 mag, fϖ= 1.23±0.16, andδ ϖ=−8.5±6.4µas(Table 4). The inferredf ϖ>1indicates that the reported parallax un- certainties may be mildly underestimated. Analysing each campaign separately with the LMC and N4258, we find fϖ= 1.24±0.18for C22 andf ϖ= 0.71±0.33for C27. In all cases,f ϖhas negligible impact on the inferred period– luminosity parameters and parallax offset. The fiducial result is robust to the choice ofδ ϖprior: switchingtoauniformpriorshiftsδ ϖby4.8µas(from−12.4","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_076,76,"the LMC and N4258, we find fϖ= 1.24±0.18for C22 andf ϖ= 0.71±0.33for C27. In all cases,f ϖhas negligible impact on the inferred period– luminosity parameters and parallax offset. The fiducial result is robust to the choice ofδ ϖprior: switchingtoauniformpriorshiftsδ ϖby4.8µas(from−12.4 to−17.2µas) whileMW H,1changes by0.013 mag, both well within the posterior uncertainties. Similarly, replacing the axisymmetricdiskpriorwiththespiral-arm-modulatedprior of Appendix A leaves the posteriors unchanged:δ ϖshifts by 0.4µasandMW H,1by0.001 mag. Posterior means and stan- dard deviations for all model variants are reported in Ta- ble 4. 0.025 0.050 0.075 0.100 0.125 0.150 σint[mag]0510152025Posterior densityC22 C27C22 + C27 (disk prior, sel.) C22 + C27 + LMC + N4258 (disk prior, sel.) Shared scatter C22 + C27 (disk prior, sel.)Figure 5.Marginalised posterior of the intrinsic scatterσ intfor C22 (solid) and C27 (dashed), comparing the MW-only model with separate per-campaign scatters (blue), the fiducial model including the LMC and N4258 with separate scatters (red), and the MW-only model with a single sharedσ intfor both campaigns (green dotted;0.051±0.015 mag). Including the LMC and N4258 and inferring their joint scatter yieldsσ int= 0.063±0.009 mag.","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,229,176,False,True,420,596
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_077,77,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration 4.2 Impact of selection modelling and distance prior To quantify the impact of selection modelling and the dis- tance prior, we replace the disk prior with a uniform-in- volumepriorfortheMWCepheids(retainingonlytheafore- mentioned per-campaignd minandd maxfor normalisation) and disable the selection modelling. The resulting posteri- ors are incompatible with the fiducial model:MW H,1shifts to brighter values by∼0.05 magandδ ϖto more negative values by∼14µas. Table 4 reports further model variants that isolate the individual contributions of the selection modelling and dis- tance prior. Disabling the selection modelling alone (disk prior, no selection) in the C22+C27+LMC+N4258 con- figuration shiftsδ ϖfrom−12.4to−22.3µasandMW",4.2 Impact of selection modelling and distance,4.2 Impact of selection modelling and distance,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,141,108,False,False,0,108
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_078,78,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration H,1 from−5.909to−5.938 mag; additionally replacing the disk prior with a uniform-in-volume prior movesδ ϖto−26.4µas andMW H,1to−5.954 mag. The selection modelling and the disk prior therefore act in the same direction, each pulling δϖtoward zero andMW H,1toward fainter values. Their con- tributions differ substantially, however. Replacing the disk prior with a uniform-in-volume prior while retaining the se- lection modelling shiftsMW H,1by only0.002 magandδ ϖ by0.2µas, well within the posterior uncertainties. Selec- tion modelling alone accounts for∼0.03 maginMW H,1and ∼10µasinδ ϖ, substantially larger than the prior effect. Once the selection function is correctly modelled, the shape of the distance prior—disk or uniform-in-volume—is effec- tively irrelevant: the per-star likelihood constrains each dis- tance to a narrow interval over which the disk geometry varies minimally. Without the LMC and N4258, the shifts are larger still: the MW-only inference without selection yieldsδ ϖ=−23.6±6.0µasandMW H,1=−5.950±0.039 mag with the disk prior, andδ ϖ=−30.1±6.2µasandMW","H,1","H,1",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,207,159,False,True,0,159
