astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_126,126,"Lavaux G., McAlpine S., Jasche J., 2026, MNRAS, 546, staf2260 Tammann G. A., Sandage A., Reindl B., 2003, A&A, 404, 423 Tully R. B., et al., 2023, ApJ, 944, 94 Udalski A., Szymanski M., Kaluzny J., Kubiak M., Mateo M., 1992, Acta Astronomica, 42, 253 Verde L., Schöneberg N., Gil-Marín H., 2024, ARA&A, 62, 287 Yasin T., Stiskalek R., Desmond H., von Hausegger S., Ferreira P. G., 2026, arXiv e-prints, p. arXiv:2602.06007 Zinn J. C., 2021, AJ, 161, 214 da Silva R., et al., 2022, A&A, 661, A104","908, L6","908, L6",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,115,88,False,False,140,228
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_127,127,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration OF THE DISTANCE PRIOR MW Cepheids are young stars that trace the Galactic spi- ral structure. The axisymmetric disk prior of Eq. (2) does not capture this azimuthal dependence and may therefore assign non-negligible probability to inter-arm regions where few Cepheids reside. We optionally modulate the disk prior with a spiral arm density profile to test whether accounting for this structure affects the inferred parameters. We adopt the four-arm log-periodic spiral model of Drimmel et al. (2025), derived from2857classical Cepheids with WISE mid-infrared distances (Skowron et al. 2025). The four arms—Scutum, Sagittarius–Carina, Local (Orion), and Perseus—are each described by a trace in the Galactic plane, parametrised as lnR= lnR 0,k−tanψ kϕ,(A1) whereRis the Galactocentric radius,ϕthe azimuthal angle, R0,kthe reference radius, andψ kthe pitch angle of thekth arm. The spiral-modulated prior is the joint position density of Eq. (2) scaled by a factor that enhances the density near the arm traces: π(di, ℓi, bi)∝π disk(di, ℓi, bi)Sarm(di, ℓi, bi),(A2) where the spiral modulation factor is Sarm= (1−f arm)",OF THE DISTANCE PRIOR,OF THE DISTANCE PRIOR,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_128,128,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration ,(A3) πdiskis the joint position density of Eq. (2),∆ k(di, ℓi, bi) is the projected distance in the Galactic plane from the Cepheid to the nearest point on thektharm trace,f arm∈[0,1]isthefractionoftheCepheidsurfacedensityattributed to spiral arms, andσ armis the Gaussian arm width. When farm= 0, the spiral factor reduces to unity and the axisym- metricdiskpriorisrecovered.Attheoppositelimitf arm= 1, the prior is concentrated entirely on the arm traces. Both farmandσ armare sampled as free parameters:f armwith a uniform prior on[0,1]andσ armwith a half-normal prior N+(0.3,0.22) kpc, truncated at zero. The nearest-point distances∆ kare computed by con- verting the heliocentric coordinates(d i, ℓi, bi)to Galacto- centric Cartesian coordinates(x GC, yGC)in the midplane and querying a KD-tree built from the densified arm traces of Drimmel et al. (2025). Because the arm traces are fixed, thesquareddistances∆2 kforeachsightlineonafinedistance grid are precomputed and cached; at inference time, the spi- ral factor is evaluated by interpolating∆2 kto the sampled distanced i. The prior of Eq. (A2) is normalised numeri- cally per sightline via Simpson’s rule on the",",(A3)",",(A3)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_129,129,"fixed, thesquareddistances∆2 kforeachsightlineonafinedistance grid are precomputed and cached; at inference time, the spi- ral factor is evaluated by interpolating∆2 kto the sampled distanced i. The prior of Eq. (A2) is normalised numeri- cally per sightline via Simpson’s rule on the same grid. An analogous precomputation is performed for the Monte Carlo sightlines entering the detection probability of Eq. (39), en- suring that the spiral modulation propagates consistently into both the per-star prior and the selection modelling. As reported in Table 4, the spiral-arm-modulated prior leaves the posteriors virtually unchanged relative to the axisym- metric disk prior.",",(A3)",",(A3)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,125,96,False,False,140,236
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_130,130,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration RESULTS Table B1 reports posteriors for the C22 and C27 cam- paigns analysed separately, each combined with the LMC and N4258; Fig. B1 shows the corresponding corner plots. The LMC and N4258 are required to break theMW H,1–δϖde- generacy inherent in parallax-only data, so we include them throughout. With the LMC and N4258—which dominate the con- straint onMW H,1—the two campaigns yield consistent zero- points:MW H,1=−5.865±0.026 mag(C22) and−5.916± 0.024 mag(C27), differing by1.4σ. The slopes and metallic- ity coefficients, likewise dominated by the LMC and N4258 data, agree to within0.5σ. Both individual-campaign zero- points are consistent with the joint C22+C27 result of MW H,1=−5.909±0.022 mag(Table 4), confirming that the twocampaignscarrynointernaltension.Selectionmodelling has a larger effect on C22 than on C27: disabling the selec- tion function shiftsMW H,1by0.040 magandδ ϖby11µasfor C22, compared with0.008 magand6µasfor C27. This is expected, as the more distant C22 sample is more strongly affected by magnitude truncation. Similarly, the nearby C27 Cepheids (d≲1.25 kpc,ϖ≳800µas) are largely insensi- tive to the parallax offset: forδ ϖ=−10µas, the induced",RESULTS,RESULTS,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_131,131,"C22, compared with0.008 magand6µasfor C27. This is expected, as the more distant C22 sample is more strongly affected by magnitude truncation. Similarly, the nearby C27 Cepheids (d≲1.25 kpc,ϖ≳800µas) are largely insensi- tive to the parallax offset: forδ ϖ=−10µas, the induced distance modulus shift is only∼0.02 mag, compared with ∼0.07 magfor the typical C22 parallax of∼300µas. The constraint onδ ϖis therefore driven by the more distant C22 sample. The fiducial C22 selection does not include the extinc- tion cutA H<0.4 mag, owing to the systematic uncertain- ties in three-dimensional dust maps discussed in Section 5.4. With the LMC and N4258 included, enabling the extinction selection has a negligible effect on the period–luminosity pa- rameters (∆MW H,1= 0.008 mag), but the inferred scatter in-",RESULTS,RESULTS,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,159,122,False,True,140,262
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_132,132,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025) 22R. Stiskalek et al. creases, consistent with residual extinction along the more distant C22 sightlines. The R21χ2rows apply the frequen- tistχ2method with intrinsic scatter fixed at0.06 mag.","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–26 (2025)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,56,43,False,False,0,43
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_133,133,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration COMPARISON The forward model samples a distanced ifor each MW Cepheid. Converting these to distance moduliµ forward,i = 5 log(d i/kpc)+10providesadirectcomparisonwiththepho- tometricdistancemoduliµ SH0ESpredictedfromthebaseline SH0ES period–luminosity parameters (MW H,1=−5.894 mag, bW=−3.299,Z W=−0.217). Figure C1 shows the per- star residuals⟨µ forward ⟩−µ SH0ESfor both the MW-only and fiducial models. The residuals scatter about zero with only a minor systematic offset. Including the LMC and N4258 tightens the period–luminosity relation but does not shift the per-star distance posteriors. Figure C2 shows the distribution of the posterior-mean residuals from the fiducial C22+C27+LMC+N4258 infer- ence, split by campaign. The C22 residuals have a mean off- set of+0.004 magwith a standard deviation of0.042 mag, while the C27 residuals have a mean of+0.025 magand a standard deviation of0.030 mag. The positive C27 off- set is expected: the referenceµ SH0ESis computed from the baseline SH0ES zero-point (MW H,1=−5.894 mag), which is0.015 magfainter than our fiducial value (MW",COMPARISON,COMPARISON,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,211,162,False,True,0,162
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_134,134,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration APPENDIX D: SIMILARITY OF THE FORWARD MODEL AND THEχ2METHOD Here we derive the relation between the forward model of Section 3 and theχ2treatment of R21. For simplicity, we treat the period and metallicity as known quantities with no population prior or selection, so that neither re- quires marginalisation; since they enter the Gaussian likeli- hood linearly, they could be marginalised analytically under a Gaussian population prior as in the main text. The per- star likelihood for a single MW Cepheid at distancedthen reduces to L(mW H,obs, ϖobs|d,θ) =N mW H,obs|M+µ(d), σ2 1",APPENDIX D: SIMILARITY OF THE,APPENDIX D: SIMILARITY OF THE,,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,137,105,False,True,0,105
