astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_136,136,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration (D2) Changing variables toϖ= 1/d−δ ϖ, so thatd= 1/(ϖ+δ ϖ) and|dd|= dϖ/(ϖ+δ ϖ)2, the parallax likelihood simplifiesand the integral becomes Lmarg∝Z dϖ(ϖ+δ ϖ)−4N mW H,obs|M+µ(ϖ), σ2 1",(D2),(D2),,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,52,40,False,True,0,40
astrobench_f31aa696a1,astrobench_f31aa696a1_chunk_137,137,"Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration ,(D3) whereµ(ϖ) =−5 log(ϖ+δ ϖ)+10. Following the main text, we define the photometric parallax as the geometric parallax corresponding to the photometric distance, ϖphot= 10−0.2 (mW H,obs−M−10).(D4) In theϖcoordinate, the magnitude likelihood peaks atϖ= ϖphot−δϖ, sinceµ(ϖ) =mW H,obs−Mwhenϖ+δ ϖ=ϖ phot. To marginalise Eq. (D3) analytically, we lineariseµ(ϖ) aroundϖ phot−δϖ. Since dµ dϖ=−5 ln 10 (ϖ+δ ϖ),(D5) a first-order Taylor expansion gives µ(ϖ)≈µ(ϖ phot−δϖ)−5 ln 10ϖ−ϖ phot+δϖ ϖphot.(D6) Substituting into the magnitude likelihood and using mW H,obs =M+µ(ϖ phot−δϖ), the residual is mW H,obs−M−µ(ϖ)≈5 ln 10ϖ−ϖ phot+δϖ ϖphot.(D7) The magnitude Gaussian becomes N mW H,obs|M+µ(ϖ), σ2 1 ≈σϖ,m σ1N␀ ϖ|ϖ phot−δϖ, σ2 ϖ,m ,",",(D3)",",(D3)",,,,,Forward-modelling Milky Way Cepheids: selection effects and physical priors in the Gaia-HST calibration,"Richard Stiskalek, Adam Riess, Harry Desmond, Guilhem Lavaux, Dan Scolnic",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-10T16:39:31Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09880v1,2603.09880,distribution_functions,151,116,False,True,0,116
