astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_021,21,"from φ= 0 when x >0 toφ=πwhenx <0. It is not hard to see that on a loop orbit the period of these oscillations in φ coincides with the period of the oscillations in both xand z, while on a box orbit the φperiod equals the period in x but is typically longer than the period in z. That is, when Jφ= 0 Ωφ=/braceleftBigg Ωx= ΩzwhenJz> Jzcrit(loop orbits) Ωx<ΩzwhenJz< Jzcrit(box orbits)(15) SinceR=|x|, on box orbits Ω r= 2Ωxand Ω φ= Ωr/2. Consider now orbits with non-zero Jφ. When Jφ/negationslash= 0 an orbit cannot reach the symmetry axis and it is no longer conﬁned to a plane. When Jφis small we have very anhar- monic evolution in φin that it changes slowly until the star approaches the axis, and then φrapidly increases by ∼π as the star passes the axis before evolving slowly as the star moves out toapocentre andback. Since the frequencies must be continuous functions on action space, Ω φmust lie close to Ωr/2 ifJzis small, and close to Ω zfor larger Jz. That is, the relationship (15) must",L∓R. (14),L∓R. (14),,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_022,22,"evolving slowly as the star moves out toapocentre andback. Since the frequencies must be continuous functions on action space, Ω φmust lie close to Ωr/2 ifJzis small, and close to Ω zfor larger Jz. That is, the relationship (15) must be approximately valid even for non-zero JφwithJzcritnow identiﬁed from the run of orbital frequencies. Applying these limiting forms of the frequency ratios to the ergodic relations (4), we ﬁnd that an ergodic DF must be such that ∂f/∂J z ∂f/∂J φ→1 asJφ→0 withJz> Jzcrit ∂f/∂J r ∂f/∂J φ→2 asJφ→0 withJz< Jzcrit.(16) For these limiting ratios of derivatives in equation to lead to required cancellations in equation (13), we must have that lim vφ→+0∂Jz ∂vφ=−lim vφ→+0∂Jφ ∂vφ(Jz> Jzcrit) (17)",L∓R. (14),L∓R. (14),,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,154,118,False,True,420,538
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_023,23,"Distribution functions for spheroids ©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000 Distribution functions for spheroids 5 Figure 4. Each black curve shows how Ω φ/Ωrvaries with cir- cularity cat a ﬁxed energy in a dark-halo like potential (upper panel) or a Plummer potential (lower panel). The red lines sh ow y=x. and lim vφ→+0∂Jr ∂vφ=−1 2lim vφ→+0∂Jφ ∂vφ(Jz< Jzcrit). (18) By considering an ergodic model we have derived relation- ships, (17) and (18), that are set by the potential alone and can be applied to models with any DF. Given that the derivatives of Jthat appear in equation (13) satisfy equations (17) and (18), it is now clear that the DF of even an anisotropic model must satisfy lim Jφ→0∂f/∂J z ∂f/∂J φ= 1 (Jz> Jzcrit). (19) and lim Jφ→0∂f/∂J r ∂f/∂J φ= 2 (Jz< Jzcrit) (20)","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000",,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,176,135,False,True,0,135
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_024,24,Distribution functions for spheroids 3.3 Summary At the beginning of this section we saw that ∂f/∂v φwill not vanish with vφunless certain products of derivatives of fand of the actions cancel nicely. We further saw that for such cancellation to occur the partial derivatives of fas Jφ→0 must satisfy ∂f/∂J r=1 2∂f/∂J φatJzless than a critical value and ∂f/∂J z=∂f/∂J φat lager Jz. These conditions should ensure that ∂f/∂v φvanishes by virtue of a relation that we inferred between derivatives of the actio ns with respect to vφ.,3.3 Summary,3.3 Summary,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,115,88,False,True,0,88
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_025,25,"Distribution functions for spheroids 4 DFS FOR SPHERICAL MODELS We now give an algorithm for deﬁning DFs f(J) that pro- duce acceptable spherical models. Given a point ( Jr,L) in reduced action space, we integrate the ODE dL dJr=−g(J), (21) whereg >0 is some positive function, in the direction ofdecreasing Jrand increasing LuntilJr= 0 and L=Lc. Then we set the value f(Jr,L) of the DF at our original point to the value f0(Lc) taken by a non-negative monotone decreasing function f0of one variable. In the case of a dark halo, a suitable choice for f0would be f0(L) = constant ×(1+J0/L)α (1+L/J0)βe−L2/J2 cut, (22) which is the DF deﬁned by equation (1) evaluated on J= (0,0,L). If the target were a dwarf spheroidal the exponen- tial DF introduced by Pascale et al. (2018) would be our starting point but the procedure would be the same. With this algorithm, the DF is deﬁned by the functions gandf0. The path of integration set by gdeﬁnes a series of orbits of decreasing eccentricity that all have the same phase-space density. The function f0says what",4 DFS FOR SPHERICAL MODELS,4 DFS FOR SPHERICAL MODELS,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_026,26,"would be the same. With this algorithm, the DF is deﬁned by the functions gandf0. The path of integration set by gdeﬁnes a series of orbits of decreasing eccentricity that all have the same phase-space density. The function f0says what this density is. If we set g= Ωr/Ωt, the Hamiltonian will be constant along the integration path, and the DF will be that of an ergodic model. If we set g <Ωr/Ωt,Lwill increase more slowly as Jrdecreases than in the ergodic case, with the consequence that our ﬁnal value LcofLwill be smaller than in the ergodic case and the DF f(Jr,L) =f0(Lc) will be larger than in the ergodic model. Thus setting g <Ωr/Ωt generates a radially biased model, while setting g >Ωr/Ωt generates a tangentially biased model. Regardless of what type of model we are generating, g must satisfy lim L→0g= 2 so∂f/∂v tvanishes with vt.",4 DFS FOR SPHERICAL MODELS,4 DFS FOR SPHERICAL MODELS,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,192,147,False,True,140,287
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_027,27,"Distribution functions for spheroids 4.1 Ergodic models We now use this algorithm to construct an ergodic model of a dark halo. This goal is achieved by setting gto an analytic approximationtoΩ r/Ωt.Weuseas ourmeasureofanorbit’s circularity c≡L L+Jr, (23) which ranges from zero for radial orbits to unity for circu- lar orbits. Each curve in Fig. 4 shows Ω t/Ωras a function of circularity along a sequence of orbits at a given energy. The curves in the lower panel are for orbits conﬁned by a Plummer potential, while those in the upper panel are for orbits conﬁned by a double-power-law potential, similar to that of a baryon-free dark halo. These two potentials diﬀer from one another as much as any two galactic potentials, yet in each case Ω t/Ωris not far from a linear function of the circularity c, so we have Ωt Ωr≃1 2+c/parenleftBigΩ κ−1 2/parenrightBig , (24) where Ω and κare the epicycle frequencies. In thecase of the double-power potential, we could reﬁne this approximation by ﬁtting a quadratic or cubic in cto the black curves in the upper panel",4.1 Ergodic models,4.1 Ergodic models,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_028,28,"Ωt Ωr≃1 2+c/parenleftBigΩ κ−1 2/parenrightBig , (24) where Ω and κare the epicycle frequencies. In thecase of the double-power potential, we could reﬁne this approximation by ﬁtting a quadratic or cubic in cto the black curves in the upper panel of Fig. 4, but the examples below indicate that this is unnecessary. The ratio of epicycle frequencies appearing in equation (24) is evaluated at the energy of the sequence of orbits through which we are integrating. From the potential we can obtain a expression for the ratio in terms of Lc(E), but L=Lcis the end-point of the integration. Hence we are obliged to guess its value. A sensible guess is Lc≃L+1.4Jr.",4.1 Ergodic models,4.1 Ergodic models,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,145,111,False,True,140,251
