astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_030,30,"vzandvφare identical and not distin- guishable fromthedistributionof vR.Infact, inthetopright panel the distribution of vzatr= 10kpc, plotted in orange, obscures the distributions of vRandvφ, plotted in black and cyan, respectively. The distributions are remarkably Gaus - sian: the dashed red curve is the Gaussian with the same standard deviation, σR= 126kms−1, as the actual vφdis- tribution. The velocity distributions at R= 50kpc plotted in Fig. 6 deviate more from a Gaussian because the trunca- tion of the vdistribution at vesc=√ −2Φ is more stringent – the dashed vertical line at right bottom of the velocity histograms marks vesc.","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000",,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,127,97,False,True,140,237
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_031,31,"Distribution functions for spheroids 4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy As explained above, to generate a radially-biased model we should take g <Ωr/Ωt, while continuing to satisfy the con- straint lim L→0g= 2. A possible choice of gis gβ(Jr,L) =gH(Jr,L)exp[−βvsin(cπ/2)], (26) wheregHis given by equation (25). The factor exp[−βvsin(cπ/2)] multiplying gHcausesgto be less than gHwhenβv>0 and greater than gHwhenβv<0, and thus creates radial/tangential bias depending on the sign o f βv. The sine within the exponential ensures satisfaction of the fundamental requirement that g→gHasL→0. The choice of a sine is arbitrary: any function that vanishes wit h its argument and has a ﬁnite range might be used. The centre row of panels in Fig. 5 shows the structure of a population with this DF when βv= 1. The left panel of the centre row shows that moving βvto a positive value has only a minor impact on the population’s radial density proﬁle but the centre panel shows that it causes σR> σt. The right panel shows that the diﬀerence, 147 /130kms−1, in the dispersions at R= 10kpc under-plays the diﬀerence between the",4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy,4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_032,32,"only a minor impact on the population’s radial density proﬁle but the centre panel shows that it causes σR> σt. The right panel shows that the diﬀerence, 147 /130kms−1, in the dispersions at R= 10kpc under-plays the diﬀerence between the vRandvtvelocity distributions. Now both dis- tributions deviate signiﬁcantly from Gaussians: the shift of orbits towards higher eccentricity has depressed the orang e curve for N(vt) around v≃vcand elevated it around vt∼0 by replacing circular orbits with eccentric orbits seen nea r apocentre. Note that at large |v|thevRandvtdistributions converge. This is because athigh speeds eccentric orbits se enat pericentre boost N(vt). The lower panel of Fig. 6 shows that atr= 50kpc the diﬀerences between the distributions ofvRandvtare qualitatively the same but quantitatively larger. The bottom row of panels in Fig. 5 shows a tangentially biased model obtained by setting βv=−0.5. The distribu- tion ofvRis now more sharply peaked than that of vtbe- cause negative βvenhances N(vt) atv∼vcwhile enhancing N(vR) atv∼0 by enhancing the number of circular orbits relative to eccentric orbits.",4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy,4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,221,170,False,True,140,310
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_033,33,"Distribution functions for spheroids 5 OBLATE MODELS The strategy used in the last section to obtain anisotropic models of spherical systems is not feasible in the oblate cas e because it requires a model of the frequency ratios Ω r/Ωφ and Ω z/Ωφas functions of J, and from section 3.2 we know that these ratios have a complex dependence on Jnear the planeJφ= 0 (cf. Fig 5 of Wright & Binney 2025). Moreover, an ergodic model cannot generate a ﬂattened gravitational potential – by the virial theorem, ﬂattening requires/angbracketleftbig v2 R/angbracketrightbig to exceed/angbracketleftbig v2 z/angbracketrightbig . Consequently, the strategy we pursued in the previous section to obtain anisotropic DFs by modifying an ergodic DF, makes less sense and will not be pursued here. Given a DF f(J), we deﬁne an auxiliary DF f′(J) by f′(J)≡/braceleftBigg f(Jr+1 2(|Jφ|−ǫ),Jz,ǫ)Jz< Jzcrit f(Jr,Jz+(|Jφ|−ǫ),ǫ)Jz> Jzcrit,(27) whereǫis a small constant with the dimensions of action and the relations Jzcrit(Jr) andJrcrit(Jz) can be computed by the algorithm described by Wright & Binney (2025) and implemented as the agamab function getJzcrit . The DF (27) satisﬁes equations (19)",5 OBLATE MODELS,5 OBLATE MODELS,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_034,34,"whereǫis a small constant with the dimensions of action and the relations Jzcrit(Jr) andJrcrit(Jz) can be computed by the algorithm described by Wright & Binney (2025) and implemented as the agamab function getJzcrit . The DF (27) satisﬁes equations (19) and (20). Let 0 /lessorequalslant w(J)/lessorequalslant1 be a weight function such that lim Jφ→0w(J) = 1 and lim Jφ→0∇Jw= 0, (28) then the DF we use for modelling is f′′(J) =wf′(J)+(1−w)f(J). (29) It’s straightforward to show that lim Jφ→0∇J(f′′−f′) = 0 (30) regardless of f, sof′′, likef′, satisﬁes equations (19) and (20). Figs. 7 and 8 show proﬁles of a stellar system that’s trapped in the potential well of a body that has the NFW density proﬁle and is ﬂattened to axis ratio c/a= 0.5. For this ﬁgure fin equations (27) and (29) took the form given by equation (1) with J= 0.7Jr+1.4Jz+|Jφ|andJ0,α,β, andJcutas given in Table 1. Fig. 7 shows in black the prin- cipal velocity dispersions at points in the equatorial plan e – note that at small radii, σφtends to σRas it should while σz is everywheresmaller.",5 OBLATE MODELS,5 OBLATE MODELS,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_035,35,"with J= 0.7Jr+1.4Jz+|Jφ|andJ0,α,β, andJcutas given in Table 1. Fig. 7 shows in black the prin- cipal velocity dispersions at points in the equatorial plan e – note that at small radii, σφtends to σRas it should while σz is everywheresmaller. Fig. 8shows the velocity distributi ons at points in the equatorial plane at R= 5kpc (upper panel) andR= 50kpc (lower panel). Crucially, none of the his- tograms has a central cusp. Note that at R= 50kpc the vz andvφhistograms have quite diﬀerent shapes even though their second moments are similar.",5 OBLATE MODELS,5 OBLATE MODELS,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,117,90,False,True,280,370
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_036,36,"Distribution functions for spheroids ©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000 Distribution functions for spheroids 7 Figure 5. Top row: a population of test particles with DF f(h0[J]) trapped in a spherical double power-law potential. In the left panel the red curve shows the density distribution that generates the potential and the black curve shows the density of test pa rticles. The square marks the point at which dln ρ/dlnr=−2. The middle panel shows the radial and tangential velocity dispersions in this isotropic model. In the right panel black and overplotted orange curve s show the distribution of vRandvtvelocity components 10kpc from the centre. The dashed red curve shows the Gaussian with the same standard deviation as N(vt). Centre row: the same diagnostics for a population of test particles with DF f(h2[J]) withβv= 1 trapped in the same potential. In the right panel the dashe d red curve shows the Gaussian with the same standard deviation as he orange di stribution of vt. Bottom row: as the middle row but for a tangentially biased population with f(h1[J]) with negative βv=−0.5. The weight","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000",,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
