astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_000,0,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects JOURNAL SECTION OR CONFERENCE SECTION Galactic disc warps from z= 2.5to modern epoch: ruling out observational eﬀects I.V. Chugunov,1,∗V.P. Reshetnikov,1and A.A. Marchuk1 1Pulkovo Astronomical Observatory, Russian Academy of Scie nces, St.Petersburg 196140, Russia (Received xx.xx.2025; Revised xx.xx.2025; Accepted xx.xx .2025) A signiﬁcant fraction of galaxies show warps in their discs, usually noticeable at its periphery. The exact origin of this phenomenon is not fully established , although multiple warp formation mechanisms are proposed. In this study, we create a sample of more than 1000 distant ( z/lessorsimilar2.5) edge-on galaxies imaged by HST and JWST. For these galaxies, we measurd characteristics of warps and ﬁnally analyse how their parameters and frequency chang e with time. We focus on our main result that galaxies with strong warps were more prevalent i n the past compared to the modern epoch. We check how selection eﬀects and varying image quali ty between objects in our sample could inﬂuence our results and conclude that varying fracti on of warped galaxies is",JOURNAL SECTION OR CONFERENCE SECTION,JOURNAL SECTION OR CONFERENCE SECTION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_001,1,"more prevalent i n the past compared to the modern epoch. We check how selection eﬀects and varying image quali ty between objects in our sample could inﬂuence our results and conclude that varying fracti on of warped galaxies is not caused by observational eﬀects, but represents a genuine evolution. Such a trend may be consistent with mergers and interactions between galaxies being the primar y mechanism of warp formation, as number density of galaxies decreases with time, implying hi gher rate of mergers and interactions in the past. PACS numbers: 98.62.Ai, 98.62.Hr Keywords: galaxies: evolution /emdash.cyr galaxies: high-redshift /emdash.cyr galaxies: spiral /emdash.cyr galaxies: structure",JOURNAL SECTION OR CONFERENCE SECTION,JOURNAL SECTION OR CONFERENCE SECTION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,137,105,False,False,140,245
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_002,2,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects 1. INTRODUCTION Galactic discs, both stellar and gaseous, can demon/hyphen.alt strate warps in vertical direction, or, in other words, deviation of their shape from the planar form. The frequency of warps depends on the detection thresh/hyphen.alt old, but common numbers in the literature indicate that nearly half of disc galaxies in the local Universe are warped. The disc of our Milky Way is also long known to be warped [ 1,2]. Warps can be primarily seen in galaxies observed edge-on, they usually have an amplitude of a few degrees and become noticeable at the periphery of a galaxy, making them harder to study [ 3–5]. As such, disc warps at high redshifts are far from being well-studied [ 6]. Although multiple scenarios are proposed to explain the origin and evolution of disc warps, it is still a mat/hyphen.alt ter of debate which of them indeed have place. Among discussed ones, there are bending modes in self-grav/hyphen.alt itating discs [ 7], perturbation of gaseous disc by ex/hyphen.alt",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_003,3,"the origin and evolution of disc warps, it is still a mat/hyphen.alt ter of debate which of them indeed have place. Among discussed ones, there are bending modes in self-grav/hyphen.alt itating discs [ 7], perturbation of gaseous disc by ex/hyphen.alt tragalactic magnetic ﬁeld [ 8], misalignment of dark matter halo [ 9], cosmic infall [ 10], and, ﬁnally, inter/hyphen.alt actions and minor mergers between galaxies [ 11,12]. It is already established that the prominence of warps depends on the environment, with the strongest warps found in the dense environment [ 13,14]. Probably, it can serve as evidence for the importance of the last mechanism. As such, in this work we study a sample of more than 1000 edge-on galaxies at redshift z/lessorsimilar2.5and measure the shapes of their discs. In this work, we ∗E-mail: chugunov21@list.ruuse a standard ﬂat ΛCDM cosmology with Ωm=0.3, ΩΛ=0.7,H0=70 km s−1Mpc−1, using a calculator of cosmological parameters [ 15].",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,198,152,True,True,140,292
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_004,4,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects 2. SAMPLE AND DATA ANALYSIS The ﬁnal sample of galaxies for this study is pre/hyphen.alt sented in [ 16] and consists of two subsamples. In the ﬁrst, there are objects from COSMOS ﬁeld observed by HST in F814W ﬁlter, selected in [ 17]. For a small part of galaxies from the mentioned work it was not possible to measure the shape of the disc (see below) and we limited ourselves with galaxies with stellar massM∗higher than 109M⊙, so only 780 galaxies (out of 950 in [ 17]) were studied in this work. These galax/hyphen.alt ies are mostly located at z/lessorsimilar1. The second subsample which was constructed in [16], is based on DAWN JWST Archive (DJA) data [ 18], which contains images from several deep JWST ﬁelds. For this JWST subsample, we end up with 247 galaxies with images available in F115W or F444W ﬁlters, or both. In total, our sample contains 1027 edge-on galaxies up to redshift z/lessorsimilar2.5. Their redshift and mass distribution is shown in",2. SAMPLE AND DATA ANALYSIS,2. SAMPLE AND DATA ANALYSIS,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_005,5,"For this JWST subsample, we end up with 247 galaxies with images available in F115W or F444W ﬁlters, or both. In total, our sample contains 1027 edge-on galaxies up to redshift z/lessorsimilar2.5. Their redshift and mass distribution is shown in Figure 1. The complete sample is available online at https:// github.com/IVChugunov/Distant_disc_warps . To measure the shapes of discs, in [ 16] we used an approach from [ 3]. The key method in this approach is the skeletonization of the isophotes. We built multi/hyphen.alt ple isophotes, and, for each one, we selected the area enclosed by it, constructed its skeleton image and se/hyphen.alt lected the longest “thread” in it. By deﬁnition, skele/hyphen.alt ton is a one-pixel wide line representing the general shape of the area inside the isophote. Using skele/hyphen.alt x–1arXiv:2602.01286v1 [astro-ph.GA] 1 Feb 2026 Modern Astronomy: Science and Education (to 270th Annivers ary of Moscow University) 0.0 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 z050100150200NSample galaxies mass and redshif",2. SAMPLE AND DATA ANALYSIS,2. SAMPLE AND DATA ANALYSIS,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,205,157,True,False,140,297
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_006,6,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects HST 109 M ⊙ Figure 1. Distribution of galaxies by redshift (top) and the diagram showing stellar mass in solar units and redshift for galaxies in the sample (bottom). tons of diﬀerent isophotes, we averaged the locations of skeletons and obtained the centre-line of a disc this way, with an example shown in Figure 2. The fol/hyphen.alt lowing measurements of warp properties, in particular their existence in each individual galaxy, warp angle and other parameters, are based on the parameters of centre-line. Formally, centre-line is the set of mul/hyphen.alt tiple measurements of the disc oﬀset from the central plane,∆H, for diﬀerent galactocentric radii r. Central plane is a tangent to the galactic disc in the centre of a galaxy. A galaxy with an S-shaped warp at z = 0. 20 Centre-line ψ e= 5.3∘ Figure 2. An example showing a galaxy, its isophotes and measured centre-line (top) and the illustration of how warp angle is deﬁned (bottom). This galaxy is located at z= 0.20and has a",HST,HST,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_007,7,"z = 0. 20 Centre-line ψ e= 5.3∘ Figure 2. An example showing a galaxy, its isophotes and measured centre-line (top) and the illustration of how warp angle is deﬁned (bottom). This galaxy is located at z= 0.20and has a conspicuous warp which angle is ψe= 5.3◦.Warp angle ψeis deﬁned in a manner similar to [ 3] and schematically shown in Figure 2. However, there is a diﬀerence between the deﬁnition from this work and from [ 3]: we measure ψeat the galactocentric radius corresponding to the outermost extent of the available centre-line measurements instead of R25. In/hyphen.alt suﬃcent image depth, as well as band-shifting eﬀects and cosmological surface brightness dimming make us unable to consistently use R25. In the same time, we are fully aware that ψemeasured in this way inher/hyphen.alt ently depend on the spatial extent of the data, and in Section 3.1we pay attention to rule out any possible observational eﬀects.",HST,HST,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,201,154,True,True,140,294
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_008,8,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects 3. RESULTS For each galaxy in the sample, we measured some of warp properties, most importantly ψe. We adopt a commonly-used distinction between S-shaped and U-shaped warps based on the apparent shape of the warp (see, for example, [ 3]). If a galaxy has warp angle ψe>4◦, we consider it to have a strong warp. The gen/hyphen.alt eral results of the measurements were presented in [ 16] and now are available online at https://github.com/ IVChugunov/Distant_disc_warps . In Figure 3, the observed fraction of strong warps against redshift is presented. For S-shaped warps, the fraction increases with z, from 10–15% at z≈0to nearly 50% at z≈2, whereas the fraction of U-shaped warps being around 10–20%, independently of z. The fraction of strong S-shaped warps at low redshift is consistend with literature (for example, 15% reported in [4] and 11% in [ 19]). If one takes galaxies with ψe>2◦into consideration, this trend will mostly remain the same: the fraction of S-shaped warps in/hyphen.alt creases with redshift, and for",3. RESULTS,3. RESULTS,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_009,9,"is consistend with literature (for example, 15% reported in [4] and 11% in [ 19]). If one takes galaxies with ψe>2◦into consideration, this trend will mostly remain the same: the fraction of S-shaped warps in/hyphen.alt creases with redshift, and for the fraction of U-shaped warps, there will be only a local maximum at z≈1. We observe no signiﬁcant correlation between discs b/a where measured by decomposition, and zneither for the entire sample, nor for S-shaped, U-shaped and un/hyphen.alt warped galaxies individually. We note that not only warp fraction, but also average warp amplitude mod/hyphen.alt erately increases with redshift (see [ 16]).",3. RESULTS,3. RESULTS,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,132,101,True,False,140,241
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_010,10,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects 3.1. VALIDATION After we observe the trend of warp frequency and amplitude to increase towards higher z, we have to ensure that it is not caused by any observational ef/hyphen.alt fects and has physical nature. First, our sample is not complete by mass, and galaxies in our data become more massive at higher z(see Figure 1). Indeed, a connection between the galaxy mass and warp angle exists, but more massive galaxies have smaller warp an/hyphen.alt gles [3,10]. Therefore, incompleteness of our sample could not create the observed trend, and the opposite should be expected. The second possible problem is the varying image quality across the sample, as we noted in Section 2. x–2 Moscow University Physics Bulletin 80(7), x (2025) 0.0 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 z0.00.10.20.30.40.50.60.7Strong warps ( ψ e> 4∘ ) fractionS-shaped U-shaped Figure 3. This diagram shows the fraction of strong S-shaped or U-shaped warps in diﬀerent redshift bins. Red/hyphen.alt shift range of the bin is shown by horizontal error bars, whereas 95%",3.1. VALIDATION,3.1. VALIDATION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_011,11,"z0.00.10.20.30.40.50.60.7Strong warps ( ψ e> 4∘ ) fractionS-shaped U-shaped Figure 3. This diagram shows the fraction of strong S-shaped or U-shaped warps in diﬀerent redshift bins. Red/hyphen.alt shift range of the bin is shown by horizontal error bars, whereas 95% conﬁdence interval is represented by vertical ones. Our measurements of ψedepend on how far to the pe/hyphen.alt riphery we were able to trace the shape of the disc, and for similar galaxies at diﬀerent redshifts this distance will not be the same. One problem here is band-shift/hyphen.alt ing which arise due to that galaxies at diﬀerent red/hyphen.alt shift observed in the same ﬁlter are seen in diﬀerent rest-frame wavelengths. This eﬀect changes the over/hyphen.alt all surface brightness of a galaxy, which is known as K-correction [ 20]. In addition, the dependence of the warp angle on the wavelength itself is also possible [ 21], however in [ 16] we compared warp angles between two ﬁlters for JWST data, and found no systematic oﬀ/hyphen.alt set. Another eﬀect is cosmological dimming, which manifests itself as a surface brightness decrease for all sources",3.1. VALIDATION,3.1. VALIDATION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,False,140,320
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_012,12,"also possible [ 21], however in [ 16] we compared warp angles between two ﬁlters for JWST data, and found no systematic oﬀ/hyphen.alt set. Another eﬀect is cosmological dimming, which manifests itself as a surface brightness decrease for all sources proportional to (1+z)4[22]. As a result, we are unable to catch peripheral parts of the most dis/hyphen.alt tant galaxies as they become too faint. It can possibly lead to various eﬀects: for example, at high redshift we may lose the majority of warps which start at large galactocentric distance z. To check this, we performed an artiﬁcial redshift/hyphen.alt ing of some galaxies in the sample. Essentially, we decreased image quality in a way to get images how the same galaxy would look at larger redshifts. We used the same approach as in [ 23], reproducing wors/hyphen.alt ened spatial resolution and decreased surface bright/hyphen.alt ness due to K-correction and cosmological dimming. Then, we applied our method of warp measurement to artiﬁcial images and we observe examples of diﬀer/hyphen.alt ent behaviour. In some cases, at large zperiphery is lost and one is",3.1. VALIDATION,3.1. VALIDATION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,False,280,460
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_013,13,"decreased surface bright/hyphen.alt ness due to K-correction and cosmological dimming. Then, we applied our method of warp measurement to artiﬁcial images and we observe examples of diﬀer/hyphen.alt ent behaviour. In some cases, at large zperiphery is lost and one is unable to trace warp anymore; in other cases, as in example in Figure 4, isophotes at largezbecome too noisy and false warp can appear. However, we did not include too bad images into our sample which makes false warps less likely to appear. In order to measure quantitatively, how strong im/hyphen.alt age quality inﬂuences our results, we introduce a pa/hyphen.alt rameter of data extent de. This is the radius at which ψewas measured (i.e. the highest galactocentric dis/hyphen.alt tance of traced centre-line), normalised to disc expo/hyphen.alt nential scale h. Naturally, this parameter represents how far from the centre we can trace warps. Then, we z = 0.24 (orig), ψ e = 1.28∘ , d e = 3.94 h z = 0.4, ψ e = 0.58∘ , d e = 3. 79 h z = 0.8, ψ e = 2. 75∘",3.1. VALIDATION,3.1. VALIDATION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_014,14,"trace warps. Then, we z = 0.24 (orig), ψ e = 1.28∘ , d e = 3.94 h z = 0.4, ψ e = 0.58∘ , d e = 3. 79 h z = 0.8, ψ e = 2. 75∘ , d e = 2. 74 h z = 1.2, ψ e = 6.24∘ , d e = 0.69 h Centre-lineArtificial redshifting exam le Figure 4. An example of a galaxy artiﬁcially redshifted to diﬀerentz. Measured warp angles and data extents are also shown. utilise data on ψe,z, stellar mass M∗and data extent defor the HST subsample (because reliable hmeasure/hyphen.alt ments with decomposition are available only for this subsample). We ﬁt ψeas a trilinear function of z,M∗ andde, aiming to discern the dependencies of ψeon these parameters from each other. We show result in Figure 5. Ultimately, we see that ψeindeed increases withz, even if dependencies on M∗anddeare taken into consideration, by more than 2.7 degrees per unit ofz. As expected, the average warp angle decreases with increasing mass and increases with increasing ex/hyphen.alt tent of the data, likely representing",3.1. VALIDATION,3.1. VALIDATION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,560,740
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_015,15,"ψeindeed increases withz, even if dependencies on M∗anddeare taken into consideration, by more than 2.7 degrees per unit ofz. As expected, the average warp angle decreases with increasing mass and increases with increasing ex/hyphen.alt tent of the data, likely representing that if data extent is too small, one risks to miss an existing warp. As a result, we can conclude that the dependence of ψeon zis physical, and observational eﬀects are ruled out.",3.1. VALIDATION,3.1. VALIDATION,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,95,73,False,False,700,773
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_016,16,"Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects 4. CONCLUSIONS In this work, we analysed the sample of more than 1000 distant edge-on galaxies, observed by the HST and JWST. We measured warp angles of their stel/hyphen.alt lar discs and studied how warp frequencies and ampli/hyphen.alt tudes change with redshift. We observe that strong S-shaped warps were much more common in the past, being present in nearly 50% at z≈2, whereas at z≈0 only 10–15% of galaxies have them. For U-shaped warp, there is no such diﬀerence. x–3 Modern Astronomy: Science and Education (to 270th Annivers ary of Moscow University) We paid attention to check how observational eﬀects could inﬂuence our ﬁndings. We examined the possi/hyphen.alt ble dependence of warp angles on wavelength, galaxy mass and image quality and conﬁrm that our trend is independent on these eﬀects. Therefore, we conclude that S-shaped warp frequency increase with redshift most likely has a physical origin. This trend can be connected to the increasing fre/hyphen.alt quency of interactions and mergers between galaxies at higher redshift. This",4. CONCLUSIONS,4. CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_ef28bece42,astrobench_ef28bece42_chunk_017,17,"independent on these eﬀects. Therefore, we conclude that S-shaped warp frequency increase with redshift most likely has a physical origin. This trend can be connected to the increasing fre/hyphen.alt quency of interactions and mergers between galaxies at higher redshift. This is shown by measurements of diﬀerent indicators of interaction rate, for exam/hyphen.alt ple statistics of close pairs [ 24], or merger observa/hyphen.alt tions [ 25]. Therefore, if interactions play a signiﬁcant role in S-shaped warp formation, one should expect higher fraction of warps in the past, when interactions were more common. More broadly, the diﬀerent evolution of S-shaped and U-shaped fractions itself can be evidence of dif/hyphen.alt ferent mechanisms of their formation. For example, tidal interactions are believed to predominantly pro/hyphen.alt duce S-shaped warps, and for U-shaped warps, ram pressure stripping is thought to be a possible way to their formation [ 5].ACKNOWLEDGMENTS Some of the data products presented herein were re/hyphen.alt trieved from the Dawn JWST Archive (DJA). DJA is an initiative of the Cosmic Dawn Center (DAWN), which is funded by the Danish National Research Foun/hyphen.alt dation under",4. CONCLUSIONS,4. CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Galactic disc warps from $z = 2.5$ to modern epoch: ruling out observational effects,"Ilia V. Chugunov, Vladimir P. Reshetnikov, Alexander A. Marchuk",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-01T15:39:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01286v1,2602.01286,distribution_functions,234,180,True,False,140,320
