astrobench_0deff610d0,astrobench_0deff610d0_chunk_025,25,"(−1,0,0)) correspond to the same physical model but appear as distinct points in parameter space. Since we only use the orientation of𝒓, its scale is redundant and introduces a new degeneracy where every combination of(ˆ𝑥,ˆ𝑦,ˆ𝑧) withthesameorientationbutdifferentmagnitudegeneratethesame stream.Tofixthat,weusethemagnitude𝑟(lengthofthevector𝒓)to definethehaloflattening𝑞.Thegoalistotransform𝒓intoauniform distribution on [𝑞 min,𝑞max]. Since𝒓ismultivariatenormalwithzeromeanandunitcovariance, the radius𝑟follows the Maxwell distribution with unit scale. Its probability density function (pdf),𝑓 𝑅, and cumulative distribution function (cdf),𝐹 𝑅, are 𝑓𝑅(𝑟)=√︂ 2 𝜋𝑟2exp −𝑟2 2 , 𝑟≥0,(6) 𝐹𝑅(𝑟)=∫𝑟 0𝑓𝑅(𝑠)d𝑠=erf𝑟√ 2 −√︂ 2 𝜋𝑟exp −𝑟2 2 ,(7)whereerfistheerrorfunctionand𝐹 𝑅isstrictlyincreasingfrom0to 1on[0,∞]. We introduce an intermediate variable 𝑢=𝐹𝑅(𝑟),(8) which by the probability integral transform satisfies 𝑢∼U(0,1),(9) independently of the orientation ˆ𝒏. Wethendefinethephysicalhaloflattening𝑞byanaffinemapping 𝑞=𝑞 min+(𝑞 max−𝑞 min)𝑢,(10) with fixed bounds𝑞 min< 𝑞 max. Since𝑢is uniform on[0,1],𝑞is exactly uniform on[𝑞 min,𝑞max]. In this work, we choose𝑞 min=0.5 and𝑞 max=1.5to obtain a uniform prior𝑞∈[0.5,1.5], centred on spherical (𝑞=1) with equal prior weight for oblate (𝑞 <1) and prolate (𝑞>1). In terms of the radius𝑟, the flattening is therefore 𝑞(𝑟)=𝑞 min+(𝑞 max−𝑞 min)"" erf𝑟√ 2 −√︂ 2 𝜋𝑟exp −𝑟2 2# .(11) This parametrisation yields: (i) anisotropicprioroverhaloorientations ˆ𝒏(restrictedtoˆ𝑛 𝑧≥0), and (ii) anexactlyuniformpriorovertheflattening𝑞on[𝑞 min,𝑞max], while avoiding","MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026)",,,,,Hierarchical bayesian inference: constraining population distribution of dark matter halo shapes via stellar streams,"David Chemaly, Elisabeth Sola, Vasily Belokurov, Sergey Koposov, GyuChul Meyong, HanYuan Zhang, Denis Erkal",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-21T19:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15373