astrobench_0deff610d0,astrobench_0deff610d0_chunk_053,53,"𝜋(𝜃𝑛)𝑍𝑛𝜋(𝜃𝑛|𝛼)d𝜃𝑛 (23) ∝𝜋(𝛼)𝑁Ö 𝑛=1∫ 𝑝(𝜃𝑛|𝑑𝑛)𝜋(𝜃𝑛|𝛼) 𝜋(𝜃𝑛)d𝜃𝑛,(24) wherewehavedroppedtheproductofevidencesÎ 𝑛𝑍𝑛,whichdoes not depend on𝛼. The integral in Eq. (24) is an expectation over the known poste- rior𝑝(𝜃𝑛|𝑑𝑛)and can be approximated with posterior samples. Let {𝜃𝑛𝑖}𝐾𝑛 𝑖=1be𝐾𝑛samples from𝑝(𝜃 𝑛|𝑑𝑛). Then ∫ 𝑝(𝜃𝑛|𝑑𝑛)𝜋(𝜃𝑛|𝛼) 𝜋(𝜃𝑛)d𝜃𝑛≈1 𝐾𝑛𝐾𝑛∑︁ 𝑖=1𝑝(𝜃𝑛𝑖|𝑑𝑛)𝜋(𝜃𝑛𝑖|𝛼) 𝜋(𝜃𝑛𝑖).(25) Thus the hierarchical posterior can be approximated as𝑝(𝛼|{𝑑𝑛})∝𝜋(𝛼)𝑁Ö 𝑛=1"" 1 𝐾𝑛𝐾𝑛∑︁ 𝑖=1𝑝(𝜃𝑛𝑖|𝑑𝑛)𝜋(𝜃𝑛𝑖|𝛼) 𝜋(𝜃𝑛𝑖)# .(26) In the application of interest, the population model acts only on the halo flattening𝑞. We decompose the parameters as 𝜃𝑛=(𝑞𝑛,𝜓𝑛),(27) where𝜓𝑛collects all nuisance parameters (e.g. halo mass, scale radius, progenitor phase–space coordinates). We assume that the individual prior factorises as 𝜋(𝜃𝑛)=𝜋(𝑞𝑛)𝜋(𝜓𝑛),(28) and that the population prior has the form 𝜋(𝜃𝑛|𝛼)=𝜋(𝑞𝑛|𝛼)𝜋(𝜓𝑛),(29) with the same prior𝜋(𝜓 𝑛)on the nuisance parameters. The ratio in Eq. (26) then simplifies to 𝜋(𝜃𝑛𝑖|𝛼) 𝜋(𝜃𝑛𝑖)=𝜋(𝑞𝑛𝑖|𝛼) 𝜋(𝑞𝑛𝑖).(30) If the individual prior on𝑞is uniform on[𝑞 min,𝑞max], 𝜋(𝑞𝑛)=  1 𝑞max−𝑞 min, 𝑞 min≤𝑞𝑛≤𝑞 max, 0,otherwise,(31)","MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026)",,,,,Hierarchical bayesian inference: constraining population distribution of dark matter halo shapes via stellar streams,"David Chemaly, Elisabeth Sola, Vasily Belokurov, Sergey Koposov, GyuChul Meyong, HanYuan Zhang, Denis Erkal",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-21T19:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15373v2,2601.15373,distribution_functions,185,142,False,True,140,282
astrobench_0deff610d0,astrobench_0deff610d0_chunk_054,54,"Hierarchical bayesian inference: constraining population distribution of dark matter halo shapes via stellar streams MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026) 12D. Chemaly et al. then𝜋(𝑞𝑛𝑖)is a constant for all posterior samples used in the reweighting. That constant can be absorbed into the overall nor- malisation of𝑝(𝛼|{𝑑 𝑛}), and we obtain 𝑝(𝛼|{𝑑𝑛})∝𝜋(𝛼)𝑁Ö 𝑛=1"" 1 𝐾𝑛𝐾𝑛∑︁ 𝑖=1𝑝(𝑞𝑛𝑖|𝑑𝑛)𝜋(𝑞𝑛𝑖|𝛼)# .(32) Equation(32)istheworkingexpressionusedtoevaluatethehier- archicallikelihoodforanyproposedhyperparameters𝛼.Thismethod is computationally efficient and scalable but relies on a critical con- dition:theconditionalpriorontheflatening,𝜋(𝑞|𝛼),mustliewithin theactualprior,𝜋(𝑞),usefortheindividualinference.Sincenonew samples are generated in this second-stage inference, the individ- ual posteriors must already sufficiently cover the relevant parameter space. This is the case here since our individual posteriors on flat- tening are usually quite broad and very rarely completely rule out a region of parameter space. As a concrete example, we consider a population model in which thephysicalflattening𝑞isdistributedasaGaussianwithmean𝜇 pop and standard deviation𝜎 pop, truncated to the same support as the individual prior,[𝑞 min,𝑞max]. The hyperparameters are 𝛼=(𝜇 pop,𝜎pop).(33) The (unnormalised) Gaussian density is 𝜙(𝑞|𝜇 pop,𝜎pop)=1√ 2𝜋𝜎 popexp"" −1 2𝑞−𝜇 pop 𝜎pop2# .(34) The truncated density is 𝜋(𝑞|𝛼)=  𝜙(𝑞|𝜇 pop,𝜎pop) Φ𝑞max−𝜇 pop 𝜎pop −Φ𝑞min−𝜇 pop 𝜎pop, 𝑞 min≤𝑞≤𝑞 max, 0,otherwise, (35) whereΦisthestandardnormalcdf.Inpractice,whenevaluatingthe","MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–15 (2026)",,,,,Hierarchical bayesian inference: constraining population distribution of dark matter halo shapes via stellar streams,"David Chemaly, Elisabeth Sola, Vasily Belokurov, Sergey Koposov, GyuChul Meyong, HanYuan Zhang, Denis Erkal",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-21T19:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15373v2,2601.15373,distribution_functions,234,18