astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_007,7,"tends to zero). The density profile𝜌(𝑟)is equivalent to the original density profile 𝜌0(𝑟)for𝑟⩽𝜉𝑟 T. For𝜉𝑟 T< 𝑟 < 𝑟 T, it gradually deviates from the original density profile until it smoothly converges to zero at the truncation radius𝑟 T. We hence have a soft truncation that sets in at 𝑟=𝜉𝑟 Tand that terminates at𝑟=𝑟 T. As the name indicates, the truncation sharpness parameter controls the width or sharpness of the truncation region: small𝜉yields a gradual, extended softening, whereas large𝜉produces a sharper transition. There is no unique functional form for the truncation function S(𝑥). To find a suitable candidate, we consider a continuous proba- bility density function𝜙(𝑥)supported on the closed interval[0,1]. Ifwethenconsiderthecumulativedensityfunction(CDF)Φ(𝑥)and set S(𝑥)=  0for𝑥⩽0, Φ(𝑥)for0<𝑥<1, 1for𝑥⩾1,(5) weobtainamonotonicallyincreasingfunctionthatsatisfiesthecondi- tion(4).Toensurethatthetruncationfunctionissufficientlysmooth, theprobabilitydensityfunctionshouldbechosensuchthatitslowest- order derivatives at𝑥=0and𝑥=1vanish. Aconvenientprobabilitydistributionisthelogit–normaldistribu- tion,widelyusedinstatisticsandneuralnetworks(Aitchison&Shen 0.0 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 /u1D45F0.00.20.40.60.81.0S(/u1D466)/u1D709=0.5 /u1D709=0.6 /u1D709=0.7 /u1D709=0.8 /u1D709=0.9 /u1D709=1 (sharp truncation)Figure1.IllustrationofthetruncationfunctionS(𝑦).Thetruncationradius is fixed at𝑟 T=2, whereas the truncation sharpness takes different values ranging from 0.5 to 1. 1980; Lenk 1988). For the standard parameters𝜇=0and𝜎=1, it is characterised by the probability density 𝜙(𝑥)=1√ 2𝜋1 𝑥(1−𝑥)e−1 2(logit𝑥)2,(6) withlogitthe inverse of the standard logistic function,",2.1 Mathematical formulation,2.1 Mathematical formulation,,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.083