astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_048,48,"Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency TRUNCATION FUNCTION In Sect. 2.1 we introduced a smooth truncation functionS(𝑥)based on the cumulative distribution function of a logit–normal distribu- tion. This choice ensures that the density and all of its derivatives vanishatthetruncationradius𝑟 T,therebypreventingthephase-space singularities that arise for sharply truncated models. The specific form ofS(𝑥)is not unique, however: any sufficiently smooth func- tion that transitions monotonically from unity to zero may be used. Ideally,S(𝑥)is infinitely differentiable (𝐶∞); it should at minimum be twice differentiable (𝐶2) for the Eddington inversion to remain well behaved. Onealternativewithspecificcontroloversmoothnessistheuseof compactly supported polynomials. Polynomial transition functions of the so-called smoothstep family are commonly used in computer graphics and machine learning (Perlin 1985; Ebert et al. 2003). The general form for the𝑛th smoothstep function is given by S𝑛(𝑥)=  0for𝑥⩽0, 𝑥𝑛+1𝑛∑︁ 𝑘=0 𝑛+𝑘 𝑘! 2𝑛+1 𝑛−𝑘! (−𝑥)𝑘for0⩽𝑥⩽1, 1for𝑥⩾1,",TRUNCATION FUNCTION,TRUNCATION FUNCTION,,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,190,146,False,True,0,146
astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_049,49,"Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency (A1) Higher values of𝑛correspond to smoother polynomials at the end points. Once𝑛⩾2, the first and second derivatives are zero at the edges of the interval. For our purposes, one would typically adopt 𝑛⩾2so that𝜌(𝑟)is at least𝐶2at the truncation radius. Wendland functions form a family of compactly supported radial basisfunctionswithminimalpolynomialdegreeforaprescribedlevel ofsmoothness(Wendland1995).Theyhavebecomestandardtoolsin scattered-data approximation and meshless numerical methods (e.g. Schaback 2007; Fasshauer & Schaback 2012), and are widely used as smoothing kernels in smoothed particle hydrodynamics (Dehnen &Aly2012;Wissingetal.2025).InPaperIVwealreadyemployed Wendland functions as the basis for dynamical models with a finite extent, and their mathematical properties make them equally suit- able as truncation functions. In three dimensions, each Wendland function, denoted as𝜙 𝑘+2,𝑘(𝑢), is a monotonically decreasing𝐶2𝑘 polynomialin𝑢oforder3𝑘+2with𝜙 𝑘+2,𝑘(0)=1and𝜙 𝑘+2,𝑘(1)=0. For any value of𝑘, the function S𝑘(𝑥)=  0for𝑥⩽0, 1−𝜙𝑘+2,𝑘(𝑥)for0⩽𝑥⩽1, 1for𝑥⩾1,(A2) satisfies the required conditions for the truncation function. Wendland-based tapers thus offer an attractive alternative to the logit–normal truncation function: they provide exact compact sup- port, explicit polynomial structure, and full control over the degree of differentiability. This",(A1),(A1),,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_050,50,"1−𝜙𝑘+2,𝑘(𝑥)for0⩽𝑥⩽1, 1for𝑥⩾1,(A2) satisfies the required conditions for the truncation function. Wendland-based tapers thus offer an attractive alternative to the logit–normal truncation function: they provide exact compact sup- port, explicit polynomial structure, and full control over the degree of differentiability. This paper has been typeset from a T EX/LATEX file prepared by the author.",(A1),(A1),,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,69,53,False,False,140,193
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_000,0,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream front_matter Draft version April 1, 2026 Typeset using L ATEX twocolumn style in AAST eX7.0.1 Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream Zi-yi Zhou ,1Long W ang ,1, 2Tereza Jerabkov a,3and Zhenghao He1 1School of Physics and Astronomy, Sun Y at-sen University, Daxue Road, 519082 Zhuhai, China 2CSST Science Center for the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macau Greater Bay Area, 519082 Zhuhai, China 3Department of Theoretical Physics and Astrophysics, F aculty of Science, Masaryk University, Kotlářská 2, Brno 611 37, Czech Republic",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,128,98,False,False,0,98
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_001,1,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream ABSTRACT W e present a suite of direct N-body simulations of the Hyades open cluster and its tidal stream in a Milky W ay potential that includes a rotating bar and spiral arms. Using the high-resolution code PETAR and an AGAMA -based multi-component Galactic model, we vary the bar and spiral pattern speeds ( Ωb,Ωs) on a discrete grid and quantify the resulting changes in stream orientation, length, and internal density structure. W e compare the simulations to Gaia EDR3 using the convergent point (CP) and compact convergent point (CCP) methods, followed by an adaptive three-dimensional nearest- neighbor matching in Cartesian space (x, y, z ). The Gaia candidate members exhibit a pronounced longitudinal density peak at Yrot≈0.1 kpc in a stream-aligned coordinate system. Models with Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1and Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1best reproduce this feature, while faster-bar models fail to match the observed density structure. These models are consistent with recent constraints favoring a relatively slow Galactic bar, and they illustrate how nearby open-cluster streams can",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_002,2,"22.5 km s−1kpc−1and Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1best reproduce this feature, while faster-bar models fail to match the observed density structure. These models are consistent with recent constraints favoring a relatively slow Galactic bar, and they illustrate how nearby open-cluster streams can provide an independent, local constraint on non-axisymmetric Galactic dynamics. Keywords: Galaxy: open clusters and associations: individual: Hyades – Galaxy: kinematics and dynamics – Galaxy: structure – Galaxy: disk – Galaxy: evolution – Methods: numerical",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,97,74,False,False,140,214
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_003,3,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 1. INTRODUCTION Constraining the Milky W ay gravitational poten- tial is central to connecting stellar kinematics to the Galaxy’s mass distribution and secular evolution ( J. Bland-Hawthorn & O. Gerhard 2016 ). Even in the solar neighborhood, degeneracies persist between the halo shape and the disk and bulge/bar contributions. While the dark matter halo is often approximated by a spherical NFW profile ( J. F. Navarro et al. 1997 ), its precise geometry remains a subject of ongoing de- bate. Many recent analyses favor a flattened, oblate configuration ( q < 1) to better match kinematic con- straints ( Y. Huang et al. 2024 ), although alternative models suggesting a prolate shape have also been pro- posed ( L. Zhu et al. 2026 ). In the disk, additional un- certainty arises from non-axisymmetric structure: the Galactic bar pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 km s−1kpc−1) and “slow” ( ∼40 km s−1kpc−1) so- lutions supported by different dynamical tracers ( W. Dehnen 1999 ;J. L. Sanders et",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_004,4,"additional un- certainty arises from non-axisymmetric structure: the Galactic bar pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 km s−1kpc−1) and “slow” ( ∼40 km s−1kpc−1) so- lutions supported by different dynamical tracers ( W. Dehnen 1999 ;J. L. Sanders et al. 2019 ;J. P . Clarke Corresponding author: Long W ang wanglong8@sysu.edu.cnet al. 2019a ;S. Lucchini et al. 2024 ;J. A. S. Hunt & E. V asiliev 2025 ). The morphology and dynamical nature of the spiral arms are likewise uncertain ( R. J. J. Grand et al. 2012 ;G. H. Hunter et al. 2024 ;Y. R. Khalil et al. 2025 ). Stellar tidal streams—formed by the gradual stripping of star clusters—provide phase-coherent tracers of the Galactic potential ( S. E. Koposov et al. 2010 ;S. L. J. Gibbons et al. 2014 ). Globular-cluster streams are typically narrow and prominent (e.g., M. Odenkirchen et al. 2001 ), but they often probe the potential at larger Galactocentric distances. In contrast, open- cluster streams reside in the disk and are therefore par- ticularly sensitive to perturbations from the bar, spi- ral arms,",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_005,5,"(e.g., M. Odenkirchen et al. 2001 ), but they often probe the potential at larger Galactocentric distances. In contrast, open- cluster streams reside in the disk and are therefore par- ticularly sensitive to perturbations from the bar, spi- ral arms, and encounters with giant molecular clouds (M. Gieles et al. 2006 ;K. Hattori et al. 2016 ;N. C. Amorisco et al. 2016 ). Their low surface density and strong field-star contamination make them challenging to identify , but the astrometric precision of Gaia now enables systematic searches for these nearby , diffuse structures. The Hyades is an exceptional laboratory be- cause its proximity permits a fine-grained comparison between observed tidal tails and dynamical models ( S. Röser et al. 2011 ;Gaia Collaboration et al. 2018 ).arXiv:2603.29360v1 [astro-ph.GA] 31 Mar 2026 2 Observational efforts initially focused on the Hyades core, using proper-motion catalogs and Hipparcos as- trometry to characterize membership within the tidal radius ( M. A. C. Perryman et al. 1998 ). S. Röser et al. (2011 ) extended the search using the PPMXL catalog, providing early evidence that the",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_006,6,"proper-motion catalogs and Hipparcos as- trometry to characterize membership within the tidal radius ( M. A. C. Perryman et al. 1998 ). S. Röser et al. (2011 ) extended the search using the PPMXL catalog, providing early evidence that the cluster extends beyond its classical tidal boundary . The extended tails became accessible with Gaia (Gaia Collaboration et al. 2016 , 2018 ;S. Meingast et al. 2019 ). In particular, S. Röser et al. (2019 ) mapped tidal-tail members in Gaia DR2 using the convergent point (CP) method, establishing an empirical reference for the stream orientation near the cluster. Numerical simulations are essential for connecting the present-day stream morphology to the underlying Galac- tic dynamics. Early N-body models primarily focused on reproducing the cluster’s mass-loss rate and the evo- lution of its core properties within a static, axisymmet- ric Galactic potential ( Y. O. Chumak et al. 2005 ;A. Ernst et al. 2011 ). The Gaia detection of asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com- plex than can be captured by an axisymmetric model ( T. Jerabkova",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_007,7,"potential ( Y. O. Chumak et al. 2005 ;A. Ernst et al. 2011 ). The Gaia detection of asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com- plex than can be captured by an axisymmetric model ( T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ). Recent work has therefore begun to incorporate non-axisymmetric structure; for example, G. F. Thomas et al. (2023 ) explored the influence of a rotating bar and a flattened halo. However, the joint impact of the bar and spiral arms—and, in particular, how their pattern speeds map into observable stream diagnostics—remains insuﬀiciently explored. In this paper we perform a systematic survey of bar and spiral pattern speeds using high-resolution PETAR N-body simulations in a multi-component AGAMA Milky W ay potential. W e confront the models with Gaia EDR3 using CP/CCP kinematic selection and an adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching procedure. W e emphasize a single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution in a stream-aligned coordinate frame, including a prominent density peak at Yrot≈0.1 kpc . W e",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_008,8,"matching procedure. W e emphasize a single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution in a stream-aligned coordinate frame, including a prominent density peak at Yrot≈0.1 kpc . W e find that models with Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1and Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1 best reproduce the observed density structure, support- ing a relatively slow Galactic bar. This paper is organized as follows. Section 2.1 de- scribes the Galactic potential model and the N-body simulation setup. Section 2.3 presents the simulated stream morphologies and kinematics across the explored (Ωb,Ωs)parameter space. In Section 3we compare the simulations to observations, using the Röser CP-selected sample and Gaia EDR3 to constrain the pattern speeds via stream orientation and density structure. W e discuss limitations and future observational tests in Section 4.2. N-BODY SIMULA TIONS 2.1. Galactic potential The Galactic gravitational potential was implemented using the AGAMA framework ( E. V asiliev 2019 ). Our po- tential is based on the multi-component model described by G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 )4, which includes contri- butions from",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,700,880
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_009,9,"potential The Galactic gravitational potential was implemented using the AGAMA framework ( E. V asiliev 2019 ). Our po- tential is based on the multi-component model described by G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 )4, which includes contri- butions from the supermassive black hole Sgr A∗, the nuclear star cluster, the nuclear stellar disk, the Galac- tic bar, the Galactic disk (comprising both axisymmetric and spiral arm components), and the dark matter halo. T o account for the flattened dark matter distribution observed in recent studies ( Y. Huang et al. 2024 ;K. Mal- han & R. A. Ibata 2019 ;R. Ibata et al. 2024 ), we mod- ified the original dark matter halo by replacing it with a triaxial spheroidal component. F ollowing Y. Huang et al. (2016 ), this component is parameterized as a gen- eralized NFW profile ( J. F. Navarro et al. 1997 ) with the following configuration: • a scale radius rs= 14.39 kpc ; • a vertical-to-radial axis ratio (flattening) q= 0.82; • a scale density ρ= 1.357×107M⊙kpc−3; • a profile shape defined by",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,840,1020
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_010,10,"( J. F. Navarro et al. 1997 ) with the following configuration: • a scale radius rs= 14.39 kpc ; • a vertical-to-radial axis ratio (flattening) q= 0.82; • a scale density ρ= 1.357×107M⊙kpc−3; • a profile shape defined by an inner slope γ= 1 , an outer slope β= 3 , and a transition sharpness α= 1 . These parameters were specifically calibrated to en- sure that the resulting circular velocity curve of our modified potential remains highly consistent with the original model described by G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 ) as shown in Fig. 1. The dynamical impact of various resonance conditions is investigated through three distinct sets of potential configurations, generated by varying ΩbandΩs. In ad- dition to these non-axisymmetric setups, we adopted the MWPotential2014 model ( J. Bovy 2015 ;L. W ang & T. Jerabkova 2021 ;T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ) via galpy as an axisymmetric baseline, representing a potential de- void of both bar and spiral arm perturbations. These models are summarized in T able 1, providing a frame- work to systematically isolate",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,980,1160
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_011,11,"2021 ;T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ) via galpy as an axisymmetric baseline, representing a potential de- void of both bar and spiral arm perturbations. These models are summarized in T able 1, providing a frame- work to systematically isolate the influence of each non- axisymmetric component. The simulation reference frame is anchored by a Sun- Galactic center distance of R0= 8.179 kpc and a so- lar circular velocity of Vc(R0) = 229 km s−1(GRA V- ITY Collaboration et al. 2022 ;G. H. Hunter et al. 4The original potential configuration is available at https://github.com/GalacticDynamics-Oxford/Agama/blob/ master/py/example_mw_potential_hunter24.py 3 T able 1. Summary of Galactic potential configurations. Set Ωb Ωs Set 1 (Bar only) Ωgrid — Set 2 (Bar + Fixed arms) Ωgrid 22.5 Set 3 (Fixed bar + Arms) 37.5 Ωgrid Baseline (Axisymmetric) MWPotential2014 Notes. All pattern speeds are in units of km s−1kpc−1. The symbol Ωgrid denotes the set of discrete pattern speeds: 10, 20, 25, 29, 35, 37.5, 40, 42.5, 45, 50, 55. 2024 ), ensuring consistency with local kinematic con- straints. F urthermore, the solar peculiar motion is taken",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_012,12,"km s−1kpc−1. The symbol Ωgrid denotes the set of discrete pattern speeds: 10, 20, 25, 29, 35, 37.5, 40, 42.5, 45, 50, 55. 2024 ), ensuring consistency with local kinematic con- straints. F urthermore, the solar peculiar motion is taken from R. Schönrich et al. (2010 ), with (U⊙, V⊙, W⊙) = (11.1,12.24,7.25) km s−1. 0.0 2.5 5.0 7.5 10.0 12.5 15.0 17.5 20.0 R [kpc]140160180200220Circular velocity [km/s] Hunter 24 without spiral arms Hunter 24 with spiral arms This work without spiral arms This work With spiral arms Figure 1. Circular-velocity curves: G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 ) without (blue) and with (red) spiral arms; potential model used in this study without (green) and with (cyan) spiral arms. Purple dot line represents R = 8.179kpc, which is close to the Sun’s position used in this work 2.2. Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performance N- body code PETAR (L. W ang et al. 2020 ) to simulate the dynamical evolution of the Hyades ( L. W ang & T. Jer- abkova 2021 ). PETAR has the ability",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1260,1440
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_013,13,"In this work, we employed the high-performance N- body code PETAR (L. W ang et al. 2020 ) to simulate the dynamical evolution of the Hyades ( L. W ang & T. Jer- abkova 2021 ). PETAR has the ability to eﬀiciently handle both the internal collisional dynamics of the star cluster and the external Galactic tidal field. Specifically , PETAR employs a hybrid algorithm that effectively accounts for gravitational interactions across all spatial scales by combining dedicated methods for short-, middle-, and long-range forces using sdar and fdps (L. W ang 2020 ; S. J. Aarseth 2003 ;J. Barnes & P . Hut 1986 ;M. Iwa- sawa et al. 2016 ,2020 ;D. Namekata et al. 2018 ). This multi-scale treatment ensures the high numerical preci- sion and computational eﬀiciency required to simulateT able 2. Present-day parameters of the Hyades (in the ICRS frame) Parameter V alue RA [deg] 67.985 Dec [deg] 17.012 Distance [pc] 47.501 pm(RA, cos(Dec)) [mas/yr] 101.005 pm(Dec) [mas/yr] −28.490 Radial velocity [km/s] 39.96 Age [Myr] 648 the long-term evolution and mass loss of stellar systems over",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1400,1580
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_014,14,"(in the ICRS frame) Parameter V alue RA [deg] 67.985 Dec [deg] 17.012 Distance [pc] 47.501 pm(RA, cos(Dec)) [mas/yr] 101.005 pm(Dec) [mas/yr] −28.490 Radial velocity [km/s] 39.96 Age [Myr] 648 the long-term evolution and mass loss of stellar systems over several hundred megayears. The present-day parameters adopted for the Hyades cluster in our simulations were partially based on L. W ang & T. Jerabkova (2021 ), as shown in T able 2. Then we used the Astropy SkyCoord package to trans- form the present-day parameters into Galactocentric po- sitions and velocities ( Astropy Collaboration et al. 2013 , 2018 ,2022 ). T o simulate the dynamical evolution of the Hyades stream, we first determined the initial phase-space coor- dinates of the cluster 648 Myr ago ( L. W ang & T. Jer- abkova 2021 ). F or each gravitational potential model, we performed a backward integration of a test particle starting from the cluster’s current Galactocentric posi- tion. However, due to internal dynamical interactions and centroid shifts inherent in N-body systems, the fi- nal centroid of the simulated cluster typically",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1540,1720
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_015,15,"model, we performed a backward integration of a test particle starting from the cluster’s current Galactocentric posi- tion. However, due to internal dynamical interactions and centroid shifts inherent in N-body systems, the fi- nal centroid of the simulated cluster typically deviates from the observed present-day position, even if a test particle returns to its origin. T o account for this dis- crepancy , we used the initial conditions derived from the test-particle integration as a first guess for a Nelder- Mead optimization ( J. A. Nelder & R. Mead 1965 ). W e iteratively refined these initial coordinates until the sim- ulated cluster’s centroid at the end of the 648 Myr evo- lution satisfied the following convergence criteria: the positional differences in xandywere less than 100 pc , thezdifference was less than 20 pc , the velocity mag- nitude deviation was below 10 km s−1, and the angular misalignment between the simulated and observed ve- locity vectors was within 5◦. W e employed the McLuster package ( A. H. W. Küp- per et al. 2011 ;L. W ang et al.",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1680,1860
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_016,16,"was below 10 km s−1, and the angular misalignment between the simulated and observed ve- locity vectors was within 5◦. W e employed the McLuster package ( A. H. W. Küp- per et al. 2011 ;L. W ang et al. 2019 ) to construct the ini- tial model of the Hyades cluster. The cluster was param- eterized as a Plummer sphere with an initial total mass of800M⊙and an initial half-mass radius of rh= 1 pc . Individual stellar masses were sampled from a P . Kroupa (2001 ) initial mass function (IMF). T o account for stellar evolution, we use the sse and bse modules ( J. R. Hurley et al. 2000 ,2002 ) updated by S. Banerjee et al. (2020 ), 4 assuming a solar metallicity of Z= 0.02 throughout the simulation. 2.3. Simulation Results 2.3.1. Stream Morphology W e first summarize the qualitative dependence of the Hyades stream morphology on (Ωb,Ωs), and we then use the orientation- and density-based observational com- parisons in Section 3to identify the subset of models consistent with Gaia . W e first explored",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1820,2000
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_017,17,"e first summarize the qualitative dependence of the Hyades stream morphology on (Ωb,Ωs), and we then use the orientation- and density-based observational com- parisons in Section 3to identify the subset of models consistent with Gaia . W e first explored the parameter space of Ωbin the absence of spiral arms. Throughout these simulations, the bar length was kept fixed to ensure that the observed morphological changes resulted solely from the variation in rotation speed. T wo critical Ωbassociated with orbital resonances are of particular interest: the corotation resonance (CR) and the outer Lindblad resonance (OLR). The CR occurs at the radius where Ωbmatches the local circular orbital frequency , while the OLR repre- sents a higher-frequency resonance where the bar po- tential couples with the radial epicyclic motion of the stars. Assuming the guiding radius of the Hyades is ap- proximately solar, Ωbrequired for CR is estimated as ΩCR=Vc/RCR≃29 km s−1kpc−1. F urthermore, the frequency associated with the OLR can be estimated following J. Binney & S. T remaine (2008 ); K. Hattori et al. (2016 ): ΩOLR = Ω",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1960,2140
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_018,18,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 2ΩR, (1) where Ωϕand ΩR are the azimuthal and radial fre- quencies, respectively . By setting N= 1 , we obtain an OLR pattern speed of ΩOLR≃50 km s−1kpc−1. The Galactocentric positions of the simulated Hyades stream are presented in Fig. 2. Upon completion of each simulation, the simulated streams were spatially aligned with the observed counterpart by applying a positional shift. The velocity direction of the stream’s centroid was then adopted as the x-axis, corresponding to the direc- tion indicated by the blue arrow in the accompanying figure. With the centroid position held fixed, the posi- tions and velocities of all stream stars were rotated into this new coordinate frame. In the low-speed regime, where Ωbis below ΩCR , the angle between the stellar stream and its centroid veloc- ity vector measured in the inertial Galactocentric frame (hereafter referred to as the misalignment angle) re- mains essentially zero and exhibits minimal sensitivity to increases in Ωb. However, as Ωbapproaches the value at the corotation radius, the","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_019,19,"its centroid veloc- ity vector measured in the inertial Galactocentric frame (hereafter referred to as the misalignment angle) re- mains essentially zero and exhibits minimal sensitivity to increases in Ωb. However, as Ωbapproaches the value at the corotation radius, the stream length undergoes a modest reduction due to corotation effects.AsΩbcontinues to increase beyond the corotation ra- dius, the misalignment angle gradually becomes more significant. In contrast, once Ωbexceeds ΩOLR , the mis- alignment angle converges back toward the configura- tion observed at low Ωb, maintaining values near zero with little variation. Notably , the reduction in stream length near the OLR is significantly more pronounced than the modest decrease observed at the corotation ra- dius. In all simulations, the final positions of the stream’s centroid and the direction of its centroid velocity align well with the observed properties of the Hyades stream. Consequently , the variations in both the misalignment angle and the length of the stream reflect how the bar’s rotating potential reshapes the spatial orientation and extent of the tidal tails. Our findings are consistent with the simulation","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_020,20,"Hyades stream. Consequently , the variations in both the misalignment angle and the length of the stream reflect how the bar’s rotating potential reshapes the spatial orientation and extent of the tidal tails. Our findings are consistent with the simulation results of G. F. Thomas et al. (2023 ). Subsequently , we included the spiral arms component in the potential, fixing Ωsat22.5 km s−1kpc−1, and again varied Ωb. W e found that the resulting config- urations were remarkably similar to those obtained in the bar-only simulations (Fig. 2), indicating that the in- fluence of the spiral arms on the Hyades is secondary to that of the bar. This result is expected, as at the Galac- tocentric distance of the Hyades, the magnitude of the gravitational potential contributed by the spiral arms is only approximately 1% of that contributed by the bar (G. H. Hunter et al. 2024 ). However, we note that for bar pattern speeds of 20 and25 km s−1kpc−1, the inclusion of spiral arms leads to a more discernible change in the stream orientation compared to the bar-only","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_021,21,"bar (G. H. Hunter et al. 2024 ). However, we note that for bar pattern speeds of 20 and25 km s−1kpc−1, the inclusion of spiral arms leads to a more discernible change in the stream orientation compared to the bar-only case. This phenomenon likely arises from a resonant interaction between the bar and the spiral arms, as their respective pattern speeds in these specific models are close to each other, enhancing the total non-axisymmetric perturbation experienced by the cluster. Nevertheless, as we show in Section 3, vary- ingΩscan still modulate the fine-grained density struc- ture along the stream even when the global orientation is largely bar-driven. F urthermore, we compared these results with the base- line model (see T able 1). In this axisymmetric case, the misalignment angle remains relatively small, further confirming that the significant deviations observed in our other simulation sets are primarily driven by the non-axisymmetric bar and spiral arms potential. W e then fixed Ωbat37.5 km s−1kpc−1, a value con- sistent with recent observational estimates, and varied Ωs(G. H. Hunter et al. 2024 ). The results","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
