astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_026,26,"4. Time evolution of the misalignment angle (red solid lines) and the local orbital curvature (blue solid lines) for the spatially matched models. The x-axis represents the time since the cluster formation in Myr. The left y-axis indicates the misalignment angle in degrees, and the right y-axis shows the orbital curvature in units of kpc−1. Panel (a) shows the impact ofΩb(with and without spiral arms), and Panel (b) focuses on the impact of Ωsat a constant Ωb. T o investigate this phenomenon more thoroughly , for each spatially matched model (see Section 3for defini- tion), we computed the time evolution of the misalign- ment angle after the formation of the stellar stream, as shown in Fig. 4. Clearly , different Ωband Ωshave a substantial impact on the evolution of the misalignment angle. Moreover, certain modulation patterns can be distinctly observed in this evolution, and the modula- tion period closely matches the orbital period of Hyades around the Galactic center. Accordingly , the evolution of curvature over time is also plotted with blue curves in Fig. 4. Here, the “curvature” refers","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1120,1300
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_027,27,"in this evolution, and the modula- tion period closely matches the orbital period of Hyades around the Galactic center. Accordingly , the evolution of curvature over time is also plotted with blue curves in Fig. 4. Here, the “curvature” refers to the geometric curva- ture of the three-dimensional centroid trajectory of the stellar stream as it evolves over time. T o compute this numerically from our simulation data, we treat the cen-troid position as a parametric curve r(t)in 3D space. W e then estimate the first and second-order derivatives, r′(t)and r′′(t), using finite difference schemes. The cur- vature κat each epoch iis determined according to the standard differential geometry definition: κi=∥r′ i×r′′ i∥ ∥r′ i∥3+ϵ, (2) where ϵis a small constant ( 10−16) included to ensure numerical stability . In some models (e.g., Ωb= 45 km s−1kpc−1and 42.5 km s−1kpc−1), a clear modulation relationship be- tween the misalignment angle and curvature is evident, whereas in other models this relationship is less pro- nounced. As a result, the observed correlation may suggest a loose link between the evolution of the mis-","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1260,1440
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_028,28,"a clear modulation relationship be- tween the misalignment angle and curvature is evident, whereas in other models this relationship is less pro- nounced. As a result, the observed correlation may suggest a loose link between the evolution of the mis- alignment angle and the orbital curvature. This would 7 imply that the time-varying tidal forces experienced by the cluster at specific orbital positions could act as one of the contributing factors—alongside other complex or- bital dynamics—in shaping the stream’s orientation. 2.3.3. Proper motions In addition to the spatial distribution, the kinematic properties of the various models merit examination. W e calculated the proper motion distributions across all simulations. W e found that while different models ex- hibit morphological variations in space, the kinematics of the stream core remain broadly similar. Discrepan- cies primarily emerge in the wings of the proper motion distributions, which correspond to the stars located in the distal tidal tails. T o clearly illustrate these kinematic behaviors, Fig- ure 5presents the 2D proper motion diagrams for a rep- resentative subset of our models, arranged in pairwise comparisons.","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1400,1580
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_029,29,"motion distributions, which correspond to the stars located in the distal tidal tails. T o clearly illustrate these kinematic behaviors, Fig- ure 5presents the 2D proper motion diagrams for a rep- resentative subset of our models, arranged in pairwise comparisons. The first and fourth rows demonstrate the impact of spiral arms. These panels represent the dynamical behavior seen in the majority of our models (not plotted here), where the presence or varying Ωsin- troduce only minor perturbations to the overall proper motion distribution. This implies that, under most stan- dard configurations ( Ωb<ΩOLR ), the proper motion of the stream is not highly sensitive to the bar and spiral arms. In contrast, the second and third rows display the extreme cases with fast bars ( Ωb= 50 and 55 km s−1kpc−1, respectively). Under these conditions, the proper motion distributions of the stream tails de- viate significantly from the typical models. The dis- tinct behavior at Ωb= 50 km s−1kpc−1is physically expected. As previously shown in Fig. 2, this pattern speed coincides with ΩOLR which strongly truncates the stream’s spatial extent.","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1540,1720
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_030,30,"the stream tails de- viate significantly from the typical models. The dis- tinct behavior at Ωb= 50 km s−1kpc−1is physically expected. As previously shown in Fig. 2, this pattern speed coincides with ΩOLR which strongly truncates the stream’s spatial extent. F urthermore, as Ωbincreases beyond ΩOLR , the proper motion of the distal tail pro- gressively diverges from the trajectory established by slower bars. Nevertheless, despite these dramatic kinematic vari- ations in the tidal tails, the dense core of the cluster consistently occupies a common locus in the proper mo- tion space across all models. This sensitivity pattern confirms that the adopted Galactic potential primarily governs the velocity-space evolution of the outer stream regions, while the core kinematics remain highly robust.","2ΩR, (1)","2ΩR, (1)",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,158,121,False,True,1680,1801
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_031,31,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS 3.1. Spatial Distribution W e constrain (Ωb,Ωs)in two steps. First, we re- quire the simulated stream orientation near the clus- 100 75 50 25 0255075 (mas yr1) b=37.5, No spiral arms b=37.5,s=22.5 100 75 50 25 0255075 (mas yr1) b=37.5,s=10 b=37.5,s=37.5 100 75 50 25 0255075 (mas yr1) b=50, No spiral arms b=50,s=22.5 40 0 40 80 120 160 200 * (mas yr1) 100 75 50 25 0255075 (mas yr1) b=55, No spiral arms 40 0 40 80 120 160 200 * (mas yr1) b=55,s=22.5 Figure 5. Proper motion distributions for a repre- sentative selection of models. The first three rows contrast the kinematics with and without spiral arms (Ωs= 22 .5 km s−1kpc−1) at bar pattern speeds of Ωb= 37 .5, 50, and 55 km s−1kpc−1, respectively . The bottom row focuses on the impact of different spiral arm speeds (Ωs= 10 ,37.5 km s−1kpc−1) for a constant bar speed of Ωb= 37 .5 km s−1kpc−1. ter to match the empirical direction",BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_032,32,"and 55 km s−1kpc−1, respectively . The bottom row focuses on the impact of different spiral arm speeds (Ωs= 10 ,37.5 km s−1kpc−1) for a constant bar speed of Ωb= 37 .5 km s−1kpc−1. ter to match the empirical direction traced by the Röser CP-selected sample. Second, for the orientation-selected models we use Gaia EDR3 to compare the internal den- sity structure along the stream, focusing on the location and prominence of the Yrot≈0.1 kpc overdensity that drives our final pattern-speed constraint. Identifying member stars in the distant tidal tails of the Hyades stream remains challenging because of strong field-star contamination and the low surface density of the stream ( S. Röser et al. 2011 ). S. Röser et al. (2019 ) identified a relatively clean set of candidates near the cluster using the convergent point (CP) method applied to Gaia DR2 (hereafter the Röser dataset), which does 8 not assume a specific N-body model or Galactic poten- tial model ( F. van Leeuwen 2009 ). Although the Röser dataset is concentrated near the cluster, one tail seg- ment is",BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_033,33,"Gaia DR2 (hereafter the Röser dataset), which does 8 not assume a specific N-body model or Galactic poten- tial model ( F. van Leeuwen 2009 ). Although the Röser dataset is concentrated near the cluster, one tail seg- ment is visible in the Galactocentric XY plane, allowing us to infer the local stream direction and reject simula- tion models whose projected orientation is inconsistent with the data. W e employed Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to determine the principal orientation of the Hyades stream in both the Röser dataset and each simulated model, characterizing the stream direction with a me- dian line ( I. T. Jolliffe & J. Cadima 2016 ). T o mit- igate the impact of spatial scatter in the XY plane and ensure a robust directional fit, we restricted the PCA calculation to stars within the X-coordinate range of[−8.3,−8.15] kpc. Fig. 6 illustrates a compari- son between the Röser dataset and a representative simulation with Ωb= 37 .5 km s−1kpc−1and Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1. 0.4 0.2 0.0 0.2 0.4 Y (kpc)8.40 8.35 8.30 8.25 8.20 8.15 8.10 8.05",BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
