astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_046,46,"in Fig. 11, the density distributions vary significantly across the different potential models. The locations of predicted over-densities differ between models, and in some cases, these features are even entirely absent. Theoretically , tidal streams originating from clusters on circular orbits within a static, axisymmetric potential are expected to exhibit periodic density enhancements, often referred to as epicyclic over-densities ( A. H. W. Küpper et al. 2010 , 2008 ). The suppression or ”smearing out” of these struc- tures in certain models highlights the impact of non- axisymmetric and time-dependent components, such as the Galactic bar and spiral arms, which perturb the stream’s phase-space structure. Notably , we find that nearly all the final members associated with different spatially matched models con- sistently exhibit a prominent density peak near Yrot≈0.1 kpc . Assuming this observed over-density accu- rately reflects the intrinsic physical structure of the Hyades stream, it suggests that potential models ca- pable of reproducing this feature may provide a more realistic representation of the Galactic potential. This morphological signature is best recovered by models with Ωs= 22 .5",BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,BAR AND SPIRAL ARMS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,2100,2280
