astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_051,51,"( R. S. de Jong et al. 2019 ) will be crucial for confirming the chem- ical and kinematic signatures of the distant tidal tail members. Integrating these 6D phase-space data with chemical tagging will allow for a more robust test of the Galactic potential models presented in this work. Alter- natively , the observed asymmetry may have a physical origin. A significant past collision or a series of close encounters with GMCs could have led to the structural disruption or erasure of one side of the stream ( M. Gieles et al. 2006 ;T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ). Beyond these observational constraints and potential stochastic events, the results of this study are also sub- ject to several inherent simplifications and uncertainties in our numerical modeling approach. In this study , we varied Ωbwhile keeping its length and strength constant. In reality , the bar’s length is often correlated with Ωb, with slower bars typically being longer ( E. Athanassoula 2003 ). F urthermore, the Galactic bar and spiral arms 12 0.10 0.05 0.000.050.10Yrot [kpc]b=37.5 km/s, s=10 km/s 0.000.020.040.060.080.100.120.14Normalized",4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600
