astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_000,0,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution front_matter Draft version March 30, 2026 Typeset using L ATEXtwocolumn style in AASTeX7 Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution Xiuyuan Zhang ,1Andreas Thoyas ,2Lina Necib ,1Andrew Wetzel ,3and Arpit Arora 4 1Department of Physics and Kavli Institute for Astrophysics and Space Research, MIT, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA 2Department of Physics, Northeastern University, Boston, MA 02115, USA 31Department of Physics & Astronomy, University of California, Davis, CA 95616, USA 4Department of Astronomy and DiRAC Institute, University of Washington, 3910 15th Ave NE, Seattle, WA, 98195, USA",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,137,105,False,False,0,105
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_001,1,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution ABSTRACT The majority of terrestrial direct detection experiments for Dark Matter (DM) rely on the Standard Halo Model (SHM), which assumes the local DM velocity distribution follows a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution. However, galaxy mergers can deposit DM that remains kinematically clustered today, inducing deviations from the smooth SHM prediction. Previous studies have suggested that the lo- cal stellar velocity distribution may serve as a tracer for DM populations originating from the same progenitor systems. In this work, we systematically investigate how merger mass and accretion time affect the correlation between local stellar and DM velocity distributions in Milky Way–like galaxies from the FIRE-2 simulations. We find a strong correlation between traceable DM components and their stellar counterparts, with the tightest correspondence arising from lower-mass mergers accreted at earlier cosmic times. For the remaining DM that lacks an identifiable stellar counterpart, which dominate the full DM fraction, we find that its velocity distribution is well described by a component- wise generalized Gaussian. Combining these two ingredients, we reconstruct the",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_002,2,"cosmic times. For the remaining DM that lacks an identifiable stellar counterpart, which dominate the full DM fraction, we find that its velocity distribution is well described by a component- wise generalized Gaussian. Combining these two ingredients, we reconstruct the full local DM velocity distribution. This framework captures merger-induced features—such as co-rotation of accreted ma- terial with the galactic disk—that are entirely absent in the SHM. Finally, we propagate uncertainties through the reconstruction and show that they are dominated by the stellar mass–halo mass relation, which is unlikely to improve substantially in the near term. We therefore argue that this framework approaches the current limit of our ability to characterize the local DM velocity distribution. Keywords: Galaxies (573) — Dark Matter (353) — Milky Way dark matter halo (1049) — Solar neighborhood (1509)",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,173,133,False,False,140,273
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_003,3,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F. Zwicky 1933; V. C. Rubin et al. 1980; G. Bertone & D. Hooper 2018) is a non-luminous component of matter whose total abundance is inferred to be approximately five times that of ordinary baryonic matter (D. N. Spergel et al. 2003; C. L. Bennett et al. 2003). It has remained a central open problem in both particle physics and astrophysics for several decades. Observations indicate that every galaxy is embedded in an extended DM halo that dominates its mass bud- get (J. P. Ostriker & P. J. E. Peebles 1973; J. Einasto 2004; G. Bertone & D. Hooper 2018). Thus, one ma- jor strategy for probing DM in terrestrial experiments is direct detection, which aims to observe rare scattering events between DM particles and Standard Model tar- Email: xiuyuan@mit.edugets (A. Drukier & L. Stodolsky 1984; M. W. Goodman & E. Witten 1985; A. K. Drukier et al. 1986; K. Freese et al. 2013). All of these searches rely critically on the assumed",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_004,4,"particles and Standard Model tar- Email: xiuyuan@mit.edugets (A. Drukier & L. Stodolsky 1984; M. W. Goodman & E. Witten 1985; A. K. Drukier et al. 1986; K. Freese et al. 2013). All of these searches rely critically on the assumed flux of DM passing through the Earth, which is determined by the local DM density and velocity dis- tribution. The most commonly adopted assumption for the DM velocity distribution is the Standard Halo Model (SHM) (A. K. Drukier et al. 1986; J. D. Lewin & P. F. Smith 1996), which describes the Galactic DM halo as an isothermal, spherically symmetric system in equilib- rium. In this framework, the local DM velocities follows a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution,5and the spherical Jeans’ equation relates the one-dimensional DM veloc- 5The isothermal solution does not formally include a cutoff; in practice, a cutoff at the Galactic escape speed is imposed, typ- ically vesc∼500−600 km /s (see e.g. M. C. Smith et al. 2007;arXiv:2603.25783v1 [astro-ph.GA] 26 Mar 2026 2 ity dispersion to the circular velocity via σDM=vc/√ 2, where a flat rotation curve with vc∼220 km /s",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_005,5,"escape speed is imposed, typ- ically vesc∼500−600 km /s (see e.g. M. C. Smith et al. 2007;arXiv:2603.25783v1 [astro-ph.GA] 26 Mar 2026 2 ity dispersion to the circular velocity via σDM=vc/√ 2, where a flat rotation curve with vc∼220 km /s is as- sumed (F. J. Kerr & D. Lynden-Bell 1986; J. Bland- Hawthorn & O. Gerhard 2016; A.-C. Eilers et al. 2019). The isothermal profile underlying the SHM assumes ρ∝r−2, with rthe spherical distance from the center of galaxy, producing this flat rotation curve – a sim- plification that deviates from the Milky Way’s actual mass distribution. While this deviation is small at the solar position, more generally, the local velocity distri- bution depends on the halo’s density slope, any radial variation in the velocity dispersion σ, and the velocity anisotropy. Furthermore, the Milky Way’s halo is not expected to be in equilibrium due to its hierarchical as- sembly history and ongoing merger events, so the true DM velocity distribution is expected to depart from the Maxwell-Boltzmann form assumed in the SHM. Several groups have used cosmological simulations to quantify",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_006,6,"be in equilibrium due to its hierarchical as- sembly history and ongoing merger events, so the true DM velocity distribution is expected to depart from the Maxwell-Boltzmann form assumed in the SHM. Several groups have used cosmological simulations to quantify these departures and their consequences for di- rect detection. Using high-resolution Milky Way–like halos, M. Vogelsberger et al. (2009) found that nu- clear recoil rates can differ from the SHM prediction by up to 10%. Y.-Y. Mao et al. (2013, 2014) char- acterized the halo-to-halo scatter in the local velocity distribution, showing that it can appreciably shift ex- clusion limits and preferred parameter regions. Analy- ses based on Via Lactea–type halos (J. Diemand et al. 2007, 2008) explicitly compared simulation-derived re- coil spectra against SHM predictions, finding that light and inelastic DM scenarios are particularly sensitive to structure in the high-velocity tail (A. Friedland & I. M. Shoemaker 2013). More recently, hydrodynamical sim- ulations incorporating baryons have revealed that disk formation and baryonic contraction reshape the local DM phase-space distribution, further modifying direct- detection interpretations N. Bozorgnia & G. Bertone",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_007,7,"high-velocity tail (A. Friedland & I. M. Shoemaker 2013). More recently, hydrodynamical sim- ulations incorporating baryons have revealed that disk formation and baryonic contraction reshape the local DM phase-space distribution, further modifying direct- detection interpretations N. Bozorgnia & G. Bertone (2017). Taken together, these studies establish that the assumed velocity distribution leads to systematic uncer- tainties in direct-detection analyses. Recent developments in observational astrometry have shown that the Milky Way (MW) has undergone multi- ple mergers throughout its history (see e.g., A. Helmi 2020, for a review). The most prominent merger is the Gaia–Sausage–Enceladus (GSE) (V. Belokurov et al. 2018; A. Helmi et al. 2018); the GSE is assumed to have been accreted to the MW about 10 billion years ago, and was the result of a 1:3 merger (J. W. Johnson et al. 2025) that contributed around one-fourth of the total mass of the MW and is likely responsible for thickening the G. Monari et al. 2018; H. H. Koppelman & A. Helmi 2021; L. Necib & T. Lin 2022; C. Roche et al. 2024).MW’s disk (A. Helmi et",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_008,8,"the total mass of the MW and is likely responsible for thickening the G. Monari et al. 2018; H. H. Koppelman & A. Helmi 2021; L. Necib & T. Lin 2022; C. Roche et al. 2024).MW’s disk (A. Helmi et al. 2018; H. H. Koppelman et al. 2020). Other mergers include the currently stripped Sagittarius Dwarf Spheroidal Galaxy (Sgr dSph) (R. A. Ibata et al. 1994; R. A. Ibata et al. 1995), as well as the infalling satellites like the Large and Small Magellanic Cloud (LMC and SMC) (N. Kallivayalil et al. 2006; G. Besla et al. 2007) that also potentially affects the local DM velocity distribution (G. Besla et al. 2019). It was then proposed in J. Herzog-Arbeitman et al. (2018) that the stellar velocity distribution can serve as a proxy for the full DM velocity distribution. Sub- sequently, using two Milky Way–like galaxies from the FIRE-2 simulations (A. R. Wetzel et al. 2016; P. F. Hop- kins et al. 2018), L. Necib et al. (2019) demonstrated a correlation between the velocity distributions of differ- ent stellar populations and",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_009,9,"using two Milky Way–like galaxies from the FIRE-2 simulations (A. R. Wetzel et al. 2016; P. F. Hop- kins et al. 2018), L. Necib et al. (2019) demonstrated a correlation between the velocity distributions of differ- ent stellar populations and distinct DM components in the solar neighborhood. In particular, DM and stars associated with early mergers (at redshift z≳3) are dynamically relaxed and therefore exhibit a strong cor- respondence. By contrast, material from more recent mergers is only partially phase-mixed, leaving behind debris flows and cold streams. While the stellar–DM correspondence remains adequate for debris-flow com- ponents, it becomes significantly less reliable for cold streams (with infall times z < 1) due to their pro- nounced spatial inhomogeneity. Consequently, a more sophisticated treatment is required to reconstruct the underlying DM velocity distribution from stellar trac- ers. Subsequent work in T. Shpigel et al. (2025) showed that, in the presence of a single late-time merger, it is nevertheless possible to recover the DM speed distribu- tion accurately by incorporating a simple boost factor into the stellar-based reconstruction. In this work, we",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_010,10,"Shpigel et al. (2025) showed that, in the presence of a single late-time merger, it is nevertheless possible to recover the DM speed distribu- tion accurately by incorporating a simple boost factor into the stellar-based reconstruction. In this work, we investigate seven m12FIRE galax- ies, comprising a total of 24 major mergers, to establish a concrete and self-consistent reconstruction of the lo- cal DM velocity distribution. We begin by decomposing the local DM population into two major components: DM accreted through mergers, and a smoothly accreted diffuse component. The merger component is then fur- ther divided into resolved and unresolved merger com- ponents. The resolved component is discussed in Sec. 3. Ranking by their contributions to the local accreted stel- lar mass, the top four contributors are designated as traceable mergers (Sec. 3.1), while the remaining is the untraceable merger component (Sec. 3.2), which is fur- ther divided into young and old untraceable mergers by their accretion time. The unresolved component and un- traceable mergers are then combined together with the diffuse component to define a single relaxed component. Thus",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,980,1160
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_011,11,"component (Sec. 3.2), which is fur- ther divided into young and old untraceable mergers by their accretion time. The unresolved component and un- traceable mergers are then combined together with the diffuse component to define a single relaxed component. Thus the local velocity component can be structured as 3 the traceable mergers and the relax component, fDM(v) =4X i=1cifi(v) + (1 −4X i=1ci)frelaxed (v),(1) where ciis the mass fraction of local DM from the i-th traceable merger, fiis the corresponding velocity dis- tribution, and frelaxed is the velocity distribution of the relaxed component. We establish a connection between the stellar and DM velocity distributions originating from the same merger, and characterize how this connection varies with accre- tion time. We model the diffuse component as a gener- alized Gaussian function and demonstrate, using simu- lations, that the total local DM velocity distribution can be robustly reconstructed. We assess both the sensitiv- ity of this reconstruction to the numerical resolution of the simulations and the main sources of error that would arise in an application to the Milky Way. Alternatively, we",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_012,12,"velocity distribution can be robustly reconstructed. We assess both the sensitiv- ity of this reconstruction to the numerical resolution of the simulations and the main sources of error that would arise in an application to the Milky Way. Alternatively, we also demonstrate that the general- ized Gaussian by itself can be directly used as an ap- proximation of the full velocity distribution. In terms of speed distribution, it is a simple yet consistent im- provement over SHM. However, it might fail to predict the azimuthal velocity distribution in the presence of co-rotation. This paper is organized as follows. In Sec. 2, we intro- duce the FIRE simulation suite: we summarize the key properties of the various m12galaxies in Sec. 2.1, and de- scribe our methodology for associating DM and stellar particles with individual mergers and tracing them back in time in Sec. 2.2. In Sec. 3, we start with a discus- sion of the merger component and examine the prop- erties of DM and stellar components associated with major mergers. We begin by introducing the statis- tical framework in Sec.",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1260,1440
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_013,13,"in Sec. 2.2. In Sec. 3, we start with a discus- sion of the merger component and examine the prop- erties of DM and stellar components associated with major mergers. We begin by introducing the statis- tical framework in Sec. 3.1.1, analyze the dependence of stellar–DM correlations on merger accretion times in Sec. 3.1.2, and present a robust comparison of the full three-dimensional velocity distributions in Sec. 3.1.3. We further investigate the use of boost factors to better align stellar and DM velocity distributions in Sec. 3.1.4. In Sec. 4, we turn to the diffuse DM component, show- ing that it dominates the local DM budget and is well described by a generalized Gaussian velocity distribu- tion. Building on this, Sec. 5 outlines our method for re- constructing the local DM speed distribution from stel- lar kinematic data alone. Additionally, we demonstrate that the generalized Standard Halo Model (gSHM) as a standalone DM distribution model provides a consistent improvement over the traditional SHM. We discuss the limitations and uncertainties of our approach in Sec. 6.1, and assess the robustness of",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1400,1580
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_014,14,"we demonstrate that the generalized Standard Halo Model (gSHM) as a standalone DM distribution model provides a consistent improvement over the traditional SHM. We discuss the limitations and uncertainties of our approach in Sec. 6.1, and assess the robustness of our results across simulation resolutions in Sec. 6.2. We conclude in Sec. 7.2.SIMULATIONS 2.1. FIRE-2 Simulation In this work, we analyze the full merger history of the Latte suite of cosmological zoom-in hydrodynamic simu- lations with FIRE-2 physics—namely m12i,m12f,m12w, m12m,m12b,m12c, and m12r—together with their corre- sponding lower-resolution realizations across 600 snap- shots from redshifts z= 0−99. We also include the higher-resolution “Triple Latte” rerun of m12i to be in- troduced in Wetzel et al., in prep. All runs employ the Feedback In Realistic Environ- ments (FIRE)-2 galaxy-formation model implemented in GIZMO with the meshless finite-mass (MFM) hy- drodynamics solver (P. F. Hopkins et al. 2014; P. F. Hopkins 2015; A. R. Wetzel et al. 2016; P. F. Hopkins et al. 2018). Gas follows radiative cooling and heat- ing from ∼10–1010K, including primordial and metal- line processes, Compton and free–free emission,",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1540,1720
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_015,15,"F. Hopkins et al. 2014; P. F. Hopkins 2015; A. R. Wetzel et al. 2016; P. F. Hopkins et al. 2018). Gas follows radiative cooling and heat- ing from ∼10–1010K, including primordial and metal- line processes, Compton and free–free emission, molecu- lar/dust cooling at low temperatures, and photoheating by a redshift-dependent UV background (with local self- shielding) (C.-A. Faucher-Gigu` ere et al. 2009). Star for- mation is restricted to dense, locally self-gravitating gas (typical criterion nH≥103,cm−3) and once a gas cell meets the criteria for star formation, it converts to a star particle on a local free-fall time (with the global efficiency regulated by feedback). Each star particle represents a single-age stellar population drawn from a Kroupa IMF (P. Kroupa 2001). The feedback model is time-resolved and includes Type II and Ia supernovae, OB and AGB stellar winds, photoionization and photo- electric heating, and radiation pressure. Mass, metals, energy, and momentum are returned to neighboring gas elements in a manifestly conservative manner, tracking multiple metal species with subgrid turbulent metal dif- fusion. The baryonic and DM mass resolutions and adopted",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1680,1860
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_016,16,"photo- electric heating, and radiation pressure. Mass, metals, energy, and momentum are returned to neighboring gas elements in a manifestly conservative manner, tracking multiple metal species with subgrid turbulent metal dif- fusion. The baryonic and DM mass resolutions and adopted cosmologies for the Latte suite are summarized in Ta- ble 1. Each halo is selected as a MW analog with virial mass ∼1012M⊙and is isolated, with no com- parably massive halo within five virial radii.6The sim- ulations adopt two standard cosmologies: ‘A’ denotes the AGORA cosmology (Ω m= 0.272, Ω Λ= 0.728, Ωb= 0.0455, h= 0.702,σ8= 0.807,ns= 0.961) and ‘P’ denotes the Planck cosmology (Ω m= 0.31, Ω Λ= 0.69, Ωb= 0.0458, h= 0.68,σ8= 0.82,ns= 0.97). Gravita- tional softening (Plummer-equivalent) are 4 pc for stars and 40 pc for DM; the minimum adaptive gas softening 6We define the virial radius R200as the radius enclosing an av- erage density 200 ρc, where ρc= 3H(t)2/(8πG), and the virial mass as the mass contained within the virial radius. 4 10 kpcm12i 10 kpcm12f 10 kpcm12w 10 kpcm12m 10 kpcm12b 10 kpcm12c 10 kpcm12r",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1820,2000
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_017,17,"R200as the radius enclosing an av- erage density 200 ρc, where ρc= 3H(t)2/(8πG), and the virial mass as the mass contained within the virial radius. 4 10 kpcm12i 10 kpcm12f 10 kpcm12w 10 kpcm12m 10 kpcm12b 10 kpcm12c 10 kpcm12r Figure 1. Edge-on stellar surface density maps at z= 0 for the seven Milky Way–mass simulations: m12i,m12f,m12w,m12m, m12b,m12c, and m12r. Stellar particles are shown within |x| ≤30 kpc and |z| ≤30 kpc in the principal-axis frame of the host galaxy. White regions correspond to higher stellar surface density. The figure highlights morphological diversity in disk structure among the simulated Milky Way analogs. Name Mhalo[M⊙]mstar[M⊙]mdm[M⊙]Cosmology m12w 1.08×10127100 39000 P m12r 1.10×10127100 39000 P m12i 1.18×10127100 35000 A m12c 1.35×10127100 35000 A m12b 1.43×10127100 35000 A m12m 1.58×10127100 35000 A m12f 1.71×10127100 35000 A Table 1. Properties of the m12simulations in this paper, including the halo mass M halo, stellar and DM particle mass, mstarand m dm, and Cosmology that specifies energy density ratios, Hubble constants, σ8andns. ‘P’ stands for Planck and ‘A’ stands for AGORA. m12r is not further included for",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1960,2140
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_018,18,"paper, including the halo mass M halo, stellar and DM particle mass, mstarand m dm, and Cosmology that specifies energy density ratios, Hubble constants, σ8andns. ‘P’ stands for Planck and ‘A’ stands for AGORA. m12r is not further included for analysis due to a late time equal mass merger that fully disrupted the stellar disk making it an inappropriate MW analog. is 1 pc. The lower-resolution counterparts use particle masses coarser by a factor of ∼8 and softening larger by∼2, whereas Triple Latte attains ∼8 times finer mass resolution (i.e., particle masses smaller by ∼8) with softening smaller by ∼2; in all cases the cosmology matches that of the corresponding parent run in A. R. Wetzel et al. (2016). Across the m12suite, we find that accreted stars pref- erentially occupy prograde orbits over a wide range of metallicities. The primary exception is m12i, which ex- hibits a pronounced prograde bias only at higher metal- licity (I. B. Santistevan et al. 2021). This behavior is broadly in agreement with the observation of the MW, which is largely dependent on the major",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,2100,2280
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_019,19,"exception is m12i, which ex- hibits a pronounced prograde bias only at higher metal- licity (I. B. Santistevan et al. 2021). This behavior is broadly in agreement with the observation of the MW, which is largely dependent on the major merger history.2.1.1. m12i Among these simulations, m12i is the first high- resolution MW halo to be extensively analyzed and re- mains a particularly well-studied analog: it has a total stellar mass M⋆≃5.5×1010M⊙, consistent with the MW’s (5 ±1)×1010M⊙(T. C. Licquia & J. A. Newman 2015; P. J. McMillan 2016), and at z= 0 hosts a thin, ex- tended stellar disk with a prominent spiral pattern and bar (P. F. Hopkins et al. 2014; S. Garrison-Kimmel et al. 2018; S. Ansar et al. 2024). It also reproduces reason- ably the MW satellite luminosity function and spatial distribution (S. Garrison-Kimmel et al. 2019; J. Samuel et al. 2020), and—critically—exhibits a quiescent re- cent merger history (post- z∼1), including a some- what plausible analog of the Gaia-Sausage/Enceladus event (a relatively massive, radial merger at z∼1.2 [see Table. 2]). However, its strongly warped, flared",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,2240,2420
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_020,20,"2019; J. Samuel et al. 2020), and—critically—exhibits a quiescent re- cent merger history (post- z∼1), including a some- what plausible analog of the Gaia-Sausage/Enceladus event (a relatively massive, radial merger at z∼1.2 [see Table. 2]). However, its strongly warped, flared outer stellar disk—the outer disk whose mid-plane bends and becomes noticeably misaligned with the inner disk and with the halo symmetry axes (R. E. Sanderson et al. 2020; J. Baptista et al. 2022), more so than in many of the other m12runs as shown in Fig. 1, which makes it an imperfect match to the MW in detail.7 2.1.2. m12f Compared to m12i,m12f sits closer to an inter- mediate “MW/M31-like” regime: it is more massive, with a higher stellar mass and a rotationally supported 7Selection criteria for GSE-analogs are discussed in Appendix A. 5 disk that experiences short-lived bar episodes at late times (S. Ansar et al. 2024; J. R. Quinn et al. 2025). There is no strongly misaligned or polar inner disk as inm12i, but the disk is more massive, thicker, and has a higher–surface-density than the Milky Way’s",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,2380,2560
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_021,21,"at late times (S. Ansar et al. 2024; J. R. Quinn et al. 2025). There is no strongly misaligned or polar inner disk as inm12i, but the disk is more massive, thicker, and has a higher–surface-density than the Milky Way’s (F. Mc- Cluskey et al. 2024). In terms of accretion history, m12f undergoes two ma- jor mergers at redshifts z∼1 and z∼0.1. The earlier event is broadly consistent with the estimated infall time of GSE, while the later one occurs too recently to serve as a GSE analog. The DM halo masses at infall for these mergers are ∼8.2×1010M⊙and∼1.5×1011M⊙, respectively—both within the expected range for GSE- like progenitors. Each merger exhibits a stellar velocity anisotropy of β∼0.7 at z= 0, indicating moderately radial orbits, albeit slightly less radial than the canoni- cal GSE value. However, when considering their stellar contributions to the solar neighborhood, both mergers deviate from standard GSE expectations. The z∼1 merger con- tributes approximately 18 .5% of the local accreted stel- lar population, which is too small to be considered a dominant GSE-like progenitor. In contrast, the",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,2520,2700
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_022,22,"to the solar neighborhood, both mergers deviate from standard GSE expectations. The z∼1 merger con- tributes approximately 18 .5% of the local accreted stel- lar population, which is too small to be considered a dominant GSE-like progenitor. In contrast, the more recent z∼0.1 merger accounts for around 44% of local accreted stars, placing it near the lower end of observa- tional estimates for the GSE contribution, but its very late accretion time makes it inconsistent with the ex- pected GSE epoch. Together, these results suggest that while both mergers share individual characteristics with GSE, neither serves as a definitive GSE analog in all respects. 2.1.3. m12b m12b is a comparatively quiescent Milky Way analog, with a relatively quiet late-time assembly history. Apart from a single massive satellite accreted at z∼0.25 which accounts for 72% of the accreted material, all major mergers occur at z≥2. By z= 0, it hosts a barred, rotationally supported disk with roughly MW- like mass and size, though again slightly more massive, more compact, and dynamically thicker than the real Galaxy (P. F. Hopkins et al.",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,2660,2840
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_023,23,"mergers occur at z≥2. By z= 0, it hosts a barred, rotationally supported disk with roughly MW- like mass and size, though again slightly more massive, more compact, and dynamically thicker than the real Galaxy (P. F. Hopkins et al. 2019; F. McCluskey et al. 2024). The disk is not as dramatically warped as in m12i; the outer-disk bending and flaring appear mod- est (M. Barry et al. 2023; A. Arora et al. 2024). The late accreted satellite overwhelmingly dominates the ac- creted stellar component and is on a moderately radial orbit, with β∼0.6, though it is less radially biased than a canonical GSE event. Nevertheless, its accretion atz∼0.25 is likely too late for it to serve as a clean GSE analog, despite its dominant contribution and ra- dial kinematics.2.1.4. m12w m12w is a MW-mass halo with a noticeably more ac- tive late-time assembly history than systems such as m12b. At z= 0, it remains dynamically hot, with a relatively thick, disturbed stellar disk and a bursty late- time star-formation history, making it a poor morpho- logical match to the",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,2800,2980
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_024,24,"ac- tive late-time assembly history than systems such as m12b. At z= 0, it remains dynamically hot, with a relatively thick, disturbed stellar disk and a bursty late- time star-formation history, making it a poor morpho- logical match to the observed Milky Way (S. Yu et al. 2021; M. E. Orr et al. 2021; A. Arora et al. 2022; S. Yu et al. 2023). One of its late mergers is a reasonably GSE-like, radial 1 : 8 event at an appropriate lookback time and mass scale, and it dominates the accreted stel- lar population in the solar neighborhood. However, this interpretation is not clean: another merger of compa- rable mass occurs around a similar epoch even though on a more tangential orbit, contributing relatively lit- tle to the local stellar content, and the radial merger event itself occurs at z∼0.5, somewhat later than typ- ically expected for a canonical GSE analog. The late merger sequence is biased toward prograde orbits (A. J. Deason et al. 2011; I. B. Santistevan et al. 2021). As a result, the accreted material carries coherently",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,2940,3120
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_025,25,"somewhat later than typ- ically expected for a canonical GSE analog. The late merger sequence is biased toward prograde orbits (A. J. Deason et al. 2011; I. B. Santistevan et al. 2021). As a result, the accreted material carries coherently aligned angular momentum that is deposited into the inner halo. This torques the pre-existing DM and produces substan- tial co-rotation of the dark halo with the stellar disk at z= 0; (N. Garavito-Camargo et al. 2023; A. Arora et al. 2025) the effect primarily reflects the angular momen- tum of the accreted DM rather than simple in-situ stel- lar heating (J. I. Read et al. 2008; J. Zavala et al. 2016; J. Baptista et al. 2023). In practice, m12w is therefore useful as a laboratory for studying GSE-like satellites in an MW-mass system, but imperfect due to halo–disk torques, and active late-time accretion. 2.1.5. m12m m12m sits at the massive, M31-like end of the suite: it resides in a halo of M200∼1.58×1012M⊙and hosts ∼1011M⊙in stars, making it significantly more massive and more richly populated with satellites than the Milky Way.",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,3080,3260
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_026,26,"active late-time accretion. 2.1.5. m12m m12m sits at the massive, M31-like end of the suite: it resides in a halo of M200∼1.58×1012M⊙and hosts ∼1011M⊙in stars, making it significantly more massive and more richly populated with satellites than the Milky Way. Its early assembly is dominated by two compara- bly massive components—the main branch and a gas- rich building block on a largely prograde orbit—which merge to form a proto-galaxy whose old, metal-poor stars remain in a very thick, strongly rotating, flat- tened configuration, co-rotating with the eventual disk atvϕ∼100km /s (I. B. Santistevan et al. 2021; D. Horta et al. 2023). As a result, m12m develops a pronounced prograde bias in its ancient stellar populations rather than a GSE-like, kinematically hot, nearly non-rotating inner halo. Morphologically, m12m forms a large, dy- namically hot stellar disc and a strong bar only at late times (bar formation at z∼0.2) (S. Garrison-Kimmel et al. 2018; F. McCluskey et al. 2024), with bar buck- 6 ling producing an X-shaped bulge that is heavily con- taminated by stars younger than ∼10 Gyr—again in tension with",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,3220,3400
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_027,27,"at late times (bar formation at z∼0.2) (S. Garrison-Kimmel et al. 2018; F. McCluskey et al. 2024), with bar buck- 6 ling producing an X-shaped bulge that is heavily con- taminated by stars younger than ∼10 Gyr—again in tension with the predominantly old ages inferred for the Galactic bulge. Taken together, m12m is interesting as a massive, bar-dominated MW-mass system with an MW- like ensemble early accretion history, but it is not a par- ticularly clean Milky Way analog: it is too massive, its bar forms too late, its old stars rotate too strongly, and its dominant early building block does not manifest as a neat, GSE-like radial “sausage” in the inner halo. 2.1.6. m12c m12c sits quite close to the Milky Way in bulk prop- erties: its halo mass M200∼1.35×1012M⊙and stellar mass∼5−6×1010M⊙are broadly MW-like, and by z= 0 it hosts an extended, fairly settled stellar disk that is somewhat older, redder, and more gas-poor than the real Galaxy’s, with at most a weak or transient bar (S. Garrison-Kimmel et al. 2018; S. Yu et al. 2023; S. Ansar et",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,3360,3540
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_028,28,"hosts an extended, fairly settled stellar disk that is somewhat older, redder, and more gas-poor than the real Galaxy’s, with at most a weak or transient bar (S. Garrison-Kimmel et al. 2018; S. Yu et al. 2023; S. Ansar et al. 2024). Its early assembly, however, is not dom- inated by a single GSE-like event, but by a couple of relatively heavy mergers of comparable mass that fall in rapid succession around z∼1−1.5. As a result, the inner accreted halo is built from several similarly important progenitors rather than exhibiting a clean, single radial “sausage,” and there is no obvious GSE analog. After z∼1,m12c’s accretion history is compar- atively quiet. Thus, while m12c is reasonably MW-like in present-day mass and the existence of a fairly undis- turbed z= 0 disk, it is a poor analog for the detailed MW assembly history. 2.1.7. m12r Finally, m12r is disfavored as a Milky Way analog: it experiences a very massive merger at z∼0.09 that effec- tively destroys the stellar disk at late times (Q. Ma et al. 2025), as can be seen in",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,3500,3680
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_029,29,"history. 2.1.7. m12r Finally, m12r is disfavored as a Milky Way analog: it experiences a very massive merger at z∼0.09 that effec- tively destroys the stellar disk at late times (Q. Ma et al. 2025), as can be seen in Fig. 1. Because its present-day morphology and kinematics are incompatible with those of the MW, we exclude m12r from further consideration. 2.2. Building the Merger History To construct the merger history, we categorize DM particles into two broad components: major mergers, de- fined as systems with total mass exceeding 109M⊙that contain at least ∼ O(10) star particles, and the diffuse component, which encompasses both smoothly accreted DM and contributions from low-mass subhaloes below the resolution limit. In contrast, star particles either form in situ or are brought in via mergers; since in- situstars are not the focus of this study, we exclusively track those originating from mergers. In this section, we describe our methodology for tracking DM and stel- 0 2 4 6 Redshift103 102 101 100Cumulative Total FractionFIREHostHalom12i Stars Mpeak>109M DiffuseFigure 2. Example accumulated fraction of the stellar and",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,3640,3820
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_030,30,"we exclusively track those originating from mergers. In this section, we describe our methodology for tracking DM and stel- 0 2 4 6 Redshift103 102 101 100Cumulative Total FractionFIREHostHalom12i Stars Mpeak>109M DiffuseFigure 2. Example accumulated fraction of the stellar and different DM components as a function of redshift for m12i. The red curve represents all accreted stars. The green curve represents DM accreted from major mergers heavier than 109M⊙and the blue curve shows the DM accreted either smoothly into the main halo or with unresolved small subhalos ( <109M⊙) lar particles and assigning them to their corresponding progenitors, thereby reconstructing the merger history. To track the subhalos and identify the particles asso- ciated with them, we utilize two complementary pack- ages for halo identification and merger-tree construc- tion: Rockstar and Consistent Trees (CT). Rockstar is a phase-space halo finder that identifies (sub)halos by clustering particles in six-dimensional phase space and assigning properties such as mass and velocity (P. S. Behroozi et al. 2012a; J. Samuel et al. 2021). Rockstar is run only on the DM particles, and stars are assigned",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,3780,3960
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_031,31,"identifies (sub)halos by clustering particles in six-dimensional phase space and assigning properties such as mass and velocity (P. S. Behroozi et al. 2012a; J. Samuel et al. 2021). Rockstar is run only on the DM particles, and stars are assigned to each halo in postprocessing using similar phase-space criteria. We adopt Rockstar to construct the merger history of each system, as it enables the association of stars with their progenitor subhalos. In each snapshot, Rockstar defines the main halo as the most massive one and thus the galactocentric frame, which allows for a reliable merger catalog out to redshifts of z≲3. However, defining the galactocentric frame using Rockstar can introduce discontinuities at earlier times. Since the identity of the most massive halo may switch abruptly from one snapshot to the next, particle posi- tions relative to the main halo can exhibit unphysical jumps. These discontinuities may also lead to the mis- classification of bound particles as part of the diffuse component. To mitigate these issues, we instead rely on CT, which builds merger trees by tracing the main progeni- tor",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,3920,4100
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_032,32,"exhibit unphysical jumps. These discontinuities may also lead to the mis- classification of bound particles as part of the diffuse component. To mitigate these issues, we instead rely on CT, which builds merger trees by tracing the main progeni- tor branch of each halo backward in time while enforcing 7 m12i m12f",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,68,52,False,False,4060,4112
