astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_043,43,"statistical metric used in this work, and in Secs. 3.1.2 and 3.1.3 we apply it to traceable mergers to quantify stellar–DM correlations. We then extend this analysis in Sec. 3.1.4, where we develop an improved mapping from stellar kinematics to the corresponding DM dis- tributions for each progenitor. Finally, in Sec. 3.2, we discuss the treatment of untraceable mergers and justify the assumptions adopted for their contributions. 3.1. The Traceable Mergers In this section, we investigate the correlation between DM and stellar velocity distributions at redshift z= 0 for traceable mergers. An illustrative example for a traceable merger in m12i is shown in Fig. 4. The veloc- ities are decomposed into radial and transverse compo- nents in spherical coordinates, centered on the galactic center, as well as the total speed distribution for both the stellar and DM components. In this case, the stellar velocity distribution appears to closely follow that of the DM. However, to quantitatively assess the level of agree- 10The exact values of these fractions are listed in Table S2 9 400 200 0 200 400 vr [km/s]01234103f(vr)[km/s]1",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
