astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_000,0,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation front_matter MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026) Preprint 27 March 2026 Compiled using MNRAS L ATEX style file v3.0 BarPropertiesandStar-by-StarBarMembershipviaActionConservation Elizabeth J. Iles,1,2★Finn A. Pal,2Joss Bland-Hawthorn,1and Ken Freeman3 1Sydney Institute for Astronomy (SIfA), Sydney University, Camperdown 2050, Australia 2School of Physics, University of New South Wales, Kensington 2052, Australia 3Research School of Astronomy & Astrophysics, Mount Stromlo Observatory, Cotter Road, Weston Creek, ACT 2611, Australia Accepted XXX. Received YYY; in original form ZZZ",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,103,79,False,False,0,79
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_001,1,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation ABSTRACT A bar-like central feature is commonly observed in both nearby and distant spiral-type galaxies, including the Milky Way. While many methods exist to categorise this morphology, no one method has emerged as the field-wide standard. To develop a rigorous and consistent method for identifying these bars, we investigate a classification scheme based on dynamical actions. In the Gaia era, actions can be estimated for individual stars in both observations and simulations, making this a natural and unifyingdiagnostic,assumingtheintrinsicerrorsandselectionfunctionsareunderstood.Ourapproachisstraightforward:stars that participate in the bar are subject to a strongly non-axisymmetric potential and, therefore, do not completely conserve their actions. We use this property to define a star-by-star criterion, formulated as an inequality and evaluated within measurement uncertainties, to identify bar members based on the degree to which their total action fails to be conserved. From tests on simulated galaxies, we find that the bar region is indeed characterised by a lower fraction of stars with conserved actions and that stars on bar orbits are represented by larger percentage changes in their actions.",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_002,2,"be conserved. From tests on simulated galaxies, we find that the bar region is indeed characterised by a lower fraction of stars with conserved actions and that stars on bar orbits are represented by larger percentage changes in their actions. We are able to classify the spatial extent of barred region via the standard parameters of bar length and orientation, while also individually separating bar-located from bar-member stars on bar orbits. As proof of concept, our automated method based on dynamical actions robustly identifies bar parameters that closely match the eye’s performance (average bar length variation∼9%) in barred snapshots of the test galaxy. Key words:galaxies: structure – galaxies: fundamental parameters – galaxies: kinematics and dynamics",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,150,115,False,False,140,255
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_003,3,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure present in the centre of many disk galaxies, while a key morphological feature for galaxy classifica- tion, is similarly significant for driving galaxy evolution. Bars have beendemonstratedtoaffecthost-galaxyprocessesonallscales:from star formation on molecular-cloud scales (10−100pc; e.g. Kuno et al. 2000; Dobbs et al. 2006, 2014; Nilipour et al. 2024; Maeda et al. 2025) to galaxy-wide dynamics (∼1000pc; e.g. Regan et al. 1999; O’Neill & Dubinski 2003; Emsellem et al. 2006; Iles et al. 2024;Géronetal.2024;Rutherfordetal.2025);includingdisk–halo and disk–bulge co-evolution (e.g. Athanassoula 2002; Valenzuela & Klypin 2003; Kormendy & Kennicutt 2004; Jogee et al. 2005; Athanassoula et al. 2013; Kruk et al. 2018), angular momentum distribution (e.g. Weinberg 1985; Athanassoula 2005; Romeo et al. 2023), Active Galactic Nuclei (AGN) (e.g. Shlosman et al. 1989; Alonsoetal.2014;Garlandetal.2023),Inter-StellarMedium(ISM) structure (Friedli & Benz 1993; Lindblad & Lindblad 1994; Pettitt etal.2020;Katsiolietal.2026),gasdepletionandquenching(Mas- ters et al. 2012; Spinoso et al. 2017; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2020; Géron et al. 2021), for example. Accounting for up to∼75% of nearbydiskgalaxies(e.g.Schinnereretal.2002;Aguerrietal.2009; Nair&Abraham2010;Saha&Elmegreen2018),anincreasingnum- ★E-mail: elizabeth.iles@sydney.edu.au)berofgalaxiesathighredshift(e.g.Guoetal.2023;Costantinetal. 2023; Liang et al. 2024; Le Conte",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_004,4,"Lindblad & Lindblad 1994; Pettitt etal.2020;Katsiolietal.2026),gasdepletionandquenching(Mas- ters et al. 2012; Spinoso et al. 2017; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2020; Géron et al. 2021), for example. Accounting for up to∼75% of nearbydiskgalaxies(e.g.Schinnereretal.2002;Aguerrietal.2009; Nair&Abraham2010;Saha&Elmegreen2018),anincreasingnum- ★E-mail: elizabeth.iles@sydney.edu.au)berofgalaxiesathighredshift(e.g.Guoetal.2023;Costantinetal. 2023; Liang et al. 2024; Le Conte et al. 2024, 2025), and even the Milky Way (e.g. Blitz & Spergel 1991; Nakada et al. 1991; Paczyn- skietal.1994;Zhaoetal.1994;Bland-Hawthorn&Gerhard2016), these features are well-studied and yet, despite their ubiquity and impact, we still lack a distinct, universally-applicable method used to define the spatial extent of the “bar” in these galaxies (see, for recent example: Dehnen et al. 2023; Lucey et al. 2023; Petersen et al. 2024; Iles et al. 2025), let alone to identify whether an in- dividual star is a member of the bar or just passing through. For example,Fourieranalysis(e.g.Elmegreen&Elmegreen1985;Ohta et al. 1990; Aguerri et al. 1998; Garcia-Gómez et al. 2017; Pettitt & Wadsley 2018; Frosst et al. 2025) has long been used to identify bars, particularly in simulations, while observational studies have traditionally relied on isophotal ellipse fitting (e.g. Abraham et al. 1999;Laineetal.2002;Erwin2005;Gadotti&deSouza2006;Mari- nova & Jogee 2007; Menendez-Delmestre",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_005,5,"al. 2017; Pettitt & Wadsley 2018; Frosst et al. 2025) has long been used to identify bars, particularly in simulations, while observational studies have traditionally relied on isophotal ellipse fitting (e.g. Abraham et al. 1999;Laineetal.2002;Erwin2005;Gadotti&deSouza2006;Mari- nova & Jogee 2007; Menendez-Delmestre et al. 2007; Consolandi 2016; Jiang et al. 2018) or photometric decomposition (e.g. Reese et al. 2007; Gadotti 2008; Durbala et al. 2008, 2009; Weinzirl et al. 2009; Kruk et al. 2018; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2025). Visual clas- sification (e.g. Nair & Abraham 2010; Hoyle et al. 2011; Masters etal.2011,2012;Melvinetal.2014;Kruketal.2018;Mastersetal. 2021; Géron et al. 2021) remains common, as bars are inherently visual features, but this is increasingly impractical for large surveys and is inherently subject to significant person-to-person variability (e.g. Lahav et al. 1995; Heidl et al. 2013; Qian et al. 2022; Iles et al. 2025). Crowdsourcing (e.g. Lahav et al. 1995; Fortson et al. ©2026 The AuthorsarXiv:2603.24952v1 [astro-ph.GA] 26 Mar 2026 2E.J. Iles et al. 2018; Foster et al. 2025) can reduce this variation but can be simi- larlyinefficientandsubjectiveifnotappliedcarefully.Morerecently, automated bar-detection methods using non-parametric deep learn- ing, via classification,",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_006,6,"al. 1995; Fortson et al. ©2026 The AuthorsarXiv:2603.24952v1 [astro-ph.GA] 26 Mar 2026 2E.J. Iles et al. 2018; Foster et al. 2025) can reduce this variation but can be simi- larlyinefficientandsubjectiveifnotappliedcarefully.Morerecently, automated bar-detection methods using non-parametric deep learn- ing, via classification, regression, and segmentation (e.g. Abraham et al. 2018; Cavanagh & Bekki 2020; Fluke & Jacobs 2020; Ca- vanagh et al. 2022; Huertas-Company & Lanusse 2023; Cavanagh et al. 2024), have become increasingly prevalent. However, since there are so many feasible methods to identify the bar in galaxies, it is necessary to select a ‘best’ method for each study. This presents a fundamental problem: do any of these methods consistently recover the same structure across all barred galaxies? The definition of a bar, or barred region, is fundamental to many studies of host-galaxy properties, such as gas flow or star formation along the bar (e.g. Downes et al. 1996; Sheth et al. 2002; Momose etal.2010;Casasolaetal.2011;Watanabeetal.2019;Querejetaetal. 2021;Ilesetal.2022),aswellascorrelationsbetweenbarlengthand large-scale galactic properties, such as longer bars in more massive galaxies(e.g.Kormendy1979;Erwin2005;Díaz-Garcíaetal.2016), or shorter bars in late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985;Combes&Elmegreen1993;Erwin2005;Aguerrietal.2009; Erwin2019)andwhetherthismayberedshiftdependent(e.g.Sheth etal.2008;Kimetal.2021).Ifthedefinitionofthebardiffersbetween methods,thephysicaltrendsweobservemaybesignificantlyeffected",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_007,7,"along the bar (e.g. Downes et al. 1996; Sheth et al. 2002; Momose etal.2010;Casasolaetal.2011;Watanabeetal.2019;Querejetaetal. 2021;Ilesetal.2022),aswellascorrelationsbetweenbarlengthand large-scale galactic properties, such as longer bars in more massive galaxies(e.g.Kormendy1979;Erwin2005;Díaz-Garcíaetal.2016), or shorter bars in late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985;Combes&Elmegreen1993;Erwin2005;Aguerrietal.2009; Erwin2019)andwhetherthismayberedshiftdependent(e.g.Sheth etal.2008;Kimetal.2021).Ifthedefinitionofthebardiffersbetween methods,thephysicaltrendsweobservemaybesignificantlyeffected by this systematic bias. So, despite our ability to define the bar in manyways,definingthebarinawaythatisdistinct,repeatable,and applicableacrossbarredgalaxiesmuststillbeapriorityforthefield. It is only in this way that we can most effectively approach a unified understandingofbarstructure,galaxyformation,andtheroleofbars in driving galaxy evolution.",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,97,74,False,False,560,634
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_008,8,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.2 Dynamical Actions We propose a different approach to classifying the bar in galaxies, particularly aimed at identifying bars in simulations with high res- olution capturing complex or non-standard systems, such as double bars, nuclear disks, or bars distorted by interactions, with the added capacity to track time evolution on galactic scales. Further, this pro- cess is designed be able to determine, not only the spatial extent of the bar but also, the membership of individual stars to the bar, dis- tinguishing stars on bar-supporting orbits, or orbits affected by the bar, from those merely passing through. This capability will enable theassessmentofimportantbar-drivenprocesses,includingstarfor- mation, radial migration and chemical mixing, on a more precise, star-by-star basis, at least in simulations. Thebasisofthismethodistoexploittheintrinsicpropertiesofthe angle–actionvariables(𝜃 𝑖,𝐽𝑖)indifferentregionsofthegalacticpo- tential. Action-space, described by these angle-action variables, has long been used to probe many properties of the Milky Way through Galacticdynamics(e.g.Lynden-Bell&Kalnajs1972;Kalnajs1977; Arnold 1978; Martinet et al. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein- berg2001;Sellwood2012).Thisisprimarilyduetotheconvenience of the action integrals, as a set of adiabatic invariant quantities for any quasi-periodic orbit in a",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_009,9,"used to probe many properties of the Milky Way through Galacticdynamics(e.g.Lynden-Bell&Kalnajs1972;Kalnajs1977; Arnold 1978; Martinet et al. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein- berg2001;Sellwood2012).Thisisprimarilyduetotheconvenience of the action integrals, as a set of adiabatic invariant quantities for any quasi-periodic orbit in a steady, or slowly varying, potential (see Binney & Tremaine 2008, for a more specific detail). Actions have been instrumental in modelling phase-mixed populations (e.g. Tremaine 1999; Antoja et al. 2018; Ting & Rix 2019; Monari et al. 2019;Drimmeletal.2023),identifyingstellarstreams(e.g.Helmi& White1999;Eyre&Binney2011;Sanders&Binney2013),andbet- ter constraining the dark matter distribution within the Galaxy (e.g. Machado & Manos 2016; Malhan et al. 2022; Carlberg et al. 2024). Most importantly, the more abstract action-angle co-ordinates can be easily related to ordinary phase-space variables(𝑥,𝑣)(e.g. Bin-ney & Tremaine 2008; Binney 2020), either directly from the stellar orbits or via the instantaneous position and velocity measurements withanapproximationofalocalStäckelpotential(e.g.Binney2012; Sanders & Binney 2015), and algorithms that compute actions are readily available, relatively widespread and mostly computationally inexpensive,practicalevenformillion-particle𝑁−bodysimulations orextremelylargeobservationaldatasets(e.g.Debattistaetal.2020; Mikkola et al. 2020; Zozulia et al. 2024). The most useful invariant property of the action, however, relies primarily on the assumption that the",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_010,10,"2015), and algorithms that compute actions are readily available, relatively widespread and mostly computationally inexpensive,practicalevenformillion-particle𝑁−bodysimulations orextremelylargeobservationaldatasets(e.g.Debattistaetal.2020; Mikkola et al. 2020; Zozulia et al. 2024). The most useful invariant property of the action, however, relies primarily on the assumption that the potential is axisymmetric and invariant. Actions cease to be conserved in non-axisymmetric envi- ronments,suchasbarsandspiralarms(e.g.Binney&Tremaine2008; Solway et al. 2012; Vera-Ciro & D’Onghia 2016); resonances, such asco-rotation(CR;e.g.Sellwood&Binney2002;Roškaretal.2012; Daniel & Wyse 2015) and the inner- and outer-Lindblad resonances (ILR/OLR; e.g. Barbanis & Woltjer 1967; Lynden-Bell & Kalnajs 1972; Carlberg & Sellwood 1985), can significantly alter individual actions; vertical action may be conserved during radial migration in general, but not necessarily on a star-by-star basis (e.g. Solway etal.2012;Vera-Ciro&D’Onghia2016;Mikkolaetal.2020);spiral perturbations can change azimuthal action while leaving radial ac- tion nearly unchanged (e.g. Sellwood & Binney 2002; Roškar et al. 2012); and rapid potential evolution, such as during bar formation andbuckling(e.g.Toomre1981;Sellwood1981;Thielheim&Wolff 1982; Raha et al. 1991), can violate adiabatic invariance entirely. Assumptionsandapproximationshavesoughttomitigatetheeffects of this variance to probe galactic history (McMillan 2011; Binney 2012;Sanders&Binney2015;Antojaetal.2018;Ting&Rix2019; Malhan et al. 2022; Carlberg et al. 2024), while recent studies have startedtoexplorehowresonancesandtheactiondistributionofstars",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_011,11,"as during bar formation andbuckling(e.g.Toomre1981;Sellwood1981;Thielheim&Wolff 1982; Raha et al. 1991), can violate adiabatic invariance entirely. Assumptionsandapproximationshavesoughttomitigatetheeffects of this variance to probe galactic history (McMillan 2011; Binney 2012;Sanders&Binney2015;Antojaetal.2018;Ting&Rix2019; Malhan et al. 2022; Carlberg et al. 2024), while recent studies have startedtoexplorehowresonancesandtheactiondistributionofstars are related to bars, spirals, and boxy/peanut bulges (e.g. Debattista etal.2020;Kawataetal.2021;Tricketal.2021;Trick2022;Drim- mel et al. 2023; Zozulia et al. 2024; Arunima et al. 2025). In this work, we are only concerned with exploiting the existence of these changes to the action, rather than reducing or categorising their ef- fects.Thepremisehereis:ifactionsaresystematicallynotconserved for bar-located stars but remain broadly conserved elsewhere in the disc,thenthenon-conservationitselfbecomesadiagnostic.Thiswill allowustodirectlyinferbarmembershipandidentifybarproperties through a simple assessment of the conservation of action for stars throughout the galactic potential. Inthiswork,wedemonstratethismethodinthecontextofnumer- ical𝑁−body simulations, making the best use of simulated data to tracktime-evolution.Inacompanionpaper(seealsoIlesetal.2026) we demonstrate the application of this method for the Milky Way bar,usingobservationsofstarsinlargesurveys.Adescriptionofthe method and test simulation is included in Section 2. The definition of a standard bar region (e.g. radial bar extent and pitch-angle) is includedinSection3,whileSection4outlinesthe‘star-by-star’use- case, identifying potential bar membership for each stellar particle inthesimulation.Finally,ourconclusionsandrecommendationsfor future applications of this method are included",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_012,12,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicality of the proposed method using 𝑁−body SPH (smoothed particle hydrodynamics) in the form of an isolated disk galaxy simulation (IsoB) from Iles et al. (2022). To test bar-finding on a star-by-star basis, simulations with high tem- poral and spatial resolution were necessary to be able to trace bar",2.1 The Test Simulations,2.1 The Test Simulations,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,85,65,False,False,0,65
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_013,13,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026) Action Bar Classification3 evolutionandstellarorbits.TheIsoBsimulationhasastellarparticle mass resolution of1044M ⊙for simulation-formed stars, a stellar softening length of 50pc and a snapshot for every 10Myr of galaxy evolution, which is approximately equivalent to the star formation timescale (𝑡 SF=10Myr). This disk was evolved withgasoline2 (Wadsley et al. 2017), comprising a dark matter component, a small stellarbulge,aninitialstellardiskandagasdiskcomponentofparti- clesrespectively,followingthestandardsub-gridprescriptions(Katz et al. 1996; Stinson et al. 2006); with super-bubble feedback (Keller et al. 2014), UV and photoelectric heating, as well as metal cooling in the form of a tabulated cooling function (Shen et al. 2010), re- covering the two-phase ISM (Wolfire et al. 2003) from an initially isothermal (104K) gas profile. Star formation was prescribed by a standard temperature (300K) and density (100atoms/cc) threshold, aswellasaconvergentflowrequirementwithanefficiencyof∼10% andaChabrier(2003)initialmassfunction(IMF).Theinitialcondi- tionwasoriginallytailoredtoobservationsofnearby,resolvedgalaxy ngc4303via the rotation curve, gas and stellar mass fractions (see Iles et al. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved for a relatively short period of1Gyr, however, this is sufficient to cover a period pre-bar formation, the initial bar formation, and a short pe- riod post-bar","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,0,180
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_014,14,"mass fractions (see Iles et al. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved for a relatively short period of1Gyr, however, this is sufficient to cover a period pre-bar formation, the initial bar formation, and a short pe- riod post-bar formation to capture action properties in each of these regimes.LatersnapshotsoftheIsoBdiskcanbeconsideredaclassic example of a barred galaxy, however, automated bar identification methodsdonotconsistentlyproducerealisticbarparametersforthis disk in every snapshot. Thus, we consider this simulation a good elementary demonstration of the proposed method, as there are suf- ficient snapshots with automated bar classification for comparison but automation can struggle with this galaxy. Additionally, we have recentlycompletedvisualclassificationforeachsnapshotoftheIsoB simulation for a study into visual bar classification (Iles et al. 2025) which, despite some limitations, can also be used as a comparison for the bar parameters of this disk as measured ‘by-eye’.","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,173,133,True,False,140,273
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_015,15,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 2.2 Calculating the Action of Star Particles Initially, we calculated actions for the simulated star particles using thegalpypythonpackageforgalacticdynamicswithaninbuiltinter- polation routine to determine an axisymmetrised potential extracted from the𝑁−body particles in the simulation (Bovy 2015). Using galpy,theactionsarecomputedundertheStäckelapproximationof Binney(2012),producingvaluesforeachstarparticleincomponent form:theradialaction(𝐽 𝑅)broadlydescribingtheeccentricityofthe particle’sorbit;theverticalaction(𝐽 𝑧)describingthedeviationaway fromthedisk-plane;andtheazimuthalaction(𝐽 𝜙),alsoknownasthe z-componentoftheangularmomentum(𝐿 𝑧)andrelatedtotheguid- ingradiusoftheparticle’sorbit.Wethencombinetheseaction-angle dependent values into an action-space (J) distance metric, 𝐽tot=|J|=√︃ 𝐽2",2.2 Calculating the Action of Star Particles,2.2 Calculating the Action of Star Particles,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,85,65,True,True,0,65
