astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_035,35,"a visual representation of the variance between𝑅 barvaluesfromoptionsA,B,CandDforeachtimeperiod andthe𝑦−axisislog_10(variance).Visually,thereisevidentlysome difference between different potentials, different action calculation methods and very slightly between young and old stellar popula- tions in the simulation. However, the variance between these results is low (≲0.1kpc), particularly in the period we care most about (≲0.01kpc,𝑡≳300Myr) when we are likely tracing a bar feature. We can be confident that regardless of the potential chosen (even a standard form of the potential derived from MW data and scaled to match our simulation), this method is able to recover similar spatial parameters for the bar in this galaxy. Finally, we note that this method was also tested on the tidally- driven disk (TideB) from Iles et al. (2022), which is undergoing an 𝑆=10%interactionwithasmallstellarcompanionsimilartoadwarf galaxy.Thisinteractionperturbsmanypropertiesandfeaturesinthe main disk while it also promotes the formation of the bar. If dealing withnon-isolatedgalaxyconditions,itisimportanttoensurethatthe main disk is properly centred before applying any method of action calculation. If the disk is incorrectly centred, the implementation of the potential and the subsequent actions returned, including the conservationoftheseactions,willbeaffectedandactionconservation cannotbeusedtoidentifyspecificfeatures.However,ifthemaindisk canbeproperlylocatedinthepotential,regardlessofanyinteracting companions,wefindnoreasonthatthismethodnotbeapplicableto galaxies in complex interacting systems. More care must","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
