astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_055,55,"= Ω ϕ(z= 0)) provides a useful assessment of the dominance of the disk component in a galactic potential. It is identically unity in perfectly spherical systems, and increases with the oblateness of the system. The left panel of Fig. 7 showsν/Ω for circular orbits in both our N-body model and the MW. The fact that this ratio is highest towards the center of the disk and decreases to almost unity in the outskirts is already an indication of the decreasing importance of disk potential with increasingJ ϕ. We can assess the situation more carefully by compar- ing the vertical acceleration due to all the disk particles in our simulation (the “total” acceleration,a total) to the acceleration from nearby stars alone (the “local” accel- eration,a local). The total acceleration is calculated di- rectly from all the particles in the simulation using the pytreegravpackage (Grudi´ c & Gurvich 2021) for de- termining gravitational potentials and accelerations of N-body simulations. The scale of the local acceleration perpendicular to thez= 0 plane is estimated by 2πGΣ 0, appealing to the acceleration due to",IN THE N-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION,IN THE N-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,560,740
