astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_000,0,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc front_matter Astronomy&Astrophysicsmanuscript no. main©ESO 2026 March 20, 2026 Kinematic Diagnostics for Non-Axisymmetry in the Milky Way’s Nuclear Stellar Disc K. Fiteni1,2, X. Li1, M. C. Sormani1, V . P. Debattista3, A. Vasini1, F. Nogueras-Lara4, J. L. Sanders5, N. Deg6, M. Schultheis7, M. Donati1, and Z.-X. Feng1 1Como Lake centre for AstroPhysics (CLAP), DiSAT, Università dell’Insubria, Via Valleggio 11, 22100 Como, Italy e-mail:karlfiteni@gmail.com 2Institute of Space Sciences & Astronomy, University of Malta, Msida MSD 2080, Malta 3Jeremiah Horrocks Institute, University of Lancashire, Preston PR1 2HE, UK 4Instituto de Astrofísica de Andalucía (CSIC), Glorieta de la Astronomía s/n, E-18008 Granada, Spain 5Department of Physics and Astronomy, University College London, London WC1E 6BT, UK 6Department of Physics, Engineering Physics, and Astronomy, Queen’s University, Kingston, ON, K7L 3N6, Canada 7Université Côte d’Azur, Observatoire de la Côte d’Azur, Laboratoire Lagrange, CNRS, Blvd de l’Observatoire, 06304 Nice, France Received December 22, 2025; accepted March 18, 2026",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,210,161,False,False,0,161
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_001,1,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc ABSTRACT There is now strong evidence that the Milky Way (MW) hosts a nuclear stellar disc (NSD). However, whether the NSD is purely axisymmetric or contains a nuclear bar remains unresolved. Since approximately 50% of barred galaxies with MW-like mass in the local Universe host a nuclear bar, investigating whether the MW hosts one is of interest. We conduct a systematic analysis to identify robust kinematic diagnostics capable of determining whether the MW hosts a nuclear bar. Using N-body simulations, we explore the kinematic signatures indicative of a nuclear bar. Using the phase-space coordinates longitude (ℓ), latitude (b), proper motions (µℓandµ b) and line-of-sight velocity (v los), we test various diagnostics assuming different nuclear bar orientations. We also evaluate how sample size, dust extinction and bar amplitude influence the efficacy of the diagnostics. We identify two independent kinematic diagnostics capable of revealing a nuclear bar in the MW: (1) the vertex deviation,l v, of the (v ℓ−v los) velocity ellipse; and (2) The asymmetry in theµ ℓvsℓdistribution. While both",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_002,2,"efficacy of the diagnostics. We identify two independent kinematic diagnostics capable of revealing a nuclear bar in the MW: (1) the vertex deviation,l v, of the (v ℓ−v los) velocity ellipse; and (2) The asymmetry in theµ ℓvsℓdistribution. While both are impacted by the sample size and extinction, the vertex deviation proves more robust, especially when combining stars from multiple observational fields. We also assess the correlation between the line-of-sight velocity and theh 3Gauss-Hermite moment (""skewness"") of the line-of-sight velocity but find no clear distinction between an NSD and a nuclear bar based on this metric. Our results suggest that data from the current KMOS survey may allow a marginal detection of a nuclear bar using the vertex deviation method. A companion paper provides further validation and detailed analysis of this approach. Nonetheless, future surveys will provide the high quality data necessary to fully exploit the diagnostics outlined in this study. Key words.Galaxy: structure – Galaxy: center – Galaxy: kinematics and dynamics 1. Introduction The central few hundred parsecs of the Milky Way (MW) con- tain a nuclear stellar disc",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_003,3,"data necessary to fully exploit the diagnostics outlined in this study. Key words.Galaxy: structure – Galaxy: center – Galaxy: kinematics and dynamics 1. Introduction The central few hundred parsecs of the Milky Way (MW) con- tain a nuclear stellar disc (NSD): a dense, rapidly rotating stellar structure that dominates the gravitational potential at Galacto- centric radii 30<R<300 pc (Schultheis et al. 2025). The NSD has a radial scale length ofR≃90 pc, a vertical scale-height of H≃30-45 pc and a stellar mass ofM NSD=10.5+1.1",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,108,83,True,True,280,363
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_004,4,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc −1.0×108M⊙ (Launhardt et al. 2002; Nishiyama et al. 2013; Schödel et al. 2014; Gallego-Cano et al. 2020; Sormani et al. 2022). Whether the MW’s NSD is purely axisymmetric remains an open question. Indeed, while a fair fraction of the current liter- ature has treated the NSD as an axisymmetric structure (e.g., Nogueras-Lara 2022), some observational studies have previ- ously suggested the existence of a nuclear bar in the MW: Alard (2001) used H- and K-band star counts from 2MASS to derive the projected stellar surface density in the bulge region. They reported a positive/negative longitude asymmetry which they in- terpreted as a signature of a nuclear bar. Rodriguez-Fernandez & Combes (2008) derived the mass distribution in the Galac- tic Centre by fitting the 2MASS star count map to a model in- cluding a disk, bulge and nuclear bar. Using this inferred grav- itational potential, they performed hydrodynamical simulationsto model the behaviour of the interstellar gas. The resulting gas flow patterns reproduced a characteristic parallelogram feature observed in the longitude-velocity",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_005,5,"model in- cluding a disk, bulge and nuclear bar. Using this inferred grav- itational potential, they performed hydrodynamical simulationsto model the behaviour of the interstellar gas. The resulting gas flow patterns reproduced a characteristic parallelogram feature observed in the longitude-velocity diagram of molecular spec- tral lines, which they interpreted as evidence of a nuclear bar whose major axis is oriented at an angle ofα≈60◦-75◦relative to the Sun-Galactic Centre line. Nishiyama et al. (2005) used red clump (RC) stars at a latitude ofb= +1◦to trace the main bar orientation as a function of longitude (−10.5◦< ℓ <10.5◦). They reported a change in the inclination angle for the Galactic bar at fields with|ℓ|<4◦(corresponding to a nuclear bar radius R∼570 pc), which they interpreted as the effect of a distinct inner bar structure. Gonzalez et al. (2011) later confirmed this result using RCs from the VISTA Variables in the Via Lactea (VVV) survey to trace the orientation of the large-scale bar at latitudesb=±1◦. While the above results seem compelling, Gerhard & Martinez-Valpuesta (2012) employed N-body simu- lations to show that these observed asymmetries",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_006,6,"from the VISTA Variables in the Via Lactea (VVV) survey to trace the orientation of the large-scale bar at latitudesb=±1◦. While the above results seem compelling, Gerhard & Martinez-Valpuesta (2012) employed N-body simu- lations to show that these observed asymmetries and the change in the slope of the star count profiles can be explained without in- voking a nuclear bar. Instead, they demonstrate that the observed features arise naturally from the intrinsic density distribution of a single, barred, boxy bulge, which transitions from elongated to more axisymmetric within the inner few degrees. This transi- Article number, page 1 of 16arXiv:2603.18738v1 [astro-ph.GA] 19 Mar 2026 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main Fig. 1: The face-on (bottom) and side-on (top) stellar density distribution of the models. Left to right: the axisymmetric NSD, isolated strong NSB, isolated weak NSB, and large-scale bar. The large-scale bar model is oriented at−27◦relative to the Solar-GC line (dotted line). The nuclear bar angle,α, is a free parameter. In all cases, the Solar position is (x,y,z)=(−8.2,0,0.012) kpc. tion causes an apparent flattening of the longitude profiles near |ℓ|≈4◦, similar to",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_007,7,"model is oriented at−27◦relative to the Solar-GC line (dotted line). The nuclear bar angle,α, is a free parameter. In all cases, the Solar position is (x,y,z)=(−8.2,0,0.012) kpc. tion causes an apparent flattening of the longitude profiles near |ℓ|≈4◦, similar to the one observed by Nishiyama et al. (2005) and Gonzalez et al. (2011), mimicking the expected signature of a nuclear bar. A key challenge in determining whether a nuclear bar is present in the MW stems from our edge-on perspective of the Galactic Centre, which is heavily obscured by non-uniformly distributed dust (e.g. Bally et al. 1988; Gonzalez et al. 2012; Schultheis et al. 2014; Nogueras-Lara et al. 2021; Henshaw et al. 2023). Notably, most of the gas/dust (∼3/4) within|ℓ|<3.5◦ resides at positive longitudes (e.g., Bally et al. 1988; Henshaw et al. 2023), which complicates the use of star counts and photo- metric methods by producing an apparent deficit of stars at pos- itive longitudes. This introduces significant biases in the results challenging the reliability of photometric analyses. An alternative approach to detecting a nuclear bar involves the use of",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_008,8,"and photo- metric methods by producing an apparent deficit of stars at pos- itive longitudes. This introduces significant biases in the results challenging the reliability of photometric analyses. An alternative approach to detecting a nuclear bar involves the use of stellar kinematics. Sormani et al. (2022) developed a dynamical equilibrium model fitted to 1D kinematic data from the KMOS NSD survey (Fritz et al. 2021). Although their results indicate that the model fits are consistent with an axisymmetric system, it remains uncertain whether the 1D velocity histograms employed in their analysis are adequate to uncover evidence of non-axisymmetry. Nonetheless, nuclear bars are relatively com- mon features in MW–like galaxies, with roughly 50% of barred galaxies with stellar masses above log(M ⋆/M⊙)>10.5 being double-barred (Erwin 2024). Therefore, the possibility that our Galaxy hosts a nuclear bar warrants careful consideration. Despite the current small sample sizes of existing 6D kine- matic datasets, the anticipated availability of large and high- quality datasets from upcoming surveys such as the KMOS VVVX-GalCen1(Nogueras-Lara et al. in prep), the Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey withRoman(Terry et al.",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,560,740
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_009,9,"current small sample sizes of existing 6D kine- matic datasets, the anticipated availability of large and high- quality datasets from upcoming surveys such as the KMOS VVVX-GalCen1(Nogueras-Lara et al. in prep), the Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey withRoman(Terry et al. 2023), MOONS (Gonzalez et al. 2020) and JASMINE (Kawata et al. 2024) will enable the dissection of the NSD region in unprece- dented detail, allowing for new insights into the formation and evolution of the Galactic Centre. The goal of this paper is to make testable kinematic predictions that can confirm or rule out the presence of a nuclear bar in the MW with future surveys. 1https://www.eso.org/sci/observing/PublicSurveys/ KMOS-surveys-projects.htmlThis paper is organized as follows: Section 2 describes the models employed in this study. Section 3 and Section 4 present the two main kinematic diagnostics for detecting a nuclear bar2: the vertex deviation and the asymmetry observed in theµ ℓversus Galactic longitude,ℓ, distribution. Section 5 provides a discus- sion of the results and their implications. Lastly, Section 6 briefly outlines prospects for future work. 2. The models We consider three models of",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,700,880
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_010,10,"deviation and the asymmetry observed in theµ ℓversus Galactic longitude,ℓ, distribution. Section 5 provides a discus- sion of the results and their implications. Lastly, Section 6 briefly outlines prospects for future work. 2. The models We consider three models of nuclear structure: – NSD: an axisymmetric nuclear stellar disc (Sect. 2.1) – W-NSB: a weak nuclear bar (Sect. 2.2) – S-NSB: a strong nuclear bar (Sect. 2.2) In addition, we employ a large-scale MW model that includes the outer bar (Sect. 2.3). We construct the full models by em- bedding the nuclear components at the centre of the large-scale MW model, which includes an outer bar but no nuclear structure. Note that for the rest of the paper, when referring to the S-NSB and NSD models, these are always embedded in the large-scale bar. Otherwise, we refer to the ""isolated"" NSD or NSB models. Fig. 1 shows edge-on (top) and face-on (bottom) views of the models. Fig. 2 shows the surface density in the plane of the sky of the large-scale MW model without a nuclear component (left), the surface",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_011,11,"NSD or NSB models. Fig. 1 shows edge-on (top) and face-on (bottom) views of the models. Fig. 2 shows the surface density in the plane of the sky of the large-scale MW model without a nuclear component (left), the surface density of the isolated NSD model (center), and the ratio between the two (right). To create the density panels, we rescale the large-scale bar model (see Sect. 2.3 for details) to achieve an NSD/bar surface density ratio consistent with Sor- mani et al. (2022). The combined models are then constructed by randomly sampling stars from each component (nuclear and large-scale bar), with the relative sampling probability at each position (ℓ,b) determined by the surface density ratio shown in the right panel of Fig. 2. This ensures the correct levels of con- tamination from large-scale bar stars. When sampling from the large-scale bar component, stars are drawn from the original, un- scaled model to preserve the correct kinematic properties. The large-scale MW model is described in more detail in Sect. 2.3. 2We also tested Gauss-Hermite moments as a third diagnostic method,",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,980,1160
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_012,12,"large-scale bar component, stars are drawn from the original, un- scaled model to preserve the correct kinematic properties. The large-scale MW model is described in more detail in Sect. 2.3. 2We also tested Gauss-Hermite moments as a third diagnostic method, but found it unsuitable for detecting a nuclear bar (see Appendix E). Article number, page 2 of 16 K. Fiteni et al.: Kinematic Diagnostics for Non-Axisymmetry in the Milky Way’s Nuclear Stellar Disc Fig. 2: The surface density of the large-scale MW model with no nuclear component (left), the isolated NSD (middle), and the ratio between the two (right). The ratio illustrates how prominent the MW’s NSD would be if we were to see it from the Sun (see Fig. 1). Fig. 3: Evolution of the NSD model.Left:the bar strength, measured by the FourierA 2parameter, as a function of time. A2is calculated considering all stars within the radiusR max that encloses 98% of the model’s mass.Right:the evolution of Rmaxwith time. The initially stable NSD starts forming a bar at t≈0.5 Gyr, which reaches maximum strength att≈1.7 Gyr. Black and blue",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1120,1300
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_013,13,"A2is calculated considering all stars within the radiusR max that encloses 98% of the model’s mass.Right:the evolution of Rmaxwith time. The initially stable NSD starts forming a bar at t≈0.5 Gyr, which reaches maximum strength att≈1.7 Gyr. Black and blue dashed lines indicate the times at which the S- NSB and W-NSB models are extracted, respectively. We note that vertical proper motions (µ b) could in principle be used to reduce contamination from large-scale bar stars, as these exhibit systematically higher|µ b|compared to the kinemat- ically cooler NSD (e.g., Shahzamanian et al. 2022; Nogueras- Lara 2022). However, as we demonstrate in Appendix F, apply- ing a conservative cut of|µ b|>5 mas yr−1reduces bar contam- ination by at most∼2%, which has negligible impact on our kinematic diagnostics. Lastly, although the large-scale MW model and the NSD/NSB models are each internally self-consistent, we do not evolve them together under their combined gravitational potential. Therefore, the combined models are not fully self- consistent. However, this has a negligible impact on the results given the very different spatial scales of the models. 2.1. The",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1260,1440
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_014,14,"we do not evolve them together under their combined gravitational potential. Therefore, the combined models are not fully self- consistent. However, this has a negligible impact on the results given the very different spatial scales of the models. 2.1. The axisymmetric NSD model We adopt the axisymmetric, self-consistent dynamical model of the NSD from Sormani et al. (2022)3. This model was fitted to 3https://github.com/GalacticDynamics-Oxford/Agama/ blob/master/py/example_mw_nsd.pythe line-of-sight and proper motion kinematic distributions ob- tained by combining the KMOS infrared (K-band) spectroscopic survey of Fritz et al. (2021) with a preliminary version of the VIRAC2 proper motion catalogue (Smith et al. 2025). The final fiducial NSD model has a total mass ofM NSD=9.7×108M⊙, an exponential radial scale length ofR disc=74 pc and a vertical scale length ofH disc=26 pc. 2.2. The strong and weak NSB models To obtain isolated nuclear bar models, we evolve the isolated NSD model of Sormani et al. (2022) within the self-consistent MW model of Binney & Vasiliev (2023)4using the N-body code GADGET-4 (Springel et al. 2021). We use softening lengths of 5, 50, and 25 pc for",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,1400,1580
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_015,15,"we evolve the isolated NSD model of Sormani et al. (2022) within the self-consistent MW model of Binney & Vasiliev (2023)4using the N-body code GADGET-4 (Springel et al. 2021). We use softening lengths of 5, 50, and 25 pc for the NSD, dark matter, and main stellar disc components, respectively. We find that the initially axisymmet- ric NSD is mildly unstable, and starts forming a nuclear bar after evolving for 0.6 Gyr. The left panel of Fig. 3, illustrates the evo- lution of theA 2bar amplitude, computed via the usualm=2 Fourier moment (e.g., Li et al. 2024), alongside the radius con- taining 98% of the NSD’s mass (right panel). The output cadence is sufficiently fine that theA 2profile is well-sampled across the full evolution; a running average is applied in Fig. 3 to reduce shot noise between successive outputs. The bar amplitude peaks att=1.7 Gyr, after which it begins to weaken. We extract two snapshots from this simulation to construct our barred NSD models: the strong NSB (S-NSB) att=1.70 Gyr (black dashed line) when the bar is at peak strength,",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,1540,1720
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_016,16,"bar amplitude peaks att=1.7 Gyr, after which it begins to weaken. We extract two snapshots from this simulation to construct our barred NSD models: the strong NSB (S-NSB) att=1.70 Gyr (black dashed line) when the bar is at peak strength, and the weak NSB (W- NSB) att=2.60 Gyr (blue dashed line) during the bar’s declin- ing phase. 2.3. The large-scale MW model We employ a self-consistent N-body+Smooth Particle Hydro- dynamic (SPH) model of a large-scale MW-like bar which cor- responds to the model TG07v3 in Deg et al. (2025). Here, we provide only a brief overview. The initial conditions consist of a bulgeless stellar disc with a Sérsic surface density profile (Sérsic indexn=1.75), a gas disc 4https://github.com/GalacticDynamics-Oxford/Agama/ blob/master/py/example_self_consistent_model_mw.py Article number, page 3 of 16 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main Fig. 4: Plots in Galactocentric Cartesian coordinates of the isolated NSD (left), strong NSB (middle) and weak NSB (right) models oriented at−60◦relative to the Sun-GC line. Overlaid are the velocity ellipses coloured by the vertex deviation,l v. We overlay an ellipse in each bin withN>25 stars. The dashed lines reflect a 2◦field-of-view",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1680,1860
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_017,17,"strong NSB (middle) and weak NSB (right) models oriented at−60◦relative to the Sun-GC line. Overlaid are the velocity ellipses coloured by the vertex deviation,l v. We overlay an ellipse in each bin withN>25 stars. The dashed lines reflect a 2◦field-of-view centred on the Solar position at (x,y)=(−8.2,0) kpc. The NSD shows radially oriented ellipses, while the S-NSB model ellipses show strong alignment with the bar’s major axis. The alignment is weaker for the W-NSB model. (7% of the stellar mass), and an NFW dark matter halo (Navarro et al. 1997) with massM h=1.17×1012M⊙and scale radius Rh=19 kpc. The stellar disc has a mass ofM d=5.5×1010M⊙ and scale lengthR d=0.43 kpc (for the Sérsic profile), while the gas disc has an exponential scale length ofR g=6.5 kpc. The model is evolved using Changa(Menon et al. 2015) for 10 Gyr using a base timestep of∆t=0.5 Myr, which provides sufficient temporal resolution that our results are insensitive to the choice of output cadence. Star formation occurs when gas density ex- ceeds 0.1 amu cm−3and temperature falls below 15,000 K, with a star formation",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,1820,2000
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_018,18,"base timestep of∆t=0.5 Myr, which provides sufficient temporal resolution that our results are insensitive to the choice of output cadence. Star formation occurs when gas density ex- ceeds 0.1 amu cm−3and temperature falls below 15,000 K, with a star formation efficiency of 5% per dynamical time and Super- nova feedback via the Keller et al. (2014) prescription. The resulting model develops a bar that reaches a stable con- figuration by∼3.5 Gyr and remains approximately constant thereafter. Att=3 Gyr, the bar has a semi-major radius of Rbar∼4.25 kpc and pattern speed ofΩ∼42 km s−1kpc−1, con- sistent with measurements of the MW (e.g., Wegg et al. 2015; Portail et al. 2017). The model also reproduces the characteris- tic bimodal distribution of red clump stars observed along var- ious lines of sight, mimicking the observed morphology of the MW’s boxy/peanut bulge (Gonzalez et al. 2015). We note that this model does not include a metal-poor spheroidal stellar com- ponent in the bulge; we assess the potential impact of such a population in Appendix A and find it has negligible effect on our",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,233,179,True,True,1960,2139
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_019,19,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc results To match the observed NSD/bar surface density ratio from Sormani et al. (2022), we rescale the model positions byα= 0.95 and masses byγ=0.44. This rescaling is essential to prevent the large-scale bar from dominating the surface den- sity in the Galactic centre region. To maintain consistency with the laws of motion, we also rescale time byβ=1.39 and velocities byδ=0.68. The resulting scaled pattern speed isΩ scaled≈31 km s−1kpc−1and the bar semi-major axis is Rbar,scaled≈4.03 kpc, both of which are consistent with observa- tional constraints (e.g., Minchev et al. 2007; Portail et al. 2015; Pontzen et al. 2013; Sanders et al. 2019; Bovy et al. 2019; Chiba & Schönrich 2021; Clarke & Gerhard 2022).3. Diagnostic 1: Vertex deviation Given a set of stellar velocities, the velocity-dispersion tensor can be defined as σ2 i j=⟨(v i−⟨v i⟩)(v j−⟨v j⟩)⟩ =⟨v ivj⟩−⟨v i⟩⟨vj⟩,(1) whereiandjrepresent velocity components in the adopted or- thogonal coordinate frame (Binney & Tremaine 2008). In the analysis in this section, we consider a 2D tensor whereiandj can represent",results,results,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_020,20,"defined as σ2 i j=⟨(v i−⟨v i⟩)(v j−⟨v j⟩)⟩ =⟨v ivj⟩−⟨v i⟩⟨vj⟩,(1) whereiandjrepresent velocity components in the adopted or- thogonal coordinate frame (Binney & Tremaine 2008). In the analysis in this section, we consider a 2D tensor whereiandj can represent either the line-of-sight direction or the transverse direction parallel to the Galactic plane in the direction of increas- ing longitudeℓ. We denote the velocities in these two direction asv losandvℓrespectively. The dispersion tensor is symmetric and can therefore always be diagonalised. The orthogonal eigen- vectors (corresponding to eigenvaluesσ maxandσ min) define the principal axes of the velocity ellipse, with the major axis indicat- ing the direction of maximum velocity dispersion (see Fig. B.1 and Appendix B). The orientation of the velocity ellipse in the (v los,vℓ) plane can be characterised by the vertex deviation,l v, which is given by: lv=1 2arctan2σ2 12 σ2 11−σ2 22,(2) where 1 and 2 denote thev losandvℓdirections respectively (see Appendix B). By definition,l vtakes values−90◦≤lv≤90◦. In an axisymmetric nuclear disc, sinceσ2 12≈0, the velocity ellipsoid of all stars along a given line of sight is",results,results,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_021,21,"2arctan2σ2 12 σ2 11−σ2 22,(2) where 1 and 2 denote thev losandvℓdirections respectively (see Appendix B). By definition,l vtakes values−90◦≤lv≤90◦. In an axisymmetric nuclear disc, sinceσ2 12≈0, the velocity ellipsoid of all stars along a given line of sight is expected to be approximately aligned with one of the coordinate axes (i.e., lv≈0◦orl v≈±90◦) depending on the direction of highest ve- locity dispersion. For example, ellipses on opposite sides of the x=0 line in the left panel Fig. 4 cancel out and their “average” orientation is aligned with thexandyaxis. Note that strictly speaking this is valid only for an observer at infinite distance. For an observer at finite distance, the lines-of-sight that do not pass exactly through the Galactic centre (e.g. the dashed lines in Fig. 4) are not exactly parallel to thexaxis. However, the nuclear disc is small enough that this effect is negligible. In the presence of a bar instead, stars preferentially stream along the bar’s major axis, leading to a non-zero covarianceσ2 i j Article number, page 4 of 16 K. Fiteni et al.: Kinematic Diagnostics for",results,results,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_022,22,"that this effect is negligible. In the presence of a bar instead, stars preferentially stream along the bar’s major axis, leading to a non-zero covarianceσ2 i j Article number, page 4 of 16 K. Fiteni et al.: Kinematic Diagnostics for Non-Axisymmetry in the Milky Way’s Nuclear Stellar Disc Fig. 5: Velocity ellipses for the models, with the major and minor axes shown in solid and dashed lines, respectively. We uniformly sampleN=2,400 stars from the region|ℓ|<0.9◦and|b|<0.25◦. In each panel we denote the vertex deviation (l v), the anisotropy (β), the bar angleα(except for the NSD case), and the velocity dispersions in the line of sight and longitude directions. The top left panel shows the ellipse for the large-scale MW model with no nuclear component. As expected, the direction of highest dispersion is in the line of sight direction. The bottom left shows the ellipse for the axisymmetric NSD model. The other panels show the ellipses for the S-NSB model with various nuclear bar angles. In this case, the direction of highest dispersion depends on the nuclear bar angle. and hence a",results,results,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_023,23,"shows the ellipse for the axisymmetric NSD model. The other panels show the ellipses for the S-NSB model with various nuclear bar angles. In this case, the direction of highest dispersion depends on the nuclear bar angle. and hence a non-vanishingl v(see middle and right panels of Fig. 4). The vertex deviation has been successfully employed in both numerical (e.g., Debattista et al. 2019) and observational (e.g., Simion et al. 2021; San Martin Fernandez et al. 2025) stud- ies of the MW bulge to detect and characterise the Galactic bar. The aspect ratio of the velocity ellipse is characterised by the anisotropy parameter: β=1−σ2 min σ2max,(3) which takes values 0≤β≤1. Forβ=0, the velocity distribu- tion is isotropic (circular), and becomes increasingly elongated asβ→1. One important limitation of using the vertex deviation is thatl vbecomes increasingly unstable asβ→0. In such cases, for a stellar population lacking a preferred direction along either coordinate axis (i.e.,σ min≈σ max), the vertex deviation becomes poorly defined and lacks statistical significance when assessed via bootstrapping (Eq. 4). 3.1. Error handling To quantify the uncertainties, we employ",results,results,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,560,740
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_024,24,"for a stellar population lacking a preferred direction along either coordinate axis (i.e.,σ min≈σ max), the vertex deviation becomes poorly defined and lacks statistical significance when assessed via bootstrapping (Eq. 4). 3.1. Error handling To quantify the uncertainties, we employ a bootstrap resampling method. Starting with a sample ofNstars, we generateBre- sampled datasets, each maintaining the original sample size. For each re-sampling, we compute the vertex deviationl∗ v. This pro- cess yields a distribution ofl∗ vvalues, from which the uncertainty is derived as: σlv=vut 1",results,results,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,111,85,False,True,700,785
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_025,25,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc BBX k=1(l∗ v,k−lv)2,(4) wherel vis the estimate from the original sample, andl∗ v,kare the bootstrap samples.We also assess the impact of observational uncertainties in vℓandv loson the derivedl vmeasurements. Using typical KMOS uncertainties (∼10 km s−1forv losand∼23 km s−1forvℓbased on Fritz et al. 2021 and VIRAC2), we applied a Monte Carlo ap- proach convolving model velocities with these errors. In agree- ment with San Martin Fernandez et al. (2025), we find that for a given sample size,N, these observational uncertainties are neg- ligible (σ ℓv<5◦); therefore, all uncertainties reported use boot- strapping only. 3.2. Vertex deviation in the models We explore whether the vertex deviation can serve as a diag- nostic for the presence of a nuclear bar. Specifically, we inves- tigate: (1) whether a nuclear bar produces anl vsignature which is distinct from that of an NSD, (2) the number of stars required to reliably measurel v, and (3) the impact of dust extinction on the measurement. We first consider results from the strong nu- clear bar model",BBX,BBX,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
