astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_045,45,"ofN sample . 3.6. An orthogonal nuclear disc External galaxies frequently host elliptical NSDs (e.g., Bittner et al. 2020). In barred galaxies, NSDs are predominantly sup- ported by thex 2orbital family, with the NSD’s major axis ori- ented orthogonal to the primary bar (Cole et al. 2014). Since the Sormani et al. (2022) NSD model assumes axisymmetry, it can- not capture this orthogonal configuration. We therefore adopt a self-consistent N-body+SPH model (see Appendix C for details) to investigate the kinematic signature of an elliptical, orthog- onally oriented NSD. We show a synthetic RGB rendering of the model in Fig. 13, which features a primary bar with radius R≈3 kpc and a∼1 kpc-scale NSD. While not MW-scaled, the large NSD allows us assess how an orthogonal configuration impacts thel vsignal. As before, the primary bar is oriented at−27◦to the Sun– GC line, with its near side at positive longitudes. The orthogo- Article number, page 9 of 16 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main Fig. 14: Velocity ellipses for the quasi-axisymmetric orthogonal NSD model, constructed from stars in the region|ℓ|<0.9◦and |b|<0.25◦.Left: All stars showl",BBX,BBX,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,2800,2980
