astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_047,47,"selection criterion. The resultingl vandβvalues which are con- sistent with the axisymmetric NSD case (see Fig. 5). Since the NSD in this model formed late, we re-sample the model again (withN=2,400) only considering stars with agesτ ⋆<5 Gyr. This effectively reduces contamination from the large-scale bar. For this younger population,βincreases by approximately a fac- tor of∼3. We also observe thatl vshifts away from the coordi- nate axis, towards positive values. Therefore, an orthogonally oriented NSD in the MW would produce positivel vsignal, mimicking the kinematic signature of a nuclear bar viewed nearly side-on with its semi-major axis at negative longitude (see Fig. 12). This degeneracy implies that weak positivel vin the Galactic Centre is consistent with either scenario and does not exclude the presence of a mildly elliptical",BBX,BBX,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,167,128,False,True,3080,3208
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_048,48,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc NSD 4. Diagnostic 2: Asymmetry in theµ ℓ-ℓplane The (µℓ,ℓ) plane provides a potential diagnostic for identifying an edge-on nuclear bar in the Galactic Centre. In an axisymmet- ric disc, the distribution of stars in the (µ ℓ,ℓ) plane is symmetric about theℓ=0◦line. Conversely, the presence of a nuclear bar can induce, depending on the orientation of the major axis, a measurable deviation from this symmetry. To capture these asymmetries, we first bin stars by their Galactic longitudeℓ. Within each bin, a 1D histogram ofµ ℓis constructed and smoothed using Gaussian kernel density esti- mation (KDE). We identify the peak of each histogram, denoted asµℓ,max. Then we plot the relationship betweenµ ℓ,max andℓ. Fig. 15 showsµ ℓ,max versusℓfor the S-NSB model (hollow red markers) with various nuclear bar angles,α. A smoothed fit to these points is shown by the red line. Blue markers and lines depict the corresponding distributions for the axisymmetric NSD case. The leftmost panels show the cases forα=0◦(top) and α=90◦(bottom), where theµ ℓ,max−ℓrelationship is roughly consistent",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_049,49,"A smoothed fit to these points is shown by the red line. Blue markers and lines depict the corresponding distributions for the axisymmetric NSD case. The leftmost panels show the cases forα=0◦(top) and α=90◦(bottom), where theµ ℓ,max−ℓrelationship is roughly consistent with that of the axisymmetric NSD. In contrast, forsmallerα(second column), the distribution shows a ""step"" fea- ture inµ ℓ,max which is not visible in the NSD case. Asαincreases (third/fourth columns) the prominence of the step features de- creases quickly, eventually becoming flat. The black open circles in Fig. 15 show theµ l,maxdistribution for stars sampled from individual fields in the midplane in order to emulate observations. In each field, we sampleN=1500 stars from the S-NSB model, with uncertainties estimated via a boot- strapping approach. For each bin inℓ, we perform resampling of the stars with replacement for 1000 iterations and the uncertainty determined via Eq. 4. In cases whereαis small (e.g.,α≲20◦), the step feature in the distribution is observable. However, asαbecomes larger, it is unlikely that this method will reveal the presence of a nuclear bar. In addition, we note",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_050,50,"the uncertainty determined via Eq. 4. In cases whereαis small (e.g.,α≲20◦), the step feature in the distribution is observable. However, asαbecomes larger, it is unlikely that this method will reveal the presence of a nuclear bar. In addition, we note that even with∼1500 sources per field the distribution can be very noisy. Nonetheless, the sample sizes available in surveys in the near future will allow this diagnostic to be employed. In Fig. 16, we test the impact of extinction on theµ l,max dis- tribution. The blue markers/curve again show the distribution for the axisymmetric NSD. We then apply the selection function de- scribed in Sec. 3.4 (see also Fig. 10) to the NSD and re-compute theµℓ,max−ℓdistribution (red markers/curve). The inset in Fig. 16 shows the density distribution of the NSD when the selection is applied. Extinction has a strong impact on theµ l,max-ℓdistri- bution, creating a strong peak which is slightly shifted towards positive longitude. If not properly accounted for, extinction can easily produce a signal mimicking that of a nuclear bar; a major drawback of this diagnostic. 5. Discussion",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_051,51,"on theµ l,max-ℓdistri- bution, creating a strong peak which is slightly shifted towards positive longitude. If not properly accounted for, extinction can easily produce a signal mimicking that of a nuclear bar; a major drawback of this diagnostic. 5. Discussion and conclusion In this study, we have employed N-body simulations to system- atically investigate kinematic diagnostics for detecting a nuclear bar in the Galactic centre. The primary objective was to develop kinematic diagnostics that are both theoretically robust and ob- servationally practical given current and future data. The models were constructed to test the difference in kinematic signatures between an axisymmetric nuclear stellar disc and a nuclear bar. We also tested the role of extinction due to the presence of dust and gas in the Galactic centre using a toy model. We assessed three methods for detecting a nuclear bar: (1) Vertex deviation of the velocity ellipse (Sec. 3), (2) the asymmetry in theµ ℓ−ℓ distribution (see Sec. 4), and (3) the correlation/anti-correlation between the Gauss-Hermite momenth 3and the line-of-sight ve- locity,V los(see Appendix E). Our study indicates that the",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_052,52,"(1) Vertex deviation of the velocity ellipse (Sec. 3), (2) the asymmetry in theµ ℓ−ℓ distribution (see Sec. 4), and (3) the correlation/anti-correlation between the Gauss-Hermite momenth 3and the line-of-sight ve- locity,V los(see Appendix E). Our study indicates that the vertex deviation,l v, provides the most robust method of detecting a nuclear bar in the Galactic centre (see Sec. 3.2). An NSD produces a velocity ellipse with its major axis aligned with the Galactic longitude velocity (i.e, lv≈±90◦), while a nuclear bar deviates from this, producing an lvvalue which depends on its angle,α. A nuclear bar with its near side at negative longitudes produces positivel v, which con- trasts sharply with the background negativel vof the large-scale bar. Conversely, a nuclear bar with its near side towards positive longitudes produces a negativel v. Importantly, contamination of roughly 20-25% from the main bar minimally affectsl vin the central region where the NSD density dominates, which spans roughly|ℓ|<0.9◦and|b|<0.25◦. However, in cases where α=±90◦, thel vsignals of an NSD and NSB are practically identical. We also explored the strong possibility that the MW’s",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,560,740
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_053,53,"main bar minimally affectsl vin the central region where the NSD density dominates, which spans roughly|ℓ|<0.9◦and|b|<0.25◦. However, in cases where α=±90◦, thel vsignals of an NSD and NSB are practically identical. We also explored the strong possibility that the MW’s NSD is not fully axisymmetric, but elliptical, with its major axis orthog- onal to the main bar, as is observed in external galaxies (e.g., Article number, page 10 of 16 K. Fiteni et al.: Kinematic Diagnostics for Non-Axisymmetry in the Milky Way’s Nuclear Stellar Disc Fig. 15: Distribution ofµ ℓ,max versusℓdistribution for the S-NSB model (red markers and smoothed fit) compared to the axisym- metric NSD (blue). Each panel shows a different nuclear bar angle,α. For small angles|α|≲20◦, the NSB produces a ""step"" feature in the central region (|ℓ|<1◦) that clearly deviates from the flat NSD distribution. This asymmetry weakens substantially as|α|≳ increases, and becomes indistinguishable from the NSD atα=0◦or±90◦, limiting this diagnostic’s applicability to a narrow range of nuclear bar orientations. Black open circles show measurements from individual midplane fields withN=1500 stars each from the S-NSB model. Uncertainties are",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,700,880
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_054,54,"substantially as|α|≳ increases, and becomes indistinguishable from the NSD atα=0◦or±90◦, limiting this diagnostic’s applicability to a narrow range of nuclear bar orientations. Black open circles show measurements from individual midplane fields withN=1500 stars each from the S-NSB model. Uncertainties are estimated via bootstrap resampling. Fig. 16: Distribution ofµ ℓ,max vsℓfor the NSD model without selection effects (blue markers and curve), and with the obser- vational selection function applied (red). The inset shows the resulting face-on density distribution after selection. The distri- bution is no longer constant, but produces a strong peak in the distribution which could be misinterpreted as evidence for a nu- clear bar if observational biases are not carefully accounted for. Bittner et al. 2020). We find that in such cases,l vis positive (see Sec. 3.6), and mimics the kinematic signature of a nuclear bar viewed nearly side-on with its semi-major axis at negative lon- gitude. Therefore, should a positivel vin the Galactic Centre be detected in future studies an NSD can not be easily ruled out. The uncertainties associated withl vare a function of sample size and",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,840,1020
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_055,55,"with its semi-major axis at negative lon- gitude. Therefore, should a positivel vin the Galactic Centre be detected in future studies an NSD can not be easily ruled out. The uncertainties associated withl vare a function of sample size and nuclear bar strength. In the weak bar case (W-NSB), stellar velocities are more isotropic (smallerβ) (see Fig. 12), which systematically increases the uncertainty. Therefore, the sample size required for a statistically significant measurement ofl vchanges with nuclear bar strength. In the strong NSB case(S-NSB), we find that roughlyN=500 is sufficient, whereas this increases toN=1,000 for a weaker NSB. In this context, one of the key advantages of using vertex deviation is the ability to combine stars from different observational fields, increasing the statistical significance of thel vmeasurement. This suggests that the data available in the current KMOS survey of the NSD (Fritz et al. 2021) may allow for the detection of a nuclear bar via the vertex deviation diagnostic. This possibility will be addressed in a companion paper. We find thatl vcould be impacted by extinction, however the effect is",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,980,1160
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_056,56,"NSD (Fritz et al. 2021) may allow for the detection of a nuclear bar via the vertex deviation diagnostic. This possibility will be addressed in a companion paper. We find thatl vcould be impacted by extinction, however the effect is not strong. We applied a three-component probabilis- tic selection function that accounts for distance-dependent de- tectability, midplane extinction and crowding, and asymmetric dust extinction as a function of Galactic longitude (Appendix D). We find that this shiftsl vtowards positive values, possibly mim- icking an NSB with its near side towards negative Galactic lon- gitudes. Similar to San Martin Fernandez et al. (2025) for a large- scale bar, we find that whilel vreveals non-axisymmetry, it should not be considered as a measure of thelevelof non- axisymmetry. Indeed, for a given nuclear bar angle,l vwill take on roughly the same value, regardless of bar strength (see Fig. 12). Instead, the anisotropy,β, of the velocity ellipsoid may be employed in order to characterise the bar amplitude, as it in- creases with bar amplitude. The observed asymmetry in theµ ℓ−ℓplane provides another method of",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,1120,1300
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_057,57,"of bar strength (see Fig. 12). Instead, the anisotropy,β, of the velocity ellipsoid may be employed in order to characterise the bar amplitude, as it in- creases with bar amplitude. The observed asymmetry in theµ ℓ−ℓplane provides another method of detecting a NSB. In Fig. 15 we show the distribution ofµ l,max versusℓfor various orientations (panels) of the NSB (red markers/curves). The NSB will induce deviations from the otherwise flat distribution produced by an axisymmetric NSD (blue points). This detection method is most effective in cases where the NSB angle is small (−20◦< α <20◦). This produces a ""step"" feature in the inner|ℓ|<1◦. For NSB angles|α|>30◦, the asymmetry (and therefore the central slope) becomes weaker, and this method becomes unreliable. In such cases, using the ver- tex deviation is preferred. Two important caveats are that unlike Article number, page 11 of 16 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main the vertex deviation, this method is highly sensitive to the asym- metric extinction in the Galactic centre (see Fig. 16). Secondly, while the bootstrapping we applied reflects howµ ℓvaries de- pending on sampling, individual",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1260,1440
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_058,58,"of 16 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main the vertex deviation, this method is highly sensitive to the asym- metric extinction in the Galactic centre (see Fig. 16). Secondly, while the bootstrapping we applied reflects howµ ℓvaries de- pending on sampling, individual sources in observational data will have their ownµ ℓuncertainties, further diluting the signal. We also tested the correlation or anti-correlation between the Gauss–Hermite momenth 3and the line-of-sight velocityV los. Although this method has proven effective for identifying NSDs in external galaxies (e.g, de Lorenzo-Cáceres et al. 2013), we find no observable distinction between the NSD and NSB in our models: both exhibit similarh 3–Vlosanti-correlation for all NSB orientations (see Appendix E). Consequently, this diagnos- tic does not provide meaningful constraints on the presence of an NSB in the MW, and we therefore include the full analysis only in the Appendix for completeness. Overall, our findings demonstrate thatl vis both robust against extinction and selection effects and sensitive across a wide range of nuclear-bar orientations. Theµ ℓ–ℓasymmetry method plays a complementary but limited role, while Gauss– Hermite diagnostics do not provide",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1400,1580
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_059,59,"for completeness. Overall, our findings demonstrate thatl vis both robust against extinction and selection effects and sensitive across a wide range of nuclear-bar orientations. Theµ ℓ–ℓasymmetry method plays a complementary but limited role, while Gauss– Hermite diagnostics do not provide discriminating power in this context. 6. Future work The number of stars required to reliably measure the vertex devi- ation is available in the KMOS survey (Fritz et al. 2021), which contains≈3000 sources. This possibility shall be explored in a companion paper. Nevertheless, a detailed picture of the NSD re- gion will likely require the larger sample sizes and precise mea- surements from future surveys such as the KMOS public survey, VVVX-GalCen (Nogueras-Lara et al. in prep). This will pro- vide temperatures, velocities, spectral indices, and metallicities for∼40 000 sources in the NSD region. The MOONS RED- dened Milky WAY (REDWAY) survey (Cirasuolo et al. 2020; Gonzalez et al. 2020), will also provide high resolution spectra for∼13,000 stars within the region|ℓ|<1◦and|b|<0.5◦, en- abling measurements of radial velocities and metallicities. Simi- larly, the JASMINE Galactic Centre survey (GCS) (Kawata et al.",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,1540,1720
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_060,60,"(REDWAY) survey (Cirasuolo et al. 2020; Gonzalez et al. 2020), will also provide high resolution spectra for∼13,000 stars within the region|ℓ|<1◦and|b|<0.5◦, en- abling measurements of radial velocities and metallicities. Simi- larly, the JASMINE Galactic Centre survey (GCS) (Kawata et al. 2024) will cover a region−0.7◦<|ℓ|<1.4◦and|b|<0.6◦, and is expected to provide accurate positions and proper mo- tion measurements for about 1.2×105stars. Lastly, theRoman Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey (Terry et al. 2023) will pro- vide high-precision photometric and astrometric measurements of∼3.3 million stars over a 0.28 deg2field centred on SgrA*, with proper motion precisions of 2.5−3.5µas/yr. These datasets will facilitate the application of the diagnostic tools discussed in this paper, advancing our understanding of the Galactic Centre. Acknowledgements.We thank the anonymous referee for providing valuable feedback which improved the manuscript. KF, MCS, ZF, XL, A V , and MD acknowledge financial support from the European Research Council under the ERC Starting Grant “GalFlow” (grant 101116226). MCS further acknowl- edges financial support from the Fondazione Cariplo under the grant ERC attrattività n. 2023-3014. JLS acknowledge support from the Royal Society (URF\R1\191555; URF\R\241030).",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,1680,1860
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_061,61,"support from the European Research Council under the ERC Starting Grant “GalFlow” (grant 101116226). MCS further acknowl- edges financial support from the Fondazione Cariplo under the grant ERC attrattività n. 2023-3014. JLS acknowledge support from the Royal Society (URF\R1\191555; URF\R\241030). The simulations in this paper were run both at the High Performance Computing Facility of the University of Lancashire and at the DiRAC Shared Memory Processing system at the University of Cambridge, operated by the COSMOS Project at the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics on behalf of the STFC DiRAC High Per- formance Computing (HPC) Facility: www.dirac.ac.uk. This equipment was funded by the Department for Business, Innovation and Skills (BIS) National E-infrastructure capital grant ST/J005673/1, STFC capital grant ST/H008586/1, and STFC DiRAC Operations grant ST/K00333X/1. DiRAC is part of the Na- tional E-Infrastructure. FNL gratefully acknowledges financial support from grant PID2024-162148NA-I00, funded by MCIN/AEI/10.13039/501100011033 and the European Regional Development Fund (ERDF) “A way of making Eu- rope”, from the Ramón y Cajal programme (RYC2023-044924-I) funded byMCIN/AEI/10.13039/501100011033 and FSE+, and from the Severo Ochoa grant CEX2021-001131-S, funded by MCIN/AEI/10.13039/501100011033.",NSD,NSD,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1820,2000
