astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_068,68,"M. C., Sanders, J. L., Fritz, T. K., et al. 2022, MNRAS, 512, 1857 Soto, M., Rich, R. M., & Kuijken, K. 2007, ApJ, 665, L31 Springel, V ., Pakmor, R., Zier, O., & Reinecke, M. 2021, MNRAS, 506, 2871 Stinson, G., Seth, A., Katz, N., et al. 2006, MNRAS, 373, 1074 Terry, S. K., Hosek, Jr., M. W., Lu, J. R., et al. 2023, arXiv e-prints, arXiv:2306.12485 van der Marel, R. P. & Franx, M. 1993, ApJ, 407, 525 Wadsley, J. W., Keller, B. W., & Quinn, T. R. 2017, MNRAS, 471, 2357 Wadsley, J. W., Stadel, J., & Quinn, T. 2004, New A, 9, 137 Wegg, C., Gerhard, O., & Portail, M. 2015, MNRAS, 450, 4050 Zakharova, D., Tikhonenko, I. S., Sotnikova, N. Y ., & Smirnov, A. A. 2024,","MNRAS, 540, 2506","MNRAS, 540, 2506",,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,172,132,False,False,140,272
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_069,69,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc MNRAS, 527, 3038 Zhao, H., Spergel, D. N., & Rich, R. M. 1994, AJ, 108, 2154 Article number, page 13 of 16 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main Appendix A: The metal-poor halo Observations reveal that while the Milky Way’s boxy-peanut bulge is predominantly traced by metal-rich stars, the more spheroidal metal-poor ([Fe/H]<−0.5) population must include a contribution of∼10% from the halo (e.g., Ness et al. 2013; Rojas-Arriagada et al. 2020). This metal-poor halo is not in- cluded in the large-scale bar simulation employed in this work and could therefore represent an additional source of contamina- tion in our analysis. To assess the potential impact of this population, we con- struct a synthetic metal-poor component by sampling velocities from Gaussian distributions with dispersionsσ r=99 km s−1and σℓ=138 km s−1(Table 1 in Babusiaux et al. (2010)), which characterizes the kinematics of metal-poor stars in the bulge. When sampling stars from our models to compute the diag- nostic metrics, we add a 10% contribution from this synthetic metal-poor population. We find that the","MNRAS, 527, 3038","MNRAS, 527, 3038",,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_070,70,"Babusiaux et al. (2010)), which characterizes the kinematics of metal-poor stars in the bulge. When sampling stars from our models to compute the diag- nostic metrics, we add a 10% contribution from this synthetic metal-poor population. We find that the resulting impact on our diagnostics is consistently within the uncertainties determined through bootstrapping (see Sec. 3.1). Given its negligible effect on our results, we exclude this component from our final mod- elling framework. Appendix B: Vertex deviation derivation In 2 dimensions, the velocity dispersion tensor defined by Eq. (1) can be written as: σi j=σ2 11σ2 12 σ2 21σ2","MNRAS, 527, 3038","MNRAS, 527, 3038",,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,129,99,False,True,140,239
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_071,71,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc 22,(B.1) where 1 refers to the first coordinate (in our case, the line- of-sight directionv los) and 2 to the second coordinate (in our case, the transverse directionv ℓ), and the tensor is symmetric, σ2 12=σ2 21. Because it is symmetric, the dispersion tensor is al- ways diagonalisable. The eigenvalues are given by: σmax/min =1 2h σ2 11+σ2 22±Di","22,(B.1)","22,(B.1)",,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,91,70,False,True,0,70
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_072,72,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc (B.4) The vertex deviation, denoted asl vand illustrated by the black arc in Fig. B.1, is defined as the angle that the eigenvector cor- responding to the largest eigenvalue (V max) makes with thev los axis. Elementary trigonometry yields: tan(2l v)=2σ2 12 σ2 11−σ2",(B.4),(B.4),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,72,55,False,True,0,55
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_073,73,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc 22 (B.6) The vertex deviationl vtakes values within the range [−90◦,90◦] and represents the angle that the semi-major axis of the velocity ellipse forms with thev losaxis as illustrated in Fig. B.1. Fig. B.1: Schematic illustration of the velocity ellipse. The two eigenvectors (Eq. B.4) of the velocity dispersion tensor (Eq. B.1) are shown as green and red arrows. The vertex devi- ation (Eq. B.6) is the angle indicated by the black arc. Note that sometimes a modified formulation is employed in the literature (e.g., Soto et al. 2007; Babusiaux et al. 2010; De- battista et al. 2019; Simion et al. 2021; San Martin Fernandez et al. 2025), which incorporates the absolute value of the de- nominator: lv=1 2arctan2σ2 12 |σ2 11−σ2",22 (B.6),22 (B.6),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,173,133,False,True,0,133
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_074,74,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc 22|.(B.7) In this case,l vtakes values in the interval [−45◦,45◦] and is the angle that the semi-major axis of the velocity ellipse makes with the nearest coordinate axis (eitherv losorvℓ). We do not use this alternative definition in this paper. Appendix C: The SPH model We employ an N-body+SPH model of an isolated disc galaxy to explore how an elliptical, orthogonal NSD impacts our re- sults. This model is equivalent to model M3_nc_b in Fiteni et al. (2021), and has also been described extensively in Cole et al. (2014) and Debattista et al. (2017). Therefore, we give here only a brief overview of the model. Initially, the model com- prises of a corona of hot gas embedded within a dark matter halo with a virial radius ofr 200≃200 kpc and a virial mass of M200=1012M⊙. Both the gas and dark matter are represented by 5×106particles each, with softening parameters ofϵ=50 pc andϵ=100 pc for the gas and dark matter respectively. All stars form from the gas, with no stars",22|.(B.7),22|.(B.7),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_075,75,"and a virial mass of M200=1012M⊙. Both the gas and dark matter are represented by 5×106particles each, with softening parameters ofϵ=50 pc andϵ=100 pc for the gas and dark matter respectively. All stars form from the gas, with no stars present att=0. The initial radial profile of the gas corona is identical to that of the dark matter halo, but contains only 10% of its mass. Gas particles are assigned a tangential velocity that results in a spin param- eterλ≡J|E|1/2/(GM5/2 vir)=0.065, whereJandEdenote the total angular momentum and the energy of the gas particles re- spectively, andGis the gravitational constant. The conditions for star-formation to occur require that the cold gas (T<15,000 K) Article number, page 14 of 16 K. Fiteni et al.: Kinematic Diagnostics for Non-Axisymmetry in the Milky Way’s Nuclear Stellar Disc density exceeds 0.1 cm−3and is part of a converging flow. Star particles forming from the cooling gas also have a softening pa- rameter ofϵ=50 pc. The star-formation efficiency is set to 5%, and we employ the feedback recipe described by Stinson et al. (2006). The supernova feedback",22|.(B.7),22|.(B.7),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_076,76,"a converging flow. Star particles forming from the cooling gas also have a softening pa- rameter ofϵ=50 pc. The star-formation efficiency is set to 5%, and we employ the feedback recipe described by Stinson et al. (2006). The supernova feedback model injects 10% of the 1051 erg per supernova back into the interstellar medium. Addition- ally, we implement turbulent diffusion for gas mixing as outlined by Shen et al. (2010). The model was evolved for 10 Gyr with gasoline(Wadsley et al. 2004, 2017), and develops a long-lived primary bar early in its evolution at roughly 3 Gyr. The orthogo- nal NSD structure forms later and is comprised mostly of young stars with agesτ ⋆<4 Gyr. Appendix D: Observational Selection Effects To generate a realistic mock observation from the models, we ap- plied a three-component probabilistic selection function that ac- counts for distance-dependent detectability, midplane extinction and crowding, and asymmetric dust extinction as a function of Galactic longitude. Each star in the model was assigned a detec- tion probability based on these effects, and the final ""observed"" sample was generated through",22|.(B.7),22|.(B.7),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_077,77,"for distance-dependent detectability, midplane extinction and crowding, and asymmetric dust extinction as a function of Galactic longitude. Each star in the model was assigned a detec- tion probability based on these effects, and the final ""observed"" sample was generated through Monte Carlo sampling. The three components of our selection function are illustrated in Fig. 10. Due to extinction effects and crowding in the midplane, stars on the far side of the disc (d>8.2 kpc) are less likely to be detected. We model this selection bias using a skewed normal distribution (Fig. 10a): P(d)=1 N·ϕskew(d;µ=8.2 kpc,σ=1 kpc,α=−1) (D.1) whereϕ skewis the skewed normal probability density function with location parameterµ=8.2 kpc, scale parameterσ=1 kpc, and skewness parameterα=−1. The negative skewness pro- duces an asymmetric distribution that favours stars on the near side of the NSD (d<8.2 kpc), mimicking the observational re- ality that nearer stars are more readily detected than those on the far side of the Galactic Centre. The distribution is normalized such that max(p dist)=1. Severe stellar crowding and extinction significantly reduce detection completeness nearb=0◦. We also model the com-",22|.(B.7),22|.(B.7),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_078,78,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc (D.2) whereb scale=0.1◦is the characteristic latitude scale. This func- tional form imposes a maximum 70% incompleteness at the Galactic plane (b=0◦), with completeness recovering exponen- tially toward higher latitudes. As visible in Fig. 10b, the detec- tion probability drops to only 30% at the midplane and recovers to nearly full completeness by|b|∼0.5◦. Observations of the inner Galaxy reveal a strong asymme- try in dust distribution, with approximately two-thirds of the ob- scuring material concentrated at positive Galactic longitudes. We model extinction using a hyperbolic tangent profile (Fig. 10c): P(ℓ)=0.5+0.25"" 1−tanh ξ·ℓ ℓscale!#",(D.2),(D.2),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,134,103,False,True,0,103
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_079,79,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc (D.3) where the extinction strength coefficient is:ξ=(3.0 forℓ>0 1.0 forℓ≤0(D.4) andℓ scale=0.5 controls the transition sharpness. This formula- tion ensures that detection probability decreases monotonically as longitude increases. The total detection probability for each star is computed as the product of the three independent components shown in Fig. 10: Ptotal(d,ℓ,b)=P(d)×P(b)×P(ℓ) (D.5) To achieve a target overall completenessC target, we normal- ize the probability distribution: pnorm(d,ℓ,b)=ptotal(d,ℓ,b) ⟨ptotal⟩×C target (D.6) where⟨p total⟩is the mean probability across all model stars. The final observed sample is generated through Bernoulli trials: each star is retained if a uniform random variateu∼U(0,1) satisfies u<p norm. For our fiducial model, we adoptedC target=0.15%, yielding a sparse sample that reflects the extreme observational challenges of detecting individual stars in the crowded, heavily extincted Galactic Centre region. The actual achieved complete- ness may differ slightly from the target due to the stochastic sam- pling process and probability clipping. Appendix E: Gauss-Hermite moments Line-of-sight velocity distributions have been widely used as kinematic diagnostics of galaxy features (e.g., Gerhard 1993; van",(D.3),(D.3),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_080,80,"achieved complete- ness may differ slightly from the target due to the stochastic sam- pling process and probability clipping. Appendix E: Gauss-Hermite moments Line-of-sight velocity distributions have been widely used as kinematic diagnostics of galaxy features (e.g., Gerhard 1993; van der Marel & Franx 1993; Chung & Bureau 2004; Bu- reau & Athanassoula 2005; Debattista et al. 2005; Iannuzzi & Athanassoula 2015; Li et al. 2018; Zakharova et al. 2024; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2025). Specifically, correlations and anti- correlations betweenh 3(""skewness"") and the mean line-of-sight velocity in edge-on discs provide insight into the underlying stel- lar orbital structure of different components. A positiveh 3−V los correlation typically indicates elongated orbits (indicative of a bar), whereas an anti-correlation suggests the presence of more circular orbits characteristic of a stellar disc (Bureau & Athanas- soula 2005; Iannuzzi & Athanassoula 2015; Li et al. 2018; Za- kharova et al. 2024). An anti-correlation is also associated with the presence of an NSD (Chung & Bureau 2004; Zakharova et al. 2024; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2025). In Fig. E.1, we plot 2D maps of the line-of-sight",(D.3),(D.3),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_081,81,"al. 2018; Za- kharova et al. 2024). An anti-correlation is also associated with the presence of an NSD (Chung & Bureau 2004; Zakharova et al. 2024; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2025). In Fig. E.1, we plot 2D maps of the line-of-sight velocity (left), theh 3Gauss-Hermite moment (middle), and the velocity dispersion (right). We show the distributions for the large-scale MW (top), NSD (middle), and S-NSB (α=90◦) models. In the S-NSB case, we tested the diagnostic for several values ofα, and found no variation among the results. We only show the results forα=90◦as a representative case. As expected, we see pos- itively correlatedh 3andV losfor the large-scale bar model (top row). Consistent with observations (e.g., Chung & Bureau 2004; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2025), the presence of the NSD (middle row) produces an anti-correlation between the mean velocity and h3. In the bottom row, we showV losandh 3for the S-NSB model. We again find that these parameters are anti-correlated, indistin- guishable from the NSD model case. This is consistent with de Lorenzo-Cáceres et al. (2013), who carried out a detailed kine- matical and",(D.3),(D.3),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_082,82,"row, we showV losandh 3for the S-NSB model. We again find that these parameters are anti-correlated, indistin- guishable from the NSD model case. This is consistent with de Lorenzo-Cáceres et al. (2013), who carried out a detailed kine- matical and stellar population analysis in a sample of four (SB0 Article number, page 15 of 16 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main Fig. E.1:Left to right: 2D maps of the line-of-sight velocity, the h3Gauss-Hermite moment, and the line-of-sight velocity disper- sion. We show the distributions for the large-scale MW (top, note different scale), NSD (middle), S-NSB (α=90◦) models. - SBb) double-barred galaxies using integral-field spectroscopy. Their Figure 4 shows the face-on 2D maps ofh 3reveal that the distribution is not dependent on the nuclear bar angle, and is thus most likely related to the NSD in which the nuclear bar is em- bedded. The velocity dispersion (right column in Fig. E.1) also shows no clear observable difference between the NSD and S- NSB cases, save for a greater central dispersion for the S-NSB. Therefore, this method is unlikely to reveal the presence of",(D.3),(D.3),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_083,83,"The velocity dispersion (right column in Fig. E.1) also shows no clear observable difference between the NSD and S- NSB cases, save for a greater central dispersion for the S-NSB. Therefore, this method is unlikely to reveal the presence of nu- clear bar in the Galactic centre. Appendix F: Vertical motion kinematics The left column of Fig. F.1 shows theµ bdistributions for stars within 7.8<d[kpc]<8.4 across various fields for the NSD, S- NSB, and large-scale bar models. The kinematically cooler NSD is characterised by low velocity dispersion, with typical values |µb|≲5 mas yr−1. The NSB shows moderately higher dispersion, reaching|µ b|≈8 mas yr−1, while a subset of large-scale bar stars extends to significantly higher|µ b|values. Applying a conserva- tive cut of|µ b|>5 mas yr−1across all fields reduces contami- nation from large-scale bar interlopers by at most∼2%, having negligible impact on our kinematic diagnostics. Nonetheless, ob- servers may wish to apply such cuts when constructing clean samples of NSD stars. The right column of Fig. F.1 shows the distance distribution of stars excluded by the|µ b|≲5 mas yr−1 cut. Fig. F.1:Left:",(D.3),(D.3),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_084,84,"our kinematic diagnostics. Nonetheless, ob- servers may wish to apply such cuts when constructing clean samples of NSD stars. The right column of Fig. F.1 shows the distance distribution of stars excluded by the|µ b|≲5 mas yr−1 cut. Fig. F.1:Left: The vertical proper motion,µ bfor several fields. We show the distribution for the isolated NSD (black), isolated S-NSB (blue) and large-scale MW (red) models for stars in the distance range 7.8<d[kpc]<8.4.Right: The distance distribu- tion for stars excluded by the vertical cut|µ b|<5 mas/yr. Article number, page 16 of 16",(D.3),(D.3),,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,117,90,False,False,700,790
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_000,0,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars front_matter MNRAS000, 1–16 (2026) Preprint 11 March 2026 Compiled using MNRAS L ATEX style file v3.3 Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars Richard A. N. Brooks1★, Jason L. Sanders1, Adam M. Dillamore1, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo2†, Vedant Chandra3, Adrian M. Price-Whelan4, Phillip Cargile3 1Department of Physics and Astronomy, University College London, London, WC1E 6BT, UK 2Department of Astronomy, University of Maryland, College Park, MD 20742, USA 3Center for Astrophysics | Harvard & Smithsonian, 60 Garden Street, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA 4Center for Computational Astrophysics, Flatiron Institute, Simons Foundation, 162 Fifth Avenue, New York, NY 10010, USA Accepted XXX. Received YYY; in original form ZZZ",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,164,126,False,False,0,126
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_001,1,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars ABSTRACT The recent pericentric passage of the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) through the Milky Way (MW) has dislodged its centre of mass, inducing a state of dynamical disequilibrium, thereflex motion, in the kinematics of outer stellar halo stars. Using data out to distances of160 kpcfrom the combined H3+SEGUE+MagE outer halo survey, we constrain the mass of the MW and LMC, as well as the resulting reflex motion and the velocity anisotropy of the stellar halo. Using a suite of 32,000 rigid MW–LMC simulations, each with a MW stellar halo evolved to the present day in the combined MW–LMC potential, we perform Simulation Based Inference by training a neural posterior estimator on the means and dispersions of the radial and tangential velocities of stars from the combined H3+SEGUE+MagE outer halo sample. Relative to halo stars at100 kpc, we find the magnitude of the reflex velocity to be𝑣 travel=39.4+7.6 −7.2km s−1. Simultaneously, we determine the enclosed MW mass to be𝑀 MW(<50 kpc)=3.63±0.16×1011M⊙and the enclosed LMC mass to",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_002,2,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars −1.81×1010M⊙ OurresultssuggestthatthetotalLMCmassmustbeatleast∼20%thatoftheMW.ThevelocityanisotropypriortotheLMC’s infall is constrained to be𝛽 0=0.61±0.03. Finally, we demonstrate that failing to account for the LMC in models biases the MW mass estimate to prefer slightly more massive values. Key words:Galaxy: kinematics and dynamics – Galaxy: halo – Galaxy: evolution – Magellanic Clouds – software: machine learning – software: simulations",−1.81×1010M⊙,−1.81×1010M⊙,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,88,67,False,True,0,67
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_003,3,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 1 INTRODUCTION The mass of the Milky Way (MW) is one of its most fundamental properties, with its value crucial to constructing dynamical models for our Galaxy. The MW mass has been estimated through a variety of techniques and visible tracers such as: stellar streams (e.g., Gib- bons et al. 2014; Küpper et al. 2015; Malhan & Ibata 2019; Erkal et al. 2019a; Vasiliev et al. 2021; Dillamore et al. 2025), satellite galaxies and globular clusters (e.g., Zaritsky et al. 1989; Vasiliev 2019b; Correa Magnus & Vasiliev 2022), and MW halo stars (e.g., Deason et al. 2012; Williams & Evans 2015; Williams et al. 2017; McMillan 2017; Bird et al. 2022; Shen et al. 2022; Medina et al. 2025), as well as via dynamics-free approaches (e.g., Zaritsky & Courtois2017).Moststudiesconvergearoundconstrainedvaluesfor the virial mass of the MW being𝑀 MW∼1−2×1012M⊙with an uncertainty of approximately20−30%(see, Wang et al. 2020; Bobylev&Baykova2023;Hunt&Vasiliev2025,forcompilations). ManyofthesepreviousestimateshavereliedontheMWbeingwell ★E-mail: richard.brooks.22@ucl.ac.uk †NASA Hubble Fellowship Program, Einstein Fellowdescribed by equilibrium models (e.g., Jeans models, Jeans 1915), i.e., not",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_004,4,"(e.g., Zaritsky & Courtois2017).Moststudiesconvergearoundconstrainedvaluesfor the virial mass of the MW being𝑀 MW∼1−2×1012M⊙with an uncertainty of approximately20−30%(see, Wang et al. 2020; Bobylev&Baykova2023;Hunt&Vasiliev2025,forcompilations). ManyofthesepreviousestimateshavereliedontheMWbeingwell ★E-mail: richard.brooks.22@ucl.ac.uk †NASA Hubble Fellowship Program, Einstein Fellowdescribed by equilibrium models (e.g., Jeans models, Jeans 1915), i.e., not undergoing a significant merger episode. Yet, the MW is currently experiencing a merger with the Large MagellanicCloud(LMC,seeVasiliev2023,foracomprehensivere- viewoftheeffectoftheLMContheMW)satellitegalaxy.TheLMC is thought to be on its first pericentric passage and to have a dark mattermass𝑀 LMC∼1011M⊙(e.g.,Beslaetal.2007,2010;Boylan- Kolchin et al. 2011; Peñarrubia et al. 2016; Erkal et al. 2019a; Ko- posovetal.2023;Kravtsov&Winney2024;Benisty2024;Watkins et al. 2024; Brooks et al. 2025). As the LMC has just completed its most recent pericentric passage at a relative velocity>300 km s−1 andhasasignificantmassratiorelativetotheMW(∼1 : 4−10),the interactionofthesetwogalaxieshasinducedstateofdynamicaldis- equilibrium throughout the entire MW (Deason & Belokurov 2024; Hunt & Vasiliev 2025). This is also expected and seen within MW– LMC analogues identified within cosmological simulations (e.g., Darragh-Ford et al. 2025; Arora et al. 2025; Mansfield et al. 2026). The infall of the LMC into the MW has generated a density wake in theMW’sdarkmatterhalo(Weinberg1998;Garavito-Camargoetal. 2019,2021a;Tamfaletal.2021;Rozieretal.2022;Footeetal.2023), aswellasleavingobservablesignatureinthedensityandkinematics of stars in the stellar halo (e.g.,",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_005,5,"within cosmological simulations (e.g., Darragh-Ford et al. 2025; Arora et al. 2025; Mansfield et al. 2026). The infall of the LMC into the MW has generated a density wake in theMW’sdarkmatterhalo(Weinberg1998;Garavito-Camargoetal. 2019,2021a;Tamfaletal.2021;Rozieretal.2022;Footeetal.2023), aswellasleavingobservablesignatureinthedensityandkinematics of stars in the stellar halo (e.g., Belokurov et al. 2019; Conroy et al. ©2026 The AuthorsarXiv:2603.08788v1 [astro-ph.GA] 9 Mar 2026 2R. A. N. Brooks et al. 2021;Cavieresetal.2025;Chandraetal.2025b;Yaaqibetal.2024; Byströmetal.2025;Fushimietal.2024;Amaranteetal.2024;Sheng et al. 2025). On large scales, the inner and outer parts of the MW halohaveexperienceddifferentstrengthsofaccelerationtowardsthe LMC (e.g., Gómez et al. 2015). To a Galactocentric observer, the Galactic northern sky appears to be red-shifted and the Galactic southernskyblue-shiftedbecausethehalomovespreferentially‘up’, towards the Galactic north. This ‘reflex’ displacement manifests as a dipole signal in density (Garavito-Camargo et al. 2021b; Conroy etal.2021;Amaranteetal.2024)thatishigherintheGalacticNorth, and similarly in stellar radial velocities (Erkal et al. 2019b; Petersen & Peñarrubia 2020, 2021; Erkal et al. 2021; Yaaqib et al. 2024; Chandra et al. 2025b; Byström et al. 2025; Brooks et al. 2025). The magnitude of the reflex velocity dipole is called thetravel velocity and its on-sky orientation is called theapexdirection of the reflex motion. If models of the",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_006,6,"al. 2024; Chandra et al. 2025b; Byström et al. 2025; Brooks et al. 2025). The magnitude of the reflex velocity dipole is called thetravel velocity and its on-sky orientation is called theapexdirection of the reflex motion. If models of the MW ignore the reflex motion induced by the LMC, then the inference for the MW mass can be overestimated by up to50%depending on the observed regions of the sky (Erkal et al. 2020) . The direction and magnitude of the reflex motion are one of the main probes of the dynamical MW–LMC interaction. As a result, precise measurements of this induced motion will help further con- strain other properties of the MW and LMC (Brooks et al. 2025, 2026). Simulation Based Inference (SBI, see Cranmer et al. 2020, for a conceptual overview) is a statistical framework for inference in complex modelling scenarios where traditional analytic methods areimpractical.Ratherthandefiningalikelihoodfunction,SBIuses manyforwardsimulationstolearnthestatisticalrelationshipbetween modelparametersandobservationaldata.SBIhasbeenincreasingly applied to a variety of astrophysics problems (e.g., Weyant et al. 2013;Alsingetal.2019;Jeffreyetal.2021;Lemosetal.2021;Her- mansetal.2021;vonWietersheim-Kramstaetal.2024;Lovelletal. 2024; Ho et al. 2024; Widmark & Johnston 2025; Sante et al. 2025; Xiangyuan Ma et al. 2025;",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,420,600
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_007,7,"modelling scenarios where traditional analytic methods areimpractical.Ratherthandefiningalikelihoodfunction,SBIuses manyforwardsimulationstolearnthestatisticalrelationshipbetween modelparametersandobservationaldata.SBIhasbeenincreasingly applied to a variety of astrophysics problems (e.g., Weyant et al. 2013;Alsingetal.2019;Jeffreyetal.2021;Lemosetal.2021;Her- mansetal.2021;vonWietersheim-Kramstaetal.2024;Lovelletal. 2024; Ho et al. 2024; Widmark & Johnston 2025; Sante et al. 2025; Xiangyuan Ma et al. 2025; Jeffrey et al. 2025; Saoulis et al. 2025; Nguyen et al. 2025; Brooks et al. 2025, 2026) . In Brooks et al. (2026), we presented and validated a novel SBI architecture for exploring large MW–LMC model parameter spaces to enable rapid and reliable parameter inference of model param- eters using the dynamics of outer halo stars. We showed that our SBI framework trained on many rigid MW–LMC simulations re- tains enough of the relevant physics that more complex simulations capture(e.g.,𝑁-bodysimulations,Garavito-Camargoetal.2019)to avoid model misspecification and vastly improve the computational efficiencyoftheinference.SubsequentlyinBrooksetal.(2025),we demonstratedthefirstapplicationofourSBIarchitecturetodatafrom the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument (DESI, DESI Collabora- tion et al. 2025) survey and the H3+SEGUE+MagE (Chandra et al. 2025b) combined outer halo survey to constrain the LMC mass, the reflex motion and the strength of dynamical friction. One limitation of these studies is that only mean velocities of outer",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_008,8,"al. 2025) survey and the H3+SEGUE+MagE (Chandra et al. 2025b) combined outer halo survey to constrain the LMC mass, the reflex motion and the strength of dynamical friction. One limitation of these studies is that only mean velocities of outer halo stars were used for inference, limiting the ability to constrain the MW mass, which is sensitive to their velocity dispersions (see the spherical Jeans equations, Jeans 1915; Binney & Tremaine 2008). Expanding this SBI framework to incorporate the velocity dispersions of outer halo stars will enable simultaneous constraints to be placed on the MW and LMC masses as well as their reflex interaction. Inthiswork,wepresentanenhancedSBIarchitecturetoconstrain theMW–LMCinteraction.Weuseboththemeananddispersionsof outer halo star velocities to place constraints on the MW and LMC masses, the velocity anisotropy parameter, and the reflex motion. Additionally,weimproveonthepreviousdistance-independentreflex motion parametrisation by implementing a linear continuity modelto capture any radial variation of the reflex motion. Using all-sky kinematicsdatafromtheH3+SEGUE+MagE(Chandraetal.2025b) combinedwiththesemodelenhancements,ourSBIframeworkisable to simultaneously produce precise constraints on the MW and LMC masses,thereflexmotionandvelocityanisotropy.Furthermore,with the release of upcoming datasets from Gaia Data Release 4 (DR4) andtheSloanDigitalSkySurveyV(SDSS-V,Chandraetal.2025a; SDSS Collaboration et al. 2025), our flexible SBI framework",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_009,9,"of the reflex motion. Using all-sky kinematicsdatafromtheH3+SEGUE+MagE(Chandraetal.2025b) combinedwiththesemodelenhancements,ourSBIframeworkisable to simultaneously produce precise constraints on the MW and LMC masses,thereflexmotionandvelocityanisotropy.Furthermore,with the release of upcoming datasets from Gaia Data Release 4 (DR4) andtheSloanDigitalSkySurveyV(SDSS-V,Chandraetal.2025a; SDSS Collaboration et al. 2025), our flexible SBI framework will be uniquely placed to properly account for variation across surveys when performing inference. Thelayoutofthispaperisasfollows.InSec.2wedescribethedata usedthroughoutthisworkanddefinethesummarystatisticsusedfor theinference.InSec.3,wegiveadescriptionoftheMW–LMCsimu- lationsusedtotraintheSBIarchitectureusedforparameterinference. In Sec. 4, we describe the SBI architecture, Bayesian statistics and themachinelearningmodelsusedforinference.InSec.5,wegivethe constraints on the MW and LMC enclosed and total masses, the re- flexmotionandthevelocityanisotropymodelparameters.InSec.6, wediscussourresultsincontextofpreviousliterature,assessmodel limitations and investigate the relative importance of including the LMC in models. Finally, we conclude and provide an outlook for upcoming surveys in Sec. 7.",1 INTRODUCTION,1 INTRODUCTION,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,142,109,False,False,840,949
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_010,10,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 2.1.1 H3 The H3 Spectroscopic Survey (Conroy et al. 2019) has conducted a spectroscopic survey of halo stars with the Hectochelle instru- ment (Szentgyorgyi et al. 2011) on the6.5mMMT telescope at the Whipple Observatory in Arizona. We use the sample of H3 stars observed up to January 2024 that have reliable stellar parameters fromMINESweeper(Cargileetal.2020)andarenotassociatedwith known MW substructures.",2.1.1 H3,2.1.1 H3,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,94,72,True,False,0,72
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_011,11,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 2.1.2 SEGUE The Sloan Extension for Galactic Understanding and Exploration (SEGUE, Yanny et al. 2009) survey observed∼250,000stars with the low-resolutionBOSS spectrograph as apart of theSloan Digital SkySurvey(SDSS,Yorketal.2000).Thesespectrahavebeenfitted usingtheMINESweeperroutinetoprovidereliablestellarparameters (Chandra et al. 2025a; Cargile & Chandra 2025).",2.1.2 SEGUE,2.1.2 SEGUE,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,67,51,False,False,0,51
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_012,12,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 2.1.3 MagE Overthepasttwoyears,atailoredspectroscopicsurveyofluminous RGB stars in the outer halo has been conducted with The Magellan Echellete Spectrograph (MagE, Marshall et al. 2008) on the6.5m Magellan Baade Telescope at Las Campanas Observatory. The se- lection procedure for the target sample of RGB stars is described in Chandra et al. (2023a), and the details of the spectroscopic survey are further described in Chandra et al. (2023b) and Chandra et al. (2025b). Stellar parameters are estimated with theMINESweeper code (Cargile et al. 2020) including parallax measurements from Gaia(GaiaCollaborationetal.2023).AsofMay2024,atotalof400 starshavebeenobserved,ofwhich∼300arespectroscopicallycon- firmed to be at a heliocentric distance beyond50 kpc, and∼100are",2.1.3 MagE,2.1.3 MagE,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,145,111,True,False,0,111
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_013,13,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars MNRAS000, 1–16 (2026) Quantifying the MW and LMC with SBI3 beyond100 kpcmaking it the largest dataset of outer halo stars be- yond50 kpc.ForamuchmoredetailedaccountoftheMagEsurvey, we direct the reader to Chandra et al. (2023a,b, 2025b). 2.1.4 Combined H3+SEGUE+MagE sample Theabovesurveyscombinetoproduceapuresampleofstarswithho- mogeneousstellarparametersandtheiruncertaintiesderivedwiththe MINESweeperpipeline, e.g., distance estimates with an uncertainty floor of10%. Subsequently, we adopt the same selection procedure to exactly reproduce the high-fidelity subset in sec. 2.3, Chandra etal.(2025b),e.g.,fractionaldistanceuncertainties<25%.Thisse- lection procedure ensures that known substructures and unphysical quantitiesareremovedfromthesample.Inparticular,theSagittarius stream (e.g., Majewski et al. 2003; Vasiliev et al. 2021) is excised usingtheangularmomentumcutsproposedbyJohnsonetal.(2020). The all-sky sample of H3+SEGUE+MagE stars used in this work contains1,296field stars between𝑟 gal∈[30−160]kpc. Note that some figures in this study will use the shorthand ‘H3+’ to refer to this combined sample.","MNRAS000, 1–16 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–16 (2026)",,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,176,135,False,False,0,135
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_014,14,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 2.2 Observational summary statistics SBI requires a set of summary statistics to be used for the inference process. For this application of an SBI framework to the dynamics of the outer MW stars, we adopt summary statistics based on their velocity field distributions. Specifically, we focus on the mean and dispersions of the radial and tangential velocities as a function of distance.Thischoiceofvelocitysummarystatisticsareinsensitiveto differential selection between the individual H3, SEGUE and MagE surveys.",2.2 Observational summary statistics,2.2 Observational summary statistics,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,114,87,False,False,0,87
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_015,15,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 2.2.1 Radial velocities We calculate the stellar radial velocities in theGalactic Standard of Rest(GSR) frame,𝑣 GSR, which accounts for the solar motion with respecttotheGalacticcentre.Foranon-rotatingand/oraxisymmetric galaxy in equilibrium, the mean radial velocity,⟨𝑣 GSR⟩, is expected to be zero throughout the entire MW. However, our Galaxy is in disequilibriumduetothemergerwiththeLMCandhence⟨𝑣 GSR⟩≠0 ubiquitously across the MW. We explore the full depth and on-sky coverage of the H3+SEGUE+MagE dataset by dividing the sky into quadrants and measuring a set of mean radial and tangential velocity summary statistics. Throughout this work, we define the quadrants as fol- lows: Quadrant 1 (Q1) as𝑙∈[+180◦,0◦],𝑏∈[0◦,+90◦], Quad- rant 2 (Q2) as𝑙∈ [0◦,−180◦],𝑏∈ [0◦,+90◦], Quadrant 3 (Q3) as𝑙∈ [+180◦,0◦],𝑏∈ [−90◦,0◦]and Quadrant 4 (Q4) as 𝑙∈ [0◦,−180◦],𝑏∈ [−90◦,0◦]. A visual representation can be seen in the Mollweide figure above Fig. 1 and Fig. 2. For the all- sky H3+SEGUE+MagE dataset, we calculate the3𝜎-clipped mean, ⟨𝑣GSR⟩, and dispersions,⟨𝜎 𝑣GSR⟩, values for the Galactocentric dis- tance range𝑟 gal∈[30−160]kpcin 5 equally spaced distance bins in the northern quadrants (Q1 and",2.2.1 Radial velocities,2.2.1 Radial velocities,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_016,16,"figure above Fig. 1 and Fig. 2. For the all- sky H3+SEGUE+MagE dataset, we calculate the3𝜎-clipped mean, ⟨𝑣GSR⟩, and dispersions,⟨𝜎 𝑣GSR⟩, values for the Galactocentric dis- tance range𝑟 gal∈[30−160]kpcin 5 equally spaced distance bins in the northern quadrants (Q1 and Q2) and the southern quadrants (Q3andQ4);seetheupperandmiddlepanelsofFig.1&Fig.2.The distance bins arelarger than any associated distanceuncertainties to minimise correlations between adjacent bins. Note, that neither the data nor the simulations account for uncertainty in the solar motion withrespecttotheGalacticcentre.Inthesefigures,datapointswith increased transparency, and distinct markers, are those which are poorly represented by simulated data; see Sec. 5 for more detail.2.2.2 Tangential velocities The tangential velocity of a star, particularly in the Galactic latitude direction,𝑣 𝑡,𝑏,tracestheLMC’sperturbationintheMW(Erkaletal. 2021; Sheng et al. 2024; Chandra et al. 2025b). This component of the tangential velocity captures the apparent ‘upward’ reflex motion ofouterhalostarsastheMW’scentreofmassisdragged‘downward’. We calculate the3𝜎-clipped mean,⟨𝑣 𝑡,𝑏⟩, and dispersions,⟨𝜎 𝑣𝑡,𝑏⟩, values for the Galactocentric distance range𝑟 gal∈[30−160]kpc in 6 equally spaced distance bins; see the lower panels of Fig. 1 & Fig. 2. These tangential velocities have been corrected for the solar motion and uncertainties determined via bootstrap resampling. In",2.2.1 Radial velocities,2.2.1 Radial velocities,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_017,17,"values for the Galactocentric distance range𝑟 gal∈[30−160]kpc in 6 equally spaced distance bins; see the lower panels of Fig. 1 & Fig. 2. These tangential velocities have been corrected for the solar motion and uncertainties determined via bootstrap resampling. In thisstudy,wedonotconsiderthetangentialvelocitiesintheGalactic longitudinal direction,𝑣 𝑡,𝑙, though future work can utilise them to constraintheLMC’spresent-dayphasespacecoordinatesanditspast orbit (Sheng et al. 2025).",2.2.1 Radial velocities,2.2.1 Radial velocities,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,75,57,True,False,280,337
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_018,18,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 3.1.1 The Milky Way To model the MW dark matter halo we use a Navarro-Frenk-White (NFW, Navarro et al. 1996, 1997) dark matter halo density profile described by𝑀 200and𝑐 200; see Table. 1 for the mass prior range considered. These quantities are defined by a sphere enclosing an overdensity that is 200 times the critical density of the Universe, 𝜌crit=3𝐻2 0/8𝜋𝐺, as denoted by the ‘200’ subscript, where Hub- ble parameter,𝐻 0, is taken to be67.6 km s−1Mpc−1using the de- faultcosmologyinastropy(AstropyCollaborationetal.2013).We constrain the normalisation of the halo mass profile such that the circular velocity at the solar position is approximately235 km s−1 (e.g., matching constraints from McMillan 2017, within the associ- ated uncertainty).1The MW stellar components are modelled using asphericalbulgewithatotalmass1.2×1010M⊙,andanexponential stellar disc with a total mass5×1010M⊙. The stellar distributions remain fixed taking the values suggested by McMillan (2017).",3.1.1 The Milky Way,3.1.1 The Milky Way,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,199,153,False,True,0,153
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_019,19,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 3.1.2 The LMC WemodeltheLMCasaHernquistdarkmatterhalo(Hernquist1990). We normalise the profile such that the derived rotation curve peaks at(91.7±18.8)km s−1atadistanceof8.7 kpcfromitscentre.This correspondstoanencloseddynamicalmassof𝑀 LMC(𝑟 <8.7 kpc)= (1.7±0.7)×1010M⊙(van der Marel & Kallivayalil 2014). See Table. 1 for the chosen total LMC mass prior distribution. 3.1.3 The MW–LMC interaction ThetrajectoriesoftheMWandLMCundertheirmutualgravitational attraction are numerically integrated (see equ. 3-6 in Brooks et al. 2026).WeaccountforaccelerationduetoChandrasekhardynamical friction,a DF, on the trajectory of the LMC (Chandrasekhar 1943; Binney & Tremaine 2008; Jethwa et al. 2016) as: aDF=−4𝜋𝐺2𝑀LMC𝜌MWlnΛ 𝑣3",3.1.2 The LMC,3.1.2 The LMC,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,120,92,False,True,0,92
