astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_020,20,"the median of radial velocity pR. The vertical dashed lines delimit the vicinity of the Sun inside 200 pc. Finally, the horizontal arrows set up a correspondence between the diagonal ridges and the MGs, while the star symbols in both panels shows the position of the Sun in these planes for reference. In the case of the elongated ellipsoids, the coefficients Ui(i= 0,1,2) are analytical functions of the variable ζ (see Michtchenko et al. 2018a for more details): U0(ζ) =1 cζ0lnp 1 +ζ2+ζ , (7) U1(ζ) =1 c3ζ3 0"" ζp 1 +ζ2−lnp 1 +ζ2+ζ# ,(8) U2(ζ) =1 2c3ζ3 0h lnp 1 +ζ2+ζ −ζp 1 +ζ2i ,(9) where cis the minor semi-axis of the ellipsoid and the variable ζis defined as: ζ2=a2−c2 c2+λ, (10) where ais the major semi-axis of the ellipsoid and ζ0 is computed for λ= 0. At the point Plying outside the layer, λis the positive square root of the quadratic equation",Φ(P) =−3,Φ(P) =−3,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,201,154,False,True,140,294
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_021,21,"Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood X∗2 a2+λ+Y∗2 c2+λ= 1, (11) while, at any point Pinside the layer, λ= 0. It is worth mentioning that the ellipsoid is a 3D figure, but here we take into account only the potential components in the plane Z= 0. In our investigations, we assumed a three-layer config- uration of the central bar with three different densities. This choice is justified by observational evidence that suggests a non-uniform density distribution between the short ,middle andlong extended structures in the central Galactic region (e.g., Bland-Hawthorn & Gerhard 2016).The resulting potential of the bar Φ barat a given point is just a sum of the contributions of the inner layer (core ellipsoid), the middle and outer layers at this point. The potential of the middle (outer) layer is just a potential given by Eq. (6) after subtracting the potential of the inner (middle) component. It should be noted that the axisymmetric contribution of the bar potential has al- ready been taken into account in the observation-based Φ0.",X∗2,X∗2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_022,22,"is just a potential given by Eq. (6) after subtracting the potential of the inner (middle) component. It should be noted that the axisymmetric contribution of the bar potential has al- ready been taken into account in the observation-based Φ0. Therefore, we subtract a spherical component from Φbar, as described in Michtchenko et al. (2018a); the re- sulting mass of the non-axisymmetric part of the bar is shown in Table 2.",X∗2,X∗2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,93,71,False,False,140,211
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_023,23,"Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood PARAMETERS To identify the dynamical effects of the non- axisymmetric structures within the observable stellar dis- tribution, it is necessary to evaluate their physical and structural parameters in the Galactic potential model. The parameters for the spiral arms model were obtained by the observation data fitting procedure, while the pa- rameters for the central bar expressions were selected from the literature. The choice (see Table 2) is briefly discussed in this section. 4.1. Spiral arms parameters The spiral arms model has already been introduced and explored in our research series (e.g., Michtchenko et al. 2017; L´ epine et al. 2017; Barros et al. 2020). The values of the structural parameters were derived through the modeling of the stellar spiral density in the Galactic disk and calculated under the self-consistency condition (see Barros et al. 2021 for details). In particular, the choice of the four-arm spiral model is favored by the spiral tracer observations, as commented on L´ epine et al. (2017). 5",PARAMETERS,PARAMETERS,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,231,177,False,False,0,177
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_024,24,"Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood TABLE 2 Physical and structural parameters of the spiral arms and the three-layer central bar of the Galactic potential model. Spiral arms Symbol Value Units Pitch angleai -14.0 deg Perturbation amplitude ζ0 150.0 km2s−1kpc−1 Number of spiral arms m 4 - Arms width σ 8.0 kpc Scale size ϵ−1 s 4.0 kpc Reference radius Ri 8.122 kpc Spirals pattern speed Ω sp 28.0 km s−1kpc−1 Initial phase angle γsp 223.34 deg Central bar Symbol Value Unit Bar-Bulge mass Mb 2.57×1010M⊙ Non-axisymmetric mass Mbar 5.33×109M⊙ Major semi-axis size Rbar 4.5 kpc Flattening fbar 0.7 - Bar’s pattern speed Ω bar 40.0 km s−1kpc−1 Initial phase angleaγbar 115.0 deg Bar’s layers Inner Middle Outer Semi-major axis (kpc) 2.0 2.9 4.5 Mass (1010M⊙) 0.85 0.85 0.85 Density (G M⊙) 0.0036 0.0012 0.0003 aIt is worth noting our choice of the negative sign of the pitch angle, which means the counterclockwise Galactic rotation from the viewpoint of an observer located towards the direction of the North Galactic Pole. The",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_025,25,"M⊙) 0.0036 0.0012 0.0003 aIt is worth noting our choice of the negative sign of the pitch angle, which means the counterclockwise Galactic rotation from the viewpoint of an observer located towards the direction of the North Galactic Pole. The choice of the angular rotation speed Ω spis very important because the position of the dynamical char- acteristics induced by the spiral arms is uniquely de- fined by this parameter. Assuming a rigid body rotation of the spiral arms structure, we use Ω sp= 28.0 km s−1kpc−1that is consistent with the values de- rived in Dias et al. (2019) and Bobylev & Bajkova (2022). Note that our choice of the Ω sp–value is in good agree- ment with the result in Kalda & Green (2025), where the authors model the distribution of stars in the SNd and obtain the best-fit for the pattern rotation speed of 28.2±0.1 km s−1kpc−1. In Sect. 8, we extend our study over a wide range of the Ω sp–values by exploring the parametric planes. It is also noteworthy that recent works present evidence that the",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_026,26,"for the pattern rotation speed of 28.2±0.1 km s−1kpc−1. In Sect. 8, we extend our study over a wide range of the Ω sp–values by exploring the parametric planes. It is also noteworthy that recent works present evidence that the spiral arms closely trace the Milky Way’s rotation curve and exhibit a transient nature (Castro-Ginard et al. 2021; Joshi & Malhotra 2023), and several authors have investigated the growth and disruption phases of the Galactic spiral structure (e.g., Liu et al. 2025). All of these hypotheses fail to account for the MGs and the DRs because the life-time of the material arms is not long enough to capture the objects in the resonant configurations. The amplitude ζ0of the spiral arms is determined from global fits of the maximum ratios of radial and tangential forces to the axisymmetric force across the Galactic disk through Eqs.1–4 above. The maximum ratio of forces, as a function of the Galactic radius, lies in an interval of 3% to 6%. These values are consistent with values of density contrast collected in the literature by Antoja",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_027,27,"Galactic disk through Eqs.1–4 above. The maximum ratio of forces, as a function of the Galactic radius, lies in an interval of 3% to 6%. These values are consistent with values of density contrast collected in the literature by Antoja et al. (2011) from both Galactic and extragalactic sources. Further- more, because we employ a four–rigid-body–arm model, the value of ζ0is the same for all arms. It should be noted that, from a dynamical point of view, the value of ζ0has no influence on location and modifies the width of Fig. 3.— Force functions of the Galactic components: the ax- isymmetric structure (red), the spiral arms (blue) and the central bar (black), calculated along the radial Galactocentric distance in the direction of the Sun ( φ= 90 deg). The dashed vertical line indicates the actual radial distance of the Sun. the resonance zones only weakly. Finally, γspis the initial phase angle of the spiral arms, determined by assuming that the Sagittarius arm is located 1 kpc from the Sun, where the distance of the Sun to the Galactic center is",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_028,28,"the resonance zones only weakly. Finally, γspis the initial phase angle of the spiral arms, determined by assuming that the Sagittarius arm is located 1 kpc from the Sun, where the distance of the Sun to the Galactic center is R⊙= 8.122 kpc and φ⊙= 90 deg. It should be stressed that this particular orientation of the spiral arms is derived from the observed positions of zero-age masers (see Barros et al. 2021 and Reid et al. 2019); naturally, using different test objects would lead to different inferred orientations of the spiral pattern (e.g., Eilers et al. 2020, Poggio et al. 2021 and Palicio et al. 2023). 4.2. Central bar’s parameters Due to ongoing uncertainties about the physical and structural characteristics of the central bar, we have cho- sen values that are commonly referenced in recent re- search in the literature. They are listed in Table 2. Mass : Taking a bar-bulge structure with total mass Mb, the mass of the bar can be taken as a fraction µ= Mbar/Mbof the total mass, where µis a real number between 0",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,560,740
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_029,29,"literature. They are listed in Table 2. Mass : Taking a bar-bulge structure with total mass Mb, the mass of the bar can be taken as a fraction µ= Mbar/Mbof the total mass, where µis a real number between 0 and 1. As an example, Portail et al. (2017) obtained the total mass Mb= 1.88×1010M⊙and the resulting bar mass of Mbar= 5.40×109M⊙, getting µ≈0.29. We use the recent value of the total mass fitted in Michtchenko & Barros (2023) listed in Table 2, and we consider by approximation that the fractional mass of the bar is µ= 0.30, which is consistent with the literature. Size: Several works involving N-body simulations sup- port the existence of the long bar structure (e.g., Bland- Hawthorn & Gerhard 2016 and Portail et al. 2017), and others show its observational evidence, as pointed by Zoccali & Valenti (2016). The estimation of its size varies between 4 and 5 kpc. Therefore, we consider a Rbar= 4.5 kpc which is consistent with the values found in the literature (e.g., Wegg et al. 2015, Tepper-Garcia et al. 2021",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,700,880
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_030,30,"& Valenti (2016). The estimation of its size varies between 4 and 5 kpc. Therefore, we consider a Rbar= 4.5 kpc which is consistent with the values found in the literature (e.g., Wegg et al. 2015, Tepper-Garcia et al. 2021 and Ghosh et al. 2023), and we also assume a flattening of 0.7, as listed in Table 2. Rotation speed : There are two main scenarios pre- sented in the literature on the value of the bar’s rota- tion pattern speed. The fast rotating bar with Ω bar≳ 50.0 km s−1kpc−1(e.g., Dehnen 2000) is the old one which could explain the origin of HMG by the mecha- nism of the -2:1 LR. The most recent scenario uses the slow rotating bar with Ω bar≲40.0 km s−1kpc−1(e.g., P´ erez-Villegas et al. 2017), which is the most acceptable 6 Fig. 4.— The topology of the Hamiltonian (12) on the X–Y plane, and the co-rotation zones originated by the spiral arms (black curves) and the central bar perturbations (red curves). The stable and unstable centers (equilibrium solutions) are indicated by the dot and",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,840,1020
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_031,31,"topology of the Hamiltonian (12) on the X–Y plane, and the co-rotation zones originated by the spiral arms (black curves) and the central bar perturbations (red curves). The stable and unstable centers (equilibrium solutions) are indicated by the dot and cross symbols, respectively. The black dashed lines in- tersecting denote the Sun’s position, whereas the dotted lines show the orientation of the central bar, which is symbolically depicted as an ellipsoid. We also plot the azimuthal minima and maxima of spiral potential (light green line and orange dashed line, respec- tively), calculated through Eq.(3). value. The slow rotating bar scenario is supported by ar- guments that associate the formation of HMG with the CR and high-order LRs (see Sect. 5). Therefore, we use the value of Ω bar= 40 .0 km s−1kpc−1, but we also ex- plore a range of Ω bar–values by using parametric planes in Sect. 8. 4.3. Comparing the force functions of the Galactic components A force function, by definition, is a gravitational po- tential with a positive sign; thus, its larger magnitude will manifest itself as",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,980,1160
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_032,32,"Ω bar–values by using parametric planes in Sect. 8. 4.3. Comparing the force functions of the Galactic components A force function, by definition, is a gravitational po- tential with a positive sign; thus, its larger magnitude will manifest itself as a stronger perturbation on the stel- lar motion. In Fig. 3, we present the force functions of the Galactic components on a logarithmic scale to facil- itate a comparison of their potential contributions. The force of the axisymmetric term (red curve) is dominant in the SNd, at least three orders of magnitude larger than those of the central bar (black curve) and the spiral arms (blue curve). Recall that the axisymmetric potential ob- tained through Eq.(2), accounts for the contributions of the Galactic disk and the spherical projections of the bulge and dark halo on the Galactic equator. The non-axisymmetric components were calculated through Eq.(3), for the spiral arm structure, and Eq.(6) (deducting spherical part), for the central bar, along the line of sight from the Sun to the Galactic center (φ= 90 deg in the reference frame described below).",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_033,33,"components were calculated through Eq.(3), for the spiral arm structure, and Eq.(6) (deducting spherical part), for the central bar, along the line of sight from the Sun to the Galactic center (φ= 90 deg in the reference frame described below). Close to the Sun’s position (vertical dashed line), the forces exerted by the bar and the spiral arms structure are of the same order of magnitude.",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,86,66,False,True,1260,1326
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_034,34,"Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood AND LINDBLAD RESONANCES In the rotating reference frame around the Z–axis, the Hamiltonian that describes the 2D stellar dynamics in the equatorial Galactic plane is defined by the axisym- metric potential Φ 0(R) and the perturbation H1(R, φ) as",AND LINDBLAD RESONANCES,AND LINDBLAD RESONANCES,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,71,54,False,False,0,54
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_035,35,"Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood R2 −ΩpLz+ Φ 0(R) +H1(R, φ),(12) where pRandLzare canonical momenta, per unit mass, conjugated to Randφ, respectively, and Ω pis a constantangular speed of the frame (rigid body approximation). The perturbation H1is defined as a sum of the spiral Φsp(R, φ) and bar’s Φ bar(R, φ) potentials: H1(R, φ) = Φ sp(R, φ) + Φ bar(R, φ). (13) As shown in Fig. 3, the non-axisymmetric terms Φsp(R, φ) and Φ bar(R, φ) are a few orders of magnitude smaller than the axisymmetric term Φ 0(R); therefore, they introduce only small perturbations in stellar mo- tions. In perturbation theories, the key assumption is that a complex problem can be treated by successive ap- proximations of a set of simpler related problems. In our case, the first-order approximation consists of the one-of-degree-of-freedom axisymmetric problem, which is perturbed separately by either the central bar or the spi- ral arms. In the second-order approximation, which ac- counts for the interaction between two non-axisymmetric structures, the resulting perturbation term becomes",R2,R2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_036,36,"approximation consists of the one-of-degree-of-freedom axisymmetric problem, which is perturbed separately by either the central bar or the spi- ral arms. In the second-order approximation, which ac- counts for the interaction between two non-axisymmetric structures, the resulting perturbation term becomes con- siderably weaker, by an order of magnitude, and will be disregarded in this work. Therefore, bar and spiral per- turbations will be examined individually in the Hamilto- nian (12). It should be noted that effects of the interac- tion of the two patterns could be of particular interest and will be examined in future work . In Fig. 4, we analyze the Hamiltonian topology plot- ting the energy levels of the effective potential (see Michtchenko et al. 2018b for details): Φeff(R, φ) = Φ 0(R) + Φ 1(R, φ)−1",R2,R2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,169,130,False,True,140,270
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_037,37,"Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood 2Ω2 pR2, (14) where the perturbation Φ 1(R, φ) is attributed to the cen- tral bar or the spiral arms. The set of red curves shows the energy levels, assuming Φ 1(R, φ) = Φ bar(R, φ), while the set of black curves shows the energy levels of the choice Φ 1(R, φ) = Φ sp(R, φ). In the first case, the pat- tern speed Ω p= Ω bar= 40.0 km s−1kpc−1, while, in the second case, Ω p= Ω sp= 28.0 km s−1kpc−1(see Table 2). In both cases, the banana-shaped islands, which appear in Fig. 4, match corotation zones on the X–Yhalf-plane (see Michtchenko et al. 2017 for more details). Originating from a corotation resonance (CR), coro- tation zones exhibit diverse attributes, shaped by the particular physical structures that generate them. For instance, the number of the zones depends on the sym- metry of the perturber; four islands are formed by the spiral pattern with four arms, while two islands arise from the elongated",2Ω2,2Ω2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_038,38,"the particular physical structures that generate them. For instance, the number of the zones depends on the sym- metry of the perturber; four islands are formed by the spiral pattern with four arms, while two islands arise from the elongated elliptic structure of the Galactic bar. The location of the zones is defined by the corotation radii of the equilibrium solutions, which are strongly de- pendent on the value of the pattern speed Ω p. In our case, the spiral corotation radius is 8 .34 kpc calculated for Ω sp= 28 .0 km s−1kpc−1, while the bar’s corotation radius is 5 .83 kpc, for Ω bar= 40.0 km s−1kpc−1. More- over, the orientation of the corotation zones with respect to the Sun on the X–Yplane is defined by the phase an- glesγbarandγsp, respectively. Finally, the extension of the corotation islands is determined by parameters such as the perturbation amplitude and the total mass, for the spiral structure and the central bar, respectively (see Table 2). Figure 5 shows the effects of gravitational perturba- tions caused by non-axisymmetric structures of stars",2Ω2,2Ω2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
