astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_000,0,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger front_matter Draft version January 30, 2026 Typeset using L ATEX default style in AASTeX631 A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger Mingji Deng,1Cuihua Du,1Jian Zhang,1HaoYang Liu,1and ZhongCheng Li1 1School of Astronomy and Space Sciences, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, P.R. China",front_matter,front_matter,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,88,67,False,False,0,67
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_001,1,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger ABSTRACT Previous studies have shown that disk warps can result from galaxy mergers. Recent research indicates a noticeable decline in the rotation curve (RC) of the Milky Way (MW), suggesting the need for a new low-mass model to describe its dynamical features. This study constructs a new Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus (GSE) merger model to characterize the RC features of our galaxy. We use the GIZMO code to simulate mergers with various orbital parameters to investigate how the disk warp evolves under different conditions. This simulation demonstrates the evolutionary mechanism of disk warp, which arises due to the asymmetric gravitational potential of the dark matter (DM) halo generated universally by galaxy mergers. The results indicate that the tilt angle of the DM halo partly reflects the gravitational strength at theZ= 0 plane, while the gravitational strength on the disk plane reflects the amplitude of disk warp. We identify a dual-regime interaction mechanism driven by the asymmetric halo potential. On short timescales, we find a distinct anti-correlation between the",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_002,2,"at theZ= 0 plane, while the gravitational strength on the disk plane reflects the amplitude of disk warp. We identify a dual-regime interaction mechanism driven by the asymmetric halo potential. On short timescales, we find a distinct anti-correlation between the halo’s tilt angle and the disk’s warp amplitude, indicating a ‘seesaw’ mechanism of angular momentum exchange. On secular timescales, however, dynamical friction drives a global alignment, causing both the halo tilt and the warp amplitude to decay simultaneously. Furthermore, we demonstrate that high-inclination mergers can sustain long-lived prograde precession, where the persistent yet decaying gravitational torque maintains the prograde bending mode against differential wind-up. Keywords:Galaxy structure (622); Milky Way disk (1050); Milky Way dark matter halo (1049); Galaxy mergers (608); Hydrodynamical simulations (767)",ABSTRACT,ABSTRACT,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,160,123,False,True,140,263
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_003,3,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 1.INTRODUCTION Disk warps are a common feature of galaxies, which is documented in previous studies (S´ anchez-Saavedra et al. 1990, 2003; Reshetnikov & Combes 1998). The Milky Way (MW), a typical example of disk and spiral galaxy, also exhibits a clear disk warp, confirmed by various works (Kerr 1957; Levine et al. 2006; Freudenreich et al. 1994; Chen et al. 2019; Shen et al. 2006). Numerous mechanisms of the Galactic warp have been proposed, including: inflow of intergalactic matter into the halo (Ostriker & Binney 1989; Quinn & Binney 1992; Jiang & Binney 1999); direct gas accretion onto the Galactic disk (L´ opez-Corredoira et al. 2002a; Roˇ skar et al. 2010; Goyary & Singh 2023); intergalactic magnetic fields (Battaner & Jim´ enez-Vicente 1998); interactions between satellites such as Sagittarius dwarf galaxy or Magellanic Clouds (Bailin 2003; Weinberg & Blitz 2006; Laporte et al. 2018a,b; Bennett et al. 2022; Poggio et al. 2021) with the disk; bending instabilities that generate self-excited or internally driven warps (Hunter",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_004,4,"satellites such as Sagittarius dwarf galaxy or Magellanic Clouds (Bailin 2003; Weinberg & Blitz 2006; Laporte et al. 2018a,b; Bennett et al. 2022; Poggio et al. 2021) with the disk; bending instabilities that generate self-excited or internally driven warps (Hunter & Toomre 1969; Nelson & Tremaine 1995; Revaz & Pfenniger 2004; Binney & Tremaine 2008; Sellwood & Debattista 2022); and misalignment of the dark matter halo with the disk (Han et al. 2023). Understanding the warp’s formation and evolution is crucial for reconstructing the dynamical history of the MW. Precise measurements based on mono-age stellar samples have revealed the detailed characteristics of the Galactic warp, and several studies have proposed that it originates from external perturbations (e.g., Poggio et al. 2020; Cheng et al. 2020). However, these works generally favor recent interactions with satellite galaxies that produce transient Corresponding author: Cuihua Du ducuihua@ucas.ac.cnarXiv:2601.21638v1 [astro-ph.GA] 29 Jan 2026 2 warps, which contradict the long-standing persistence of the Galactic warp (Roˇ skar et al. 2010; L´ opez-Corredoira et al. 2014; Li et al. 2023). Moreover, Bosma (1991) reported that at least half",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,140,320
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_005,5,"Cuihua Du ducuihua@ucas.ac.cnarXiv:2601.21638v1 [astro-ph.GA] 29 Jan 2026 2 warps, which contradict the long-standing persistence of the Galactic warp (Roˇ skar et al. 2010; L´ opez-Corredoira et al. 2014; Li et al. 2023). Moreover, Bosma (1991) reported that at least half of spiral galaxies exhibit warps, implying the existence of a long-lived and universal mechanism. Within the framework of ΛCDM cosmology, a defining feature is hierarchical assembly (e.g., White & Frenk 1991), suggesting that warps may form universally through galaxy mergers (Hammer et al. 2009). By combining the long-standing feature of the MW’s S-shaped warp, which has persisted for over 5 Gyr, with its timeline that may coincide with the completion of the gas-rich Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus (GSE) merger (Ciuc˘ a et al. 2024), we previously developed a Galactic warp evolution model primarily driven by the GSE merger (Deng et al. 2024). This model offers insights into the long-term evolution of the Galactic disk warp. Substantial evidence indicates that the GSE, regarded as the MW’s last major merger, constitutes the bulk of the inner halo (e.g., Belokurov et al. 2018; Helmi et al.",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,280,460
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_006,6,"model offers insights into the long-term evolution of the Galactic disk warp. Substantial evidence indicates that the GSE, regarded as the MW’s last major merger, constitutes the bulk of the inner halo (e.g., Belokurov et al. 2018; Helmi et al. 2018; Naidu et al. 2020) and has consequently been extensively studied through analog simulations (e.g., Bignone et al. 2019; Elias et al. 2020; Fattahi et al. 2019; Grand et al. 2020; Helmi et al. 2018; Koppelman et al. 2020). In Deng et al. (2024), our merger model builds upon the tailored GSE merger simulations of Naidu et al. (2021), which employed a grid of 500 idealized merger runs with the GADGET code (Springel et al. 2005, 2021) to identify a fiducial model that best matches the H3 survey data (Conroy et al. 2019a). By incorporating a gas component into this framework, we successfully reproduced the Galactic disk warp. With the release of Gaia DR3 (Gaia Collaboration et al. 2023), several new studies have applied previously developed techniques to infer distances for stars at large radii (even out to 30 kpc)",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_007,7,"framework, we successfully reproduced the Galactic disk warp. With the release of Gaia DR3 (Gaia Collaboration et al. 2023), several new studies have applied previously developed techniques to infer distances for stars at large radii (even out to 30 kpc) and to fit thev c(R) rotation curve of MW (e.g. Jiao et al. 2023; Ou et al. 2023). Their results show a sharp decrease in the Galactic rotation curve, two estimated total mass of the Milky Way is very similar and the average value would be M tot= 2.06+0.24 −0.13×1011M⊙, a significant low value, which hints at the need for a new model to reconstruct the dynamical features of the MW. Here, we further analyze the mechanism by which galaxy mergers induce warps in the Galactic disk. In this study, we identify the vertical bending mode and investigate its behavior using Fourier decomposition. To examine the connection between the dark matter halo and the disk, we calculate the vertical acceleration exerted by dark matter particles on the disk and evaluate its misalignment relative to the disk plane. In this study,",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,560,740
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_008,8,"using Fourier decomposition. To examine the connection between the dark matter halo and the disk, we calculate the vertical acceleration exerted by dark matter particles on the disk and evaluate its misalignment relative to the disk plane. In this study, we deepen our understanding by creating a gas-rich GSE merger simulation within a low-mass model to explore how the warp evolves and what causes the disk warp resulting from a galaxy merger. In Section 2, we provide a description of the simulation model and the initial conditions. In Section 3, we illustrate the DM halo profile. In Section 4, we show the warp evolution in different orbital settings and how DM halo raises the disk warp. Discussions and results are given in Section 5 and Section 6.",1.INTRODUCTION,1.INTRODUCTION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,166,127,False,False,700,827
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_009,9,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.MODEL AND SIMULATION In our previous work (Deng et al. 2024), we developed a realistic merger model for the MW. This model successfully reproduced the Galactic disk warp, capturing almost all its observed features. However, recent studies (Jiao et al. 2023; Ou et al. 2023) suggest that the MW rotation curve declines more rapidly at larger radii. Therefore, an improved model is needed to accurately represent the mass distribution of the galaxy. The numerical initial conditions (ICs) were generated using the Disk Initial Conditions Environment (DICE) code (Perret et al. 2014; Perret 2016). Density profiles for DM, stars, and gas were input into DICE as distribution functions. These functions were then used to generate Lagrangian particles via the Metropolis-Hastings Monte Carlo Markov Chain algorithm (Metropolis et al. 1953). The mass resolution is 1×105M⊙for baryonic particles and DM particles, such resolution is sufficient to track the warp structures, as the presence of warp has also been observed in some lower resolution cosmological simulation such as TNG100",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_010,10,"et al. 1953). The mass resolution is 1×105M⊙for baryonic particles and DM particles, such resolution is sufficient to track the warp structures, as the presence of warp has also been observed in some lower resolution cosmological simulation such as TNG100 (Semczuk et al. 2020). The modeling approach in this study utilizes the GIZMO code (Hopkins 2015). We implemented a star formation and feedback module following Wang et al. (2012), enabling gas particles to convert into stellar particles and vice versa through feedback mechanisms. GIZMO is a variant of the smooth particle hydrodynamics code Gadget-3, employing Adaptive Gravity Softening (see details in Hopkins et al. 2018).",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,137,105,True,False,140,245
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_011,11,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.1.GSE Our starting point is based on GSE mass measured by Lane et al. (2023). Using high-purity samples of APOGEE DR16 red giants selected by kinematics and chemistry, they estimated the mass of the GSE remnant as 1.45+0.92 −0.51×108M⊙, with a total mass∼6×1010M⊙. The GSE model consists of a stellar disk, a gas disk, and a DM halo. Considering mass loss during the merger process, the total mass of the GSE progenitor used in the simulation is set to 7.2×1010M⊙, which is 20% higher than the reference value (Lee et al. 2018). The stellar and gas disk models 3 Table 1.Initial Conditions atz∼2 Progenitor Parameters Milky Way GSE Units Dark Matter mass 11.55 6.93 1×1010M⊙ α3.5 3.25 N/A Dark Matter scale radius 12.867 8.02 kpc Stellar disk mass 15 0.2 1×109M⊙ Stellar scale length 2 0.6 kpc Stellar scale height 0.8 0.3 kpc Gas disk mass 30 2.5 1×109M⊙ Gas scale length 6 2.4 kpc Gas scale height 3 1.2 kpc Bulge mass 19.5 N/A",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_012,12,"Stellar disk mass 15 0.2 1×109M⊙ Stellar scale length 2 0.6 kpc Stellar scale height 0.8 0.3 kpc Gas disk mass 30 2.5 1×109M⊙ Gas scale length 6 2.4 kpc Gas scale height 3 1.2 kpc Bulge mass 19.5 N/A 1×109M⊙ Bulge scale 0.9 N/A kpc Orbital Parameters Eccentricity (e) 0.9 Initial distance 100.5 kpc Inclination angle 15◦,30◦,45◦,55◦,60◦,75◦degree Relative velocity 73.02 km/s follow an exponential + sech-z profile. The stellar mass is set to the median reference value of 2×108M⊙. The stellar disk scale lengthR ⋆(approximately 0.6 kpc), is determined using the size-mass relation (SMR) from Mowla et al. (2019). The disk scale height is 0.5×R ⋆, based on simulations showing thatz∼2 disks become thick due to turbulent gas and remain thick (Park et al. 2021). Given that it is a gas-rich merger atz∼2, we assume a high gas fraction of 0.92 for the GSE. Following assumptions from galaxy merger and metallicity gradient simulations by Cox et al. (2008) and Rocha et al. (2008), the gas disk scale length is set to 4×R ⋆, with a scale height equal to",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,140,320
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_013,13,"of 0.92 for the GSE. Following assumptions from galaxy merger and metallicity gradient simulations by Cox et al. (2008) and Rocha et al. (2008), the gas disk scale length is set to 4×R ⋆, with a scale height equal to half of this length. 2.2.Milky Way The MW mass is determined based on the hypothesis that the GSE merger was a major merger with a mass ratio of 2.5 : 1 (Belokurov et al. 2018; Naidu et al. 2021). Therefore, the virial mass of the MW is set to be 1.8×1011M⊙. Additionally, we require an appropriate representation of the MW during the merger epoch. Ages derived from various methods suggest that nearly all of the current high-α/thick disk and the bulge formed atz >1. Therefore, we model the MW atz∼2 as a combination of the present-day thick disk, gas disk, and bulge. Xiang et al. (2025) studied the age-dependent structure and star formation rate of the MW disk using high-αstars with substantial orbital angular momentum and precise age determinations. Referring to Fig. 4 in their work, we set the stellar",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_014,14,"and bulge. Xiang et al. (2025) studied the age-dependent structure and star formation rate of the MW disk using high-αstars with substantial orbital angular momentum and precise age determinations. Referring to Fig. 4 in their work, we set the stellar disk mass at approximately 1.5×1010M⊙atz∼2. Using the size-mass relation (SMR), the disk scale length (R ⋆) is set to be 2 kpc. The disk thickness is set to 0.4×R ⋆, based on Fig. 3 in Xiang et al. (2025). The gas fraction of the MW progenitor is set at 0.667. The scale length of the gas disk is 3×R ⋆, with a thickness of half scale length. Both disks follow an exponential + sech-z profile. For the MW bulge atz∼2, we adopt the Bulge-E model from Jiao et al. (2023), with a mass of 1.95×1010M⊙and a scale length (R b) of 0.9 kpc. The bulge is modeled using an Einasto profile (Einasto 1965), analogous to the S´ ersic profile (S´ ersic 1968) but applicable to three-dimensional mass density distributions (Coe 2010). This choice is reasonable given that the S´ ersic",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,420,600
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_015,15,"of 0.9 kpc. The bulge is modeled using an Einasto profile (Einasto 1965), analogous to the S´ ersic profile (S´ ersic 1968) but applicable to three-dimensional mass density distributions (Coe 2010). This choice is reasonable given that the S´ ersic profile is widely used to fit galaxy surface densities. Combining the above components, the total mass is set at 6.72×1010M⊙. This value aligns with the baryonic mass of approximately 6×1010M⊙reported by Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023). 2.3.Dark Matter Profile To accurately reproduce the observational features of the MW rotation curve, we adopted the Einasto profile (Einasto 1965) for the DM halos within the GSE and MW. This profile effectively describes a faster decline observed at large radii (Jiao et al. 2023; Ou et al. 2023). The Einasto profile is defined as follows 4 0 5 10 15 20 25 30 R (kpc)050100150200250vc (km/s) T otal Gas Stars DM Jiao et al. 2023 Ou et al. 2023 Figure 1.Rotation curve decomposition of the simulated galaxy att= 11 Gyr (for the 15◦inclination model). The solid lines represent the",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,560,740
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_016,16,"15 20 25 30 R (kpc)050100150200250vc (km/s) T otal Gas Stars DM Jiao et al. 2023 Ou et al. 2023 Figure 1.Rotation curve decomposition of the simulated galaxy att= 11 Gyr (for the 15◦inclination model). The solid lines represent the circular velocity contributions from different galactic components (DM, Gas, Stars and total), calculated using the PROFILE function of the PYNBODY library (Pontzen et al. 2013). The data points with error bars indicate observational constraints from the Milky Way, taken from Jiao et al. (2023) (blue triangles) and Ou et al. (2023) (red stars). ρEin(r) =M0 4πr3sexp[−(r/r s)α],(1) Here,M 0represents the mass normalization,r sis the scale radius, and the parameterαdetermines the rate at which density decreases with galactic radius—a largerαindicates a faster decline. The parameterαhas been measured as approximately 2.3 by Jiao et al. (2023) and 0.91 by Ou et al. (2023). Prior research provides limited guidance regarding suitableαvalues atz∼2, highlighting a significant gap in the current methodological framework. Enhanced mass deposition in the galactic outskirts due to major mergers naturally flattens the decline of the rotation curve, imposing strict constraints",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,700,880
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_017,17,"(2023). Prior research provides limited guidance regarding suitableαvalues atz∼2, highlighting a significant gap in the current methodological framework. Enhanced mass deposition in the galactic outskirts due to major mergers naturally flattens the decline of the rotation curve, imposing strict constraints on the initial mass concentrations of progenitor galaxies. After extensive testing, we determinedα parameters of 3.5 for the MW progenitor and 3.25 for the GSE progenitor. Corresponding scale lengths (R d) were calculated to be 12.867 kpc and 8.02 kpc, respectively. 2.4.Orbital Parameters For the orbital configuration, two galaxies with opposite disk spins were placed on a radially-biased, retrograde orbit. The initial separation was determined by summing the virial radii of the two progenitors, while the initial relative velocity was computed based on a Keplerian orbit. Here, we adopted a high eccentricity (e= 0.9). Such a high value is necessary to reproduce the observed velocity and anisotropy features observed in GSE debris within this low-mass model. Additionally, highly eccentric orbits result in increased star formation rates and enhanced mass accumulation in the galactic inner regions. The inclination angle is another",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,840,1020
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_018,18,"to reproduce the observed velocity and anisotropy features observed in GSE debris within this low-mass model. Additionally, highly eccentric orbits result in increased star formation rates and enhanced mass accumulation in the galactic inner regions. The inclination angle is another crucial parameter in GSE simulations (Naidu et al. 2021; Belokurov et al. 2023). In this study, we aim to investigate how mergers influence vertical disturbances in galactic disks. Therefore, we varied the inclination angle while keeping all other parameters constant, setting angles from 15◦ to 75◦with intervals of 15◦. In our previous work, we modeled the inclination angle as 55◦, this angle is included again here. All inclination angles considered in this simulation are: 15◦, 30◦, 45◦, 55◦, 60◦, and 75◦. In the DICE configuration, five parameters define the orbit: the polar angle (θ) and azimuthal angle (φ) of the orbital plane’s normal vector, and the spin angles (θ 1, θ2, κ). Here,θ 1andθ 2represent the angles between the spin vectors of each galaxy and the orbital plane, respectively, whileκdenotes the angle between the two spin vectors (more detail in",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,980,1160
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_019,19,"orbital plane’s normal vector, and the spin angles (θ 1, θ2, κ). Here,θ 1andθ 2represent the angles between the spin vectors of each galaxy and the orbital plane, respectively, whileκdenotes the angle between the two spin vectors (more detail in Fig. 2 of Perret et al. 2014). For example, to position two galaxies on a retrograde orbit with opposite spins and a 15◦inclination, we set the parameters as (θ, φ, θ 1, θ2, κ) = (0◦,165◦,165◦,15◦,180◦). After a gas-rich merger, gas particles inherit angular momentum from the orbital momentum of the merger and subsequently redistribute into a thin disk (Barnes 2002). The subsequent virialization phase, during which the disk rebuilds, typically lasts several gigayears (Hopkins et al. 2009; Hammer et al. 2009). During this period, instabilities may induce oscillations that we aim to investigate. Table 1 summarizes the initial conditions used in our simulation.",2.1.GSE,2.1.GSE,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,188,144,True,True,1120,1264
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_020,20,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION Due to the high eccentricity, the merger progresses rapidly, ta king approximately 1 Gyr between the first and final pericenter passages and completing within the initial 3 Gyrs. In Figure 1, we present the rotation curve at 11 Gyr 5 102 101 100 r/rvir102103104105106107108DM [M kpc3] 102 101 100 r/rvir0.000.250.500.751.001.251.501.75Mtotal(<R)[M] 1e11 102 101 r/rvir0.00.20.40.60.81.0 101 r/rvir0.00.10.20.30.40.5Triaxiality 101 r/rvir010203040TiltAngle[deg] -200000 -100000 0 E(105km2s2) 100101102103104log(n)0.00.20.40.6 Stellar halo DM halo Figure 2.Top left panelshows the DM density profile along with the radius.Top middle panelshows mass enclosed within a given radius of DM halo. It can be observed that the mass of the dark matter halo in our model is concentrated within 0.2 r vir. Top right panelis the anisotropy parameterβof the stellar halo (blue solid line) and the DM halo (gray solid line) with Galactocentric distance in a spherical coordinate system.Bottom left panelshows the triaxiality of the DM halo, which is measured with DM particles for each bins, as the radius is divided into",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_021,21,"solid line) and the DM halo (gray solid line) with Galactocentric distance in a spherical coordinate system.Bottom left panelshows the triaxiality of the DM halo, which is measured with DM particles for each bins, as the radius is divided into 100 bins with the same number of particles from 0 to r vir, the dense polylines around∼0.1 r virexhibits a highly oblate shape.Bottom middle panelis the tilt angle between DM halo’s short axis with the disk plane in each radius bins, and it shows an increasing trend from the center outward.Bottom right panel shows hist profile of DM halo energy distribution within r vir. for the 15◦inclination case as an example, as differences between various inclination angle settings are negligible. Observational data from Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023) are included for comparison, demonstrating good agreement with our simulation. The rotation curve exhibits no flattening and shows a noticeable decline beyond ∼15 kpc. Since the outer region of the RC mainly depends on the DM profile, additional details of the DM halo are provided in Figure 2.",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_022,22,"our simulation. The rotation curve exhibits no flattening and shows a noticeable decline beyond ∼15 kpc. Since the outer region of the RC mainly depends on the DM profile, additional details of the DM halo are provided in Figure 2. The virial radius (R vir) of this MW model is about 131.05 kpc, defined as the radius within which the average dark matter density equals 200 times the critical density of the universe (ρ cr). This value is slightly higher than the 121.03 and 123.80 kpc reported by Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023), respectively. The top-middle panel of Figure 2 shows the enclosed mass within the virial radius (R vir) of the DM halo. Including the baryonic mass, the total mass is approximately 2.4×1011M⊙, comparable to the range of 2.44−2.53×1011M⊙and 3.8−5.4×1011M⊙reported by Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023), respectively. Here, we measured the anisotropy parameter (β) for both stellar and DM halos, illustrating its radial variation using different colors. The parameterβis defined as: β= 1−σ2 θ+σ2 ϕ 2σ2r,(2) Here,σ irepresents the velocity dispersions",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_023,23,"and Ou et al. (2023), respectively. Here, we measured the anisotropy parameter (β) for both stellar and DM halos, illustrating its radial variation using different colors. The parameterβis defined as: β= 1−σ2 θ+σ2 ϕ 2σ2r,(2) Here,σ irepresents the velocity dispersions in spherical coordinates. The stellar halo exhibits an increasing anisotropy trend at smaller radii, followed by a subsequent decline, similar to the observed features of GSE debris. In contrast, the anisotropy of the DM halo steadily increases up to the virial radius, indicating the orbits of DM particles become increasingly radial. We also determine the shapes and orientations of the principal axes of the halo using a standard iterative procedure based on the shape tensor method (Emami et al. 2021). In summary, particles are selected within the ellipsoidal volume defined by r min≤rell<rmax, where the ellipsoidal radius r ellis defined as: |rell|2=rbody,1 a2 +rbody,2 b2 +rbody,3 c2 ,(3) 6 and rbody=ET(r−r 0).(4) Here,r body describes particle positions within the halo “body” frame (i.e., its position with respect to the halo principal axes). The halo centerr 0is taken to be the",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,420,600
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_024,24,"as: |rell|2=rbody,1 a2 +rbody,2 b2 +rbody,3 c2 ,(3) 6 and rbody=ET(r−r 0).(4) Here,r body describes particle positions within the halo “body” frame (i.e., its position with respect to the halo principal axes). The halo centerr 0is taken to be the position of the most bound halo particle (i.e., where the potential is lowest). The variablesa,b, andcare the halo shape parameters, and E gives the directions of the principal axes. We initialize these values witha=b=c= 1 andE=I, and update the values by diagonalizing the shape tensor whose elements are Si,j=1",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,116,89,False,True,560,649
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_025,25,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger MNX k=1mk(rk)i(rk)j.(5) The axis lengthsa,b, andcare proportional to the square root of the eigenvalues of the shape tensor, and they are normalized such that the ellipsoidal shell maintains a constant volume during iteration. The eigenvectors define the orientation of the halo principal axesE, and are updated each iteration. At every step, the halo shape is computed as the ratio of the minor-to-major axes (q=c/a), and the ratio of the intermediate-to-major axes (p=b/a). The iteration process is terminated when the residual of both tow ratios converges to a level below: MAX(((q−q old)/q)2,((p−p old)/p)2)≤10−3.(6) Where MAX refers to the maximum between the two quantities. Then we can calculate the triaxiality withT= (1−p2)/(1−q2).T >0.6 denotes a prolate halo, andT <0.3 indicates an oblate halo. By measuring the orientation of the axes (with the disk plane’s normal vector aligned parallel to the Z-axis), we are able to determine the tilt angle. Our measurement indicate that the major and intermediate axes are degenerate, a scenario also observed in Kazantzidis et",MNX,MNX,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_026,26,"of the axes (with the disk plane’s normal vector aligned parallel to the Z-axis), we are able to determine the tilt angle. Our measurement indicate that the major and intermediate axes are degenerate, a scenario also observed in Kazantzidis et al. (2004) and Shao et al. (2021). This degeneracy renders the measurement unstable if these axes are used. However, the minor axis is nondegenerate and can be reliably used to measure the tilt angle of the DM halo with respect to the disk plane. In the bottom panel of Figure 2, we analyze the DM halo by this method, dividing the radius into 100 bins, each containing an equal number (17191) of DM particles. The dense polylines are concentrated within 0.2 r vir, indicating that the inner DM halo is more prolate, which corresponds to the formation of a dark matter bar. Meanwhile, in the densest region, the halo exhibits a highly oblate shape. The triaxiality varies only slightly with increasing radius. The tilt angle of each bin shows an increasing trend from the center outward. However the angles are",MNX,MNX,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_027,27,"matter bar. Meanwhile, in the densest region, the halo exhibits a highly oblate shape. The triaxiality varies only slightly with increasing radius. The tilt angle of each bin shows an increasing trend from the center outward. However the angles are highly consistent within 0.2 r vir, indicating that the dark matter halo is compact. The non-zero triaxiality and the radial variation of the tilt angle confirm that the halo is triaxial and misaligned with the disk. We also apply the same method to measure the time evolution of triaxiality and tilt angle under different inclination settings. We selected DM particles within 20 kpc, a region that contains the majority of the galactic mass. In the top panel of Figure 3, we plot triaxiality over simulation time from 3 to 11 Gyr. The inclination angles are divided into two groups: high (i >45◦) and low (i≤45◦). In the early stages, the high-inclination group exhibits larger triaxiality, even approaching prolate shapes. However, in the later stages, DM halos in all inclination settings tend to become oblate. Additionally, simulations withi= 15◦,30◦,45◦display quasi-periodic evolution",MNX,MNX,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_028,28,"(i >45◦) and low (i≤45◦). In the early stages, the high-inclination group exhibits larger triaxiality, even approaching prolate shapes. However, in the later stages, DM halos in all inclination settings tend to become oblate. Additionally, simulations withi= 15◦,30◦,45◦display quasi-periodic evolution in halo shape. Figure 3 bottom panel shows the evolution of the tilt angle relative to the disk plane, derived by tracking the short axis of the DM halo. In the high-inclination group (i= 75◦,60◦,55◦also in the 45◦), the tilt angle varies significantly over time, whereas in the low-inclination group (30◦,15◦), the evolution is more stable. Nonetheless, even subtle changes in the tilt angle can manifest as gravitational disturbances in the galactic disk, which will be discussed later.",MNX,MNX,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,154,118,False,True,420,538
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_029,29,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES 4.1.Evolution of disk warp The geometric shape of the warp can be approximated by a power-law warp or m = 1 mode: Zw(R≥R w) =a(R−R w)bsin(ϕ−ϕ w); (7) Zw(R < R w) = 0.(8) Here,RandZ ware Galactocentric cylindrical coordinates, wherearepresents the amplitude of the warp,bis the power-law index,ϕ wis the polar angle of the warp’s line of nodes (LON hereafter) indicates the orientation of the 7 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 10 11102 101 Triaxialityi=75 i=60 i=55 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 10 11102 101 i=45 i=30 i=15 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 10 11 SimulationTime(Gyr)05101520253035TiltedAngle(deg)i=75 i=60 i=55 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 10 11 SimulationTime(Gyr)510152025 i=45 i=30 i=15 Figure 3.Time evolution of the DM halo’s shape and orientation for different inclination groups (indicated by different colors and lines in each subgraph), calculated using DM particles within the central 20 kpc .Top panelsshow the evolution of the DM halo’s triaxiality parameter, defined asT= (1−p2)/(1−q2),",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_030,30,"DM halo’s shape and orientation for different inclination groups (indicated by different colors and lines in each subgraph), calculated using DM particles within the central 20 kpc .Top panelsshow the evolution of the DM halo’s triaxiality parameter, defined asT= (1−p2)/(1−q2), from 3 to 11 Gyr. The horizontal dashed line marksT= 0.3; values below this threshold indicate an oblate shape, while valuesT≥0.6 indicate a prolate shape.Bottom panelsindicate the evolution of the DM halo’s tilted angle with respect to the disk panel, derived by tracking the short axis of the DM halo. The halo evolution shows distinct behaviors roughly based on inclination: high-inclination models (i >45◦) , and low-inclination models (i≤45◦), they both retain tilted with respect to the disk all the time. warp andR wis the onset radius of the warp. This model was employed by Chen et al. (2019) to accurately fit the Cepheid tracers, prompting us to select stars within the young stars’ age range in the simulation for comparison with the Cepheid data. As Chen et al. (2019) note, the parameters ofa,bandR wshow a clear correlation, whereasϕ wis",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_031,31,"accurately fit the Cepheid tracers, prompting us to select stars within the young stars’ age range in the simulation for comparison with the Cepheid data. As Chen et al. (2019) note, the parameters ofa,bandR wshow a clear correlation, whereasϕ wis a more independent parameter in the geometric warp model. Consequently, while different studies might yield varying a,bandR wvalues,ϕ wtends to be more consistent across measurements of the Galactic warp. Figure 4 illustrates the time evolution of the warp amplitude in the stellar and gas disks. The amplitude is calculated by fitting Eq. 7 to the warp every 0.01 Gyr starting fromt= 3 Gyr—after the merger is completed—and identifying the maximum amplitude atR= 16 kpc, which is near the simulated disk edge. The warp is measured using young disk particles selected based on circularityϵ=L Z/LZ,max(E) >0.65 and age less than 1.5 Gyr in the simulation. This selection reflects that the warp is most prominent among the youngest stars and spans multiple age populations (Chen et al. 2019; Cheng et al. 2020; Huang et al. 2024). The dashed lines in both panels",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_032,32,"Gyr in the simulation. This selection reflects that the warp is most prominent among the youngest stars and spans multiple age populations (Chen et al. 2019; Cheng et al. 2020; Huang et al. 2024). The dashed lines in both panels represent the observed amplitudes of the Milky Way’s stellar and gas disk warps (Chen et al. 2019; Levine et al. 2006). We specifically focus on this young population for three reasons. First, older stellar populations (e.g.,≤4 Gyr) exhibit significant vertical dispersion, which introduces noise into the warp signal (Deng et al. 2024; Han et al. 2023). Second, observational studies indicate that warp kinematics derived from young tracers, such as Cepheids (∼100 Myr), show consistent constraints on the precession rate and LoN orientation, whereas results from older tracers remain inconclusive (see Figure 8 in Cabrera-Gadea et al. 2024). Finally, we tested the robustness of our selection by comparing it with a broader sample including stars up to 4 Gyr. We found that in the early stage, when the proportion of old stars is high, the measurements will deviate from those of",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600
