astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_034,34,"both measured at R = 16 kpc, from 3 Gyr to 11 Gyr, and the stellar disk amplitude is fitting with stars younger than 1.5 Gyr. The curves represent different inclination models as defined in Figure 3. The dash lines indicate the amplitude of MW in observation. High-inclination models show larger initial amplitudes, and the warp displays a regeneration phenomenon: the amplitude often decays to a near-flat state before rising again to a new peak. two measurements will gradually become consistent. Regarding the measurement of precession, we found that there is not much difference between the two sampling methods. Therefore, the specific choice of sampling does not affect our final conclusions. Overall, we maintain that choosing young stars ensures the robustness of our results while being more realistic with observations. Since the Galactic stellar and gas disks exhibit similar warp amplitudes, the stellar and gas disks within the same inclination group also show comparable evolutionary trends in Figure 4. This suggests they are dominated by the same evolutionary mechanism or influenced by the same gravitational potential. In high-inclination groups, the",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,700,880
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_035,35,"amplitudes, the stellar and gas disks within the same inclination group also show comparable evolutionary trends in Figure 4. This suggests they are dominated by the same evolutionary mechanism or influenced by the same gravitational potential. In high-inclination groups, the warp amplitude is larger during the early stages, possibly due to the higher vertical angular momentum of the merger orbit. Most notably, a regeneration mechanism appears in the warp evolution. At times, the disk warp amplitude declines to a near-flat state, only to regenerate and rise again to a new peak. Since the simulation involves only a single initial merger event, this persistent, quasi-periodic regeneration provides strong evidence that the warp is driven by long-lived internal processes. 4.2.Kinematic warp model In the previous subsection, we examined the evolution of the Galactic warp, emphasizing that its rapid changes in amplitude reveal the inherently dynamical nature of warps. Understanding this dynamism is essential for tracing the formation history of galaxies and determining the mass distribution of their halos. The changing geometry of the warp can be characterized by the variation in the",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_036,36,"the inherently dynamical nature of warps. Understanding this dynamism is essential for tracing the formation history of galaxies and determining the mass distribution of their halos. The changing geometry of the warp can be characterized by the variation in the direction of its lines of nodes (LONs), which precess at a rateω. This relationship is given byϕ w(t) =ϕ w,0+ωtwhereϕ w,0denotes the current position of the LONs. A time-dependent 9 warp model based on this framework is presented by Poggio et al. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ, t= 0) = (Vϕ R−ω(R))h w(R)cos(ϕ−ϕ w) +∂hw ∂tsin(ϕ−ϕ w).(9) Hereh w(R) =a(R−R w)b, as described in Eq. 1, VZrepresents the mean vertical velocity and Vϕrepresents the mean azimuthal velocity. To simplify the calculations, we neglect the time derivative term∂hw ∂tand minimize the length of theRbin and assume thatωremains constant with respect toR. Although the warp is evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effect on the precession results. Under these assumptions, the model can be expressed in the same form as Equation 7 in Poggio et al. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ) = (Vϕ",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,980,1160
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_037,37,"warp is evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effect on the precession results. Under these assumptions, the model can be expressed in the same form as Equation 7 in Poggio et al. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ) = (Vϕ R−ω)h w(R)cos(ϕ−ϕ w).(10) Using this kinematic model, once the geometric shape is determined by fitting Eq.7, we can substitute the parameters hwandϕto measure the precession rateωbased on the simulation’s kinematic data. For this analysis, we select young stars (age<1.5 Gyr) within the radial rangeR= 7−17 kpc. The region is then divided into 20 radial bins to track and plot the temporal evolution ofω. Figure 5 shows time evolution of stellar disk precession across different inclination groups. Long-term prograde precession is more frequently observed in the high-inclination group, although transient high prograde precession is also occasionally observed in the low-inclination group, it appears as a temporary feature and is generally dominated by retrograde precession for the majority of the simulation time. The disk warp can be described as an m = 1 bending wave propagating in a kinematically cool disk. Such",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_038,38,"it appears as a temporary feature and is generally dominated by retrograde precession for the majority of the simulation time. The disk warp can be described as an m = 1 bending wave propagating in a kinematically cool disk. Such a wave can be viewed as the superposition of ‘fast’ (+) and ‘slow’ (−) waves circulating with frequenciesω=mΩ(R)±ν(R), where Ω(R) is the angular rotation curve,ν(R) is the vertical frequency, and m-fold rotational symmetry is assumed. The inclusion of the disk’s selfgravity raisesν(R), making bending waves more stable, i.e. stiffer (contrary to density waves) – see Binney & Tremaine (2008). For a given rotation curveω(R), bending waves can only propagate in regions that satisfy the condition [ΩP−Ω(R)]2≥ν2 h.(11) where Ω p=ω/mis the pattern speed andν his the frequency of vertical oscillation contributed by the halo potential. This defines, for m = 1, a ‘forbidden’ region, Ω−ν h<Ω p<Ω+ν h, where bending waves cannot propagate. Generally, ν >Ω, so the ‘fast’ wave is prograde, with a frequencyωdepending strongly onRfor most radii, so differential rotation winds it up rapidly and it decays. The",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1260,1440
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_039,39,"1, a ‘forbidden’ region, Ω−ν h<Ω p<Ω+ν h, where bending waves cannot propagate. Generally, ν >Ω, so the ‘fast’ wave is prograde, with a frequencyωdepending strongly onRfor most radii, so differential rotation winds it up rapidly and it decays. The ‘slow’ wave, on the other hand, is retrograde and circulates with frequency only weakly depending onRfor most radii. This wave is thus expected to wind up slowly and be long-lived. In other words, prograde waves generally disappear quickly, except for persistent disturbances. In addition, according to Eq. 10, a positiveω(prograde precession) leads to a reduction in the vertical velocity of the disk. Conversely, retrograde precession increases the vertical velocity. Therefore, when the disk is in a high prograde but transient precession phase, the warp amplitude often reaches a minimum in a short time of its evolution, and a low prograde but long-lasting precession, the warp amplitude is in the process of continuous attenuation For example, these behaviors can be seen during the periods of 3−3.5 Gyr and 5−5.5 Gyr in the 15◦inclination, and during the period of 3−11 Gyr of",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1400,1580
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_040,40,"but long-lasting precession, the warp amplitude is in the process of continuous attenuation For example, these behaviors can be seen during the periods of 3−3.5 Gyr and 5−5.5 Gyr in the 15◦inclination, and during the period of 3−11 Gyr of the 75◦inclination in Figure 5 respectively. 4.3.Perturbation from the DM halo As discussed previously, the disk appears to be influenced by a gravitational disturbance. In our simulation, however, the only source of disturbance is the DM halo. To quantify the impact of the dark matter halo on the vertical structure of the disk, we calculated the vertical gravitational accelerationa zexerted on both theZ= 0 plane and the actual warped disk plane (which typically deviates fromZ= 0 near the edges) at each simulation snapshot with the DM particles within the R≤20 kpc range. This is computationally faster than calculating accelerations on all disk particles. This mesh has 100×100 bins in the X and Y directions and extends to 17 kpc (X,Y⊆[−17,17] kpc). We used the following expression az,bin =NpartX i−Gm i r2 i,binrz;i,bin ri,bin,(12) where the vertical acceleration at each bin",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1540,1720
