astrobench_d82fccf1ca,astrobench_d82fccf1ca_chunk_053,53,"These slopes are shown in the bottom panel of Fig. 9 as black arrows. Byexaminingthe𝐽 𝑅−𝐽𝜙space,andtheslopesofthearrows(i.e. thewayinwhichstarsscatterattheresonances),wecanbegintosee whythereisastrongridgeformingatthe1:1resonance,withaslope equal to 1; for the retrograde 1:1 resonance, the scattering vector coincides with the slope of the ARL. Thus, stars in the retrograde 1:1 resonance experience strong scattering since they remain on the resonance as they scatter in𝐽 𝑅−𝐽𝜙space7. This is what drives the strong ridge feature at the retrograde 1:1 resonance. This is also reflectedinthe𝐸−𝐿 𝑧space:theslopeoftheARLatthe1:1retrograde resonance is aligned with the slope of𝐸 𝐽, which corresponds to the slope along which stars scatter in this space. Therefore, stars at the 1:1 resonance scatter along𝐸 𝐽, staying on resonance as they scatter. On the other hand, stars at the CR, OLR, and prograde 1:1 resonances, move away from their ARLs when they are scattered at these resonances.","MNRAS000, 000–000 (2026)","MNRAS000, 000–000 (2026)",,,,,Stirring Things Up: Bar-induced substructures in the stellar halo of a cosmological Milky Way analogue,"Thomas Tomlinson, Francesca Fragkoudi, Andreia Carrillo, Azadeh Fattahi, Paula Gherghinescu, Alis Deason, Rüdiger Pakmor, Robert J. J. Grand, Facundo A. Gómez, Freeke van de Voort, Rebekka Bieri",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-20T19:14:36Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14409v1,2601.14409,galactic_dynamics,176,135,False,False,140,275
astrobench_d82fccf1ca,astrobench_d82fccf1ca_chunk_054,54,"Stirring Things Up: Bar-induced substructures in the stellar halo of a cosmological Milky Way analogue 4.2 Direction of scattering In the above, we discussed the slope with which stars scatter at the 1:1resonance,andweshowedthatin𝐽 𝑅−𝐽𝜙spacethescatteringof stars at the 1:1 resonance has the same slope as the ARL. However, 7A similar process has been suggested to occur for stars at the ILR by Sellwood(2010).Forthesestars,thescatteringvectorisalsoalignedwiththe location of the ILR in𝐽 𝑅−𝐽𝜙space, which allows for stars to remain on resonance as they are scattered.determiningthedirectionofangularmomentumtransportatthisres- onance(i.e.,thedirectioninwhichthearrowpoints)isalsorelevant forunderstandingtheshapeofthe1:1ridge:whenstarsscatteratthe retrograde 1:1 resonance, do they gain, or lose angular momentum? The change in angular momentum at the ILR, CR and OLR reso- nancesisdiscussedinLynden-Bell&Kalnajs(1972);herewefollow theirstepstounderstandwhetheronewouldexpectstarsattheretro- grade1:1resonancetoloseorgainangularmomentum.Lynden-Bell & Kalnajs (1972) show that the change in angular momentum at the resonances is given by: ¤𝐻𝑙𝑚=−1 8𝜋∬∞ 0𝑚(𝑙𝜕𝐹 𝜕𝐽𝑅+𝑚𝜕𝐹 𝜕𝐽𝜙)|𝜓𝑙𝑚|2𝛿(𝑙Ω𝑅+𝑚Ω𝜙+𝜔)d𝐽𝑅d𝐽𝜙. (11) where¤𝐻is the change in angular momentum. The right-hand side of the equation is an integral over the radial and azimuthal actions of the distribution function,𝐹, which itself is a function of𝐽 𝑅and 𝐽𝜙. As previously,𝑙and𝑚refer to the integer values describing the resonances. The delta function inside the integral stipulates that angular",4.2 Direction of scattering,4.2 Direction of scattering,,,,,Stirring Things Up: Bar-induced substructures in the stellar halo of a cosmological Milky Way analogue,"Thomas Tomlinson, Francesca Fragkoudi, Andreia Carrillo, Azadeh Fattahi, Paula Gherghinescu, Alis Deason, Rüdiger Pakmor, Robert J. J. Grand, Facundo A. Gómez, Freeke van de Voort, Rebekka Bieri",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-20T19:14:36Z,http://