astrobench_d82fccf1ca,astrobench_d82fccf1ca_chunk_056,56,"larger absolute values of𝐽 𝜙. This is the case for reasonable distribution functions,aswellasforthecaseofasimplesphericalhalo8.Thus,as long as|𝜕𝐹/𝜕𝐽 𝜙|>|𝜕𝐹/𝜕𝐽 𝑅|, which is the case for most standard distribution functions, stars will lose angular momentum (i.e., the angular momentum becomes more negative) at the retrograde 1:1 resonance.Asaconsequence,starswillingeneralbescatteredfrom 𝐿𝑧∼0to more retrograde orbits. 4.3𝐿𝑧and𝐽𝑅changes at the resonances Now that we have an expectation about how stars will scatter at the 1:1 retrograde resonance – i.e., we know that they will move along a line with slope 1, towards more negative angular momenta – we can explore how the actions of stars trapped in different resonances change in our simulation. We explore this by comparing the actions 8Itisstraightforwardtocreateasphericaldistributionfunctionthatsatisfies this condition. All that is needed is a slight radial velocity anisotropy. This is the case for the halo of our simulation, and is also the case for the stellar halooftheMilkyWay(Deasonetal.2013),aswellasinothercosmological simulations (Sales et al. 2007; Navarro et al. 2010).",4.2 Direction of scattering,4.2 Direction of scattering,,,,,Stirring Things Up: Bar-induced substructures in the stellar halo of a cosmological Milky Way analogue,"Thomas Tomlinson, Francesca Fragkoudi, Andreia Carrillo, Azadeh Fattahi, Paula Gherghinescu, Alis Deason, Rüdiger Pakmor, Robert J. J. Grand, Facundo A. Gómez, Freeke van de Voort, Rebekka Bieri",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-20T19:14:36Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14409v1,2601.14409,galactic_dynamics,197,151,False,False,280,431
